{
  "raw": {
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14609": {
      "title": "Senior Procurement Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Procurement Office incorporating Printing Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We're looking for a Senior Procurement Officer to join one of our 4 Category* teams; Laboratories, Medical, Veterinary Medical (Labs), Estates & Facilities (Estates), Information Services (IS), Professional & Campus Services (PCS) UE07: £41,064 – £48,822 per annum CSG / Procurement (Finance) Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) The Opportunity: The University of Edinburgh is looking for a Senior Procurement Officer to join one of our 4 Category* teams; Laboratories, Medical, Veterinary Medical (Labs) Estates & Facilities (Estates) Information Services (IS) Professional & Campus Services (PCS) The vacancies are currently in the IS and Estates teams. Whilst experience in this areas will offer an advantage, we encourage applications from all backgrounds. Reporting to the relevant Category Head, the successful candidate will be responsible for the tendering, award and contract management of a range of procurement projects; as well as strategic initiatives stemming from market engagement and spend analyses. The post-holder will be expected to contribute to the University’s financial wellbeing and international research profile, by delivering cash savings and other cost efficiencies; and supporting the knowledge creation and transfer which lies at the heart of the University’s Strategy 2030. The post-holder will join a forward-thinking, ambitious and supportive procurement team, already well advanced in a service transformation programme. As part of the wider Finance Directorate, we aim to deliver world-class services to the University’s academic teaching and research staff and professional services colleagues, in keeping with the institution’s Top-20 World Ranking. You will engage directly with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders, ensuring that Procurement delivers innovative, fit for purpose solutions which are aligned to University objectives and Government policy. Candidates will be encouraged to exercise initiative and discretion, taking ownership of strategic procurements that deliver maximum benefit across the University. Your skills and attributes for success: Self-motivated and proactive, with a passion to help shape and develop the service. Practical experience and sound theoretical knowledge of end-to-end tendering procedures, including use of eTendering systems. A solid grounding of in-life contract and supplier relationship management. Good computing literacy and numerical analysis skills with excellent attention to detail. Strong communication and negotiation skills and commercial judgement. Click here for a copy of the full job description How to apply Please apply online and submit a CV, a brief research statement, and a cover letter indicating in particular the desired start date. In addition, please provide the names and email addresses of two referees who will be contacted at the shortlisting stage. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from candidates currently underrepresented in the School of Mathematics. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 26 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Finance Department is responsible for the effective financial management, stewardship and long-term financial health of the University, which has a turnover in excess of £900m per year. It exercises control over the University’s finances, provides expert financial guidance particularly for key decision making, and provides an effective services and information for the financial conduct of the University’s business. It follows core values and behaviours to value and strengthen its team and delivery of its services – One Team; Expert; Innovation; Customer Focused; and Transparent. http://www.ed.ac.uk/schools-departments/finance/home "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14953": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: Mathematics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to join Dr Ofer Busani’s research group in the School of Mathematics at the University of Edinburgh. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr Busani and collaborators within the Maxwell Institute for Mathematical Sciences. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum CSE /School of Mathematics Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 24 months fixed term contract with the option to extend for a further 12 months The Opportunity: We are looking for a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to join Dr Ofer Busani’s research group in the School of Mathematics at the University of Edinburgh. The position is funded by Dr Busani’s ERC Starting Grant “UnivKPZ” and offers the opportunity to contribute to cutting-edge projects in random growth models, the KPZ universality class, and probability theory . The successful candidate will work closely with Dr Busani and collaborators within the Maxwell Institute for Mathematical Sciences , a joint research centre between the University of Edinburgh and Heriot-Watt University. Light teaching opportunities (e.g. undergraduate tutorials, project supervision) are available for candidates who wish to broaden their career development. Your skills and attributes for success PhD in Mathematics (or near completion) Strong background in probability theory Ability to conduct independent and collaborative research Ability to disseminate research outcomes through publications and presentations Excellent communication and teamwork skills Strong organisational skills (e.g. contributing to seminars, workshops) Research experience in the KPZ universality class, first/last passage percolation, directed polymers, or integrable probability (desirable) The position is due to start on 1 st September 2027. View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Research statement - Contact details of three academic referees. References will be sought for shortlisted candidates. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from women or other under-represented groups in applied mathematics. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 6 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . Interviews date for shortlisted candidates to be confirmed. The School of Mathematics The University of Edinburgh's School of Mathematics is one of the world’s leading mathematical sciences departments, home to world-class researchers and to an innovative educational environment for both undergraduates and postgraduates. We are proud to have a highly international faculty and students from around the world. As a School we value both the breadth and depth of our research and teaching. We have around 110 permanent faculty and around 45 research fellows and research associates, who cover a broad range of research topics in pure and applied mathematics, operational research and statistics. The School has an enviable record of attracting prestigious fellowships and currently hosts, for instance, 6 ERC grant holders and 7 Royal Society University Research and Dorothy Hodgkin Fellows. In the 2023 Shanghai Global Ranking of Academic Subjects we are ranked 18 th among the world’s best Mathematics departments. Together with the Departments of Mathematics and Actuarial Mathematics & Statistics at Heriot-Watt University, we form the Maxwell Institute; our joint submission to REF2021was ranked 3rd in the UK for Mathematical Sciences by research power, based on the quality and breadth of our combined research. The School delivers a world-leading educational experience for its students and is constantly exploring new evidence-based ideas in mathematical education in order to provide a stimulating learning environment. There are excellent possibilities for imaginative curriculum development, including online activities, and course design and delivery. The School teaches pure and applied Mathematics, operational research and statistics. We have approximately 1000 undergraduate students, around 210 MSc students, and our Graduate School has close to 160 PhD students. Edinburgh is home to the International Centre for Mathematical Sciences, one of the UK’s two major conference centres in the mathematical sciences and operated jointly by the University with Heriot-Watt University. Over the past 30 years the centre has established a superb reputation for hosting workshops at the highest scientific level on all mathematical topics, attracting many of the best mathematicians in the world to the UK. The School is located in the James Clerk Maxwell Building which is situated on the King's Buildings campus, approximately 3 kilometres south of the city centre. Further information about the School, our research and our teaching is available on the School of Mathematics Website ( https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk ) (Opens in new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14964": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/12/2026 Department: Mathematics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position in mathematical physics, within the School of Mathematics at the University of Edinburgh. The post is available from 1st September 2027, for 24 months on a fixed-term, full-time contract. This position is supported by the Simons Collaboration on Celestial Holography grant held by Tim Adamo. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum, pro rata if part-time CSE /School of Mathematics Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 24 months fixed term contract The Opportunity: Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position in mathematical physics, within the School of Mathematics at the University of Edinburgh. The post is available from 1st September 2027, for 24 months on a fixed-term, full-time contract. This position is supported by the Simons Collaboration on Celestial Holography grant held by Tim Adamo. The Mathematical Physics group at the University of Edinburgh currently consists of: Tim Adamo, Sayantani Bhattacharyya, José Figueroa-O’Farrill, Jelle Hartong, James Lucietti, Bernd Schroers and Joan Simon, as well as six postdoctoral researchers and multiple PhD students. In addition to working in collaboration with the grant-holder (Adamo), the successful candidate will be encouraged to develop and pursue their own approaches to research. This position also comes with substantial travel and research budgets. People have always been at the heart of our work. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Applicants should have a PhD in mathematical or theoretical physics and a track record of research excellence. Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Research statement (no more than 3 pages) In addition, arrange for at least 3 letters of reference to be sent to t.adamo@ed.ac.uk . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 1 December 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . Interviews date for shortlisted candidates will be confirmed. The School of Mathematics The University of Edinburgh's School of Mathematics is one of the world’s leading mathematical sciences departments, home to world-class researchers and to an innovative educational environment for both undergraduates and postgraduates. We are proud to have a highly international faculty and students from around the world. As a School we value both the breadth and depth of our research and teaching. We have around 110 permanent faculty and around 45 research fellows and research associates, who cover a broad range of research topics in pure and applied mathematics, operational research and statistics. The School has an enviable record of attracting prestigious fellowships and currently hosts, for instance, 6 ERC grant holders and 7 Royal Society University Research and Dorothy Hodgkin Fellows. In the 2023 Shanghai Global Ranking of Academic Subjects we are ranked 18 th among the world’s best Mathematics departments. Together with the Departments of Mathematics and Actuarial Mathematics & Statistics at Heriot-Watt University, we form the Maxwell Institute; our joint submission to REF2021was ranked 3rd in the UK for Mathematical Sciences by research power, based on the quality and breadth of our combined research. The School delivers a world-leading educational experience for its students and is constantly exploring new evidence-based ideas in mathematical education in order to provide a stimulating learning environment. There are excellent possibilities for imaginative curriculum development, including online activities, and course design and delivery. The School teaches pure and applied Mathematics, operational research and statistics. We have approximately 1000 undergraduate students, around 210 MSc students, and our Graduate School has close to 160 PhD students. Edinburgh is home to the International Centre for Mathematical Sciences, one of the UK’s two major conference centres in the mathematical sciences and operated jointly by the University with Heriot-Watt University. Over the past 30 years the centre has established a superb reputation for hosting workshops at the highest scientific level on all mathematical topics, attracting many of the best mathematicians in the world to the UK. The School is located in the James Clerk Maxwell Building which is situated on the King's Buildings campus, approximately 3 kilometres south of the city centre. Further information about the School, our research and our teaching is available on the School of Mathematics Website ( https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk ) (Opens in new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14985": {
      "title": "ECAT Clinical Lectureships Med Surg 2027 intake (round 1)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Medicine and Veterinary Medicine College Office Category: Clinical JobFunction: Doctor in Training JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking to appoint up to 3 highly ambitious individuals who wish to undertake a PhD and who aspire to become the next generation of clinical academic leaders. Grade ACN2: £52,229 – £77,389 per annum C ollege of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine Full-time: 40 hours per week Fixed-term: to CCT date + 6 months The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a university that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. Join the University of Edinburgh and you’ll be making a difference to everything around you. Since 2008, the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) at the University of Edinburgh has appointed some of the brightest academic minds to undertake a clinical PhD via the Wellcome-funded ECAT programme. Building on the research training excellence developed over that period, and with a combination of external and institutional (CMVM) PhD funding, the ECAT programme continues to go from strength to strength. We now seek to fill up to three ECAT Clinical Lectureships as part of Public Services Delivery (PSD) Scotland’s doctoral SCREDS (Scottish Clinical Research Excellence Development) Scheme. Successful candidates will have access to cutting-edge infrastructure, large-scale data resources and a diverse supervisory pool of internationally competitive investigators. This environment will support ambitious, curiosity-driven researchers, building upon established strengths in genetics, cell biology, in vivo model systems, human disease and health data science while enabling novel, interdisciplinary approaches to fundamental biological, technological and social mechanisms underpinning health and disease. Before beginning the three-year PhD project, 0.2 FTE funding is provided for a lead-in year when selection of supervisors and planning of the PhD project are optimised with the support of the programme directors. Following the PhD, there is funding for continued academic activity and research training (0.2 FTE) and clinical practice (0.8 FTE) up to CCT + six months. Successful candidates will be expected to develop a competitive PhD fellowship application for institutional or external PhD funding. Vacancies are open to: Trainees in all GMC-recognised medical and surgical specialties; Trainees with a Scottish National Training Number (NTN). UK NTN holders may apply but an inter-deanery transfer will be necessary. Your skills and attributes for success: You must be able to demonstrate a commitment to a research career and should have a track record appropriate to your career stage in basic, translational or clinical research. You must have (or obtain by the start date of the post) a specialty training position with a National Training Number (NTN) in your chosen discipline in Scotland. UK NTN holders may apply but an inter-deanery transfer will be necessary. You must be GMC-registered and must be practicing clinically in the UK. You must have relevant clinical experience in your chosen specialty. You must be eligible for higher specialist clinical training. Candidates who have already undertaken a period of research leading to a doctoral degree are not eligible to apply. Protecting Vulnerable Groups (PVG) check required. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits, including leading-edge research, opportunities for publication and working within one of the world’s leading universities. This post is full-time (40 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply: IMPORTANT: there are two application streams and you should apply to the relevant stream for your professional background (Medical & Surgical or Vet – please ensure that this is the correct stream for you. If not, please visit the University’s jobs page and find the correct stream (search for ‘ECAT’). In this round of recruitment, there is a total of up to four vacancies across both streams (three Medical & Surgical and one Vet). Applications must be accompanied by a CV and 500-word supporting statement. Please attach the supporting statement to your CV and upload it as ONE document under “Resume” in the online system. There is no need to include a cover letter. For the sake of fairness, documents other than the CV and supporting statement will not be considered. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 9 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11.59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK , the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held in person in Edinburgh on 16 December 2026. The College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine brings people together from all over the world to learn to become research scientists, doctors, vets, dental professionals or simply explore new ways of learning. It's a place where scientists have the facilities and equipment they need to explore the most challenging of research questions, where clinical trials explore new treatment options and new ideas get tested every day. All this research and teaching activity is enabled by people working in roles that lead on IT, HR, finance, communications, risk management, strategy, events, marketing, research management, recruitment and many more specialisms; the list is as diverse as our community itself. We pride ourselves on being a place where ideas can grow and people are given opportunities to flourish. If you would like to contribute to research and teaching that can change the world then please submit your application. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15025": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Royal Dick Veterinary Studies Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to understand horses’ experiences when being transported via road, and leading to the development of a welfare assessment tool for transportation. Fixed Term post, 36 months. Grade UE07: £41,064- £48,822 per annum CMVM/ Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 36 months The Opportunity: Take your next step. Be part of something bigger We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to understand horses’ experiences when being transported via road, and leading to the development of a welfare assessment tool for transportation. This position is funded by World Horse Welfare, and the successful candidate will collect both physiological and behavioural measures of horses being transported by road to better understand the horses’ experience. This position represents a unique opportunity to join a renowned equine behaviour and welfare research team at the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. This post is full-time (35 hours per week) and will involve flexible working hours View the ful l job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30th October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held at the beginning of November. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15030": {
      "title": "ECAT Veterinary Clinical Lecturer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Royal Dick Veterinary Studies Category: Veterinary Clinical JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies has an exciting opening for a ECAT Veterinary Clinical Lecturer for an August 2027 start. Building on the research training excellence developed over that period, and with a combination of external and institutional (CMVM) PhD funding, the ECAT programme continues to go from strength to strength. UE08, £50,253 - £61,759, 5 year fixed term full-time (35 hours per week). Grade UE08: £50,253- £61, 759 per annum CMVM/ Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 5 years Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a university that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. Join the University of Edinburgh and you’ll be making a difference to everything around you. The Opportunity: Since 2008, the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) at the University of Edinburgh has appointed some of the brightest academic minds to undertake a clinical PhD via the Wellcome-funded ECAT programme. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. Building on the research training excellence developed over that period, and with a combination of external and institutional (CMVM) PhD funding, the ECAT programme continues to go from strength to strength. We now seek to recruit an ECAT Veterinary Clinical Lecturer for an August 2027 start. The successful candidate will have access to cutting-edge infrastructure, large-scale data resources and a diverse supervisory pool of internationally competitive investigators. This environment will support ambitious, curiosity-driven researchers, building upon established strengths in genetics, cell biology, in vivo model systems, human disease and health data science while enabling novel, interdisciplinary approaches to fundamental biological, technological and social mechanisms underpinning health and disease. Before beginning the three-year PhD project, a lead-in year will feature 0.7 FTE dedicated research time to support development of external funding applications, and the post-PhD year will feature protected research time to work towards substantiative further funding. Candidates will be expected to identify and apply for external PhD funding during their lead-in year. Your skills and attributes for success: evidence of high academic achievement you will have completed an internship and/or residency or experience in clinical practice Candidates who have already undertaken a period of research leading to a doctoral degree are not eligible to apply. A Skilled Worker Visa is an essential requirement for non-UK citizens. Level 2 Disclosure check (Standard Disclosure) required. A career with us has a range of other benefits, including leading-edge research, opportunities for publication and working within one of the world’s leading universities. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. View the f ull job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply: IMPORTANT: there are two application streams and you should apply to the relevant stream for your professional background (Medical & Surgical or Vet – please ensure that this is the correct stream for you. If not, please visit the University’s jobs page and find the correct stream (search for ‘ECAT’). Applications must be accompanied by a CV and 500-word supporting statement. Please attach the supporting statement to your CV and upload it as ONE document under “Resume” in the online system. There is no need to include a cover letter. For the sake of fairness, documents other than the CV and supporting statement will not be considered. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 9 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11.59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK , the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held in person in Edinburgh on 16 December 2026. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15052": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Regenerative Livestock Systems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Royal Dick Veterinary Studies Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the Division of Global Agriculture & Food Systems within RDSVS and contribute to the University’s role in Nutrifeeds, a major Horizon Europe project transforming how animal feed is produced, sourced and valued across Europe. This is an exciting opportunity for a researcher who wants to work at the interface of livestock systems, agricultural economics, sustainability science, and social research methods, and who thrives in collaborative, multi‑country research environments. Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum School/Department: RDSVS Division of Global Agriculture & Food Systems Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 27 months The Opportunity: Help shape the future of sustainable livestock systems across Europe The University of Edinburgh is a place where people are inspired to explore, innovate and make a difference. We are driven by curiosity, committed to excellence, and united by a shared ambition to create positive change. As part of our community, you will join colleagues who are principled, inclusive, and motivated by research that matters. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the Division of Global Agriculture & Food Systems within RDSVS and contribute to the University’s role in Nutrifeeds , a major Horizon Europe project transforming how animal feed is produced, sourced and valued across Europe. This is an exciting opportunity for a researcher who wants to work at the interface of livestock systems, agricultural economics, sustainability science, and social research methods , and who thrives in collaborative, multi‑country research environments. In this role, you will take responsibility for planning and delivering key elements of the Nutrifeeds work programme, including farm‑level data collection , semi‑quantitative surveys , and analysis of regenerative and circular livestock feeding systems . You will design and manage analytical workflows using R or Python , contribute to modelling and evidence‑synthesis tasks, and help shape new ideas and approaches within the project. You will work closely with partners across Europe, participate in project meetings and workshops, and contribute to policy‑relevant outputs that support the EU Farm to Fork Strategy and the transition to a climate‑neutral food system. You will disseminate findings through publications and presentations, and you may supervise students or junior researchers involved in data collection or analysis. We are looking for someone who brings strong analytical skills , excellent communication , and a collaborative, adaptable approach . You will be comfortable working independently, managing your own workstreams, and contributing proactively to project design. Experience with livestock systems, agricultural economics, or mixed‑methods farm research will be valuable, and familiarity with regenerative agriculture or sustainability assessment frameworks is an advantage. If you are motivated by research that delivers real‑world impact, and you want to contribute to shaping more sustainable and resilient food systems, we would love to hear from you. This post is full‑time (35 hours per week) for 27 months. We are open to considering flexible or hybrid working arrangements. View the full job description (opens in a new tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: CV Cover letter outlining your suitability for the role As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Family‑friendly policies and flexible working options Staff discounts, wellbeing resources, and professional development opportunities Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from groups currently underrepresented in our School and discipline. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates The closing date for applications is Friday 30th October 2026 Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview date: Friday 20 th November 2026 The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14782": {
      "title": "Chef de Partie",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: ACE Catering Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a Chef De Partie to be part of our team at Canopy Kitchen & Courtyard, based at the iconic Edinburgh Futures Institute, in the heart of the Meadows. Canopy provides a busy Grab & Go style cafe service, where all the products are made fresh daily in house. Grade UE03: £24,729 - £25,804 per annum plus a 15% premium band for flexible working CSG / Accommodation Catering and Events / Catering Full Time: 35 hours per week, 5 over 7 days Open Ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, we are a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. We are looking for a Chef De Partie to be part of our team at Canopy Kitchen & Courtyard, based at the iconic Edinburgh Futures Institute, in the heart of the Meadows. Canopy provides a busy Grab & Go style cafe service, where all the products are made fresh daily in house. A 70-cover restaurant serving up brunch 6 days per week and dinner 3 days per week, focusing on seasonal, Scottish ingredients, whilst we also provide delivered hospitality and event catering to the clients in the building. This is a complex and fast paced operation making it an excellent opportunity a CDP or an experienced Commis Chef looking to take the next step in their culinary career. Reporting to the Sous Chef, you will support the day -to- day running of the kitchen, managing your sections independently, preparing excellent quality food to company standards and contributing to the coaching of commis chefs. Key responsibilities include ; ensuring that all dishes are prepared, cooked and presented in line with recipes, specifications and food safety standards while maintaining consistently high levels of quality. You will help maintain a clean, organised and efficient kitchen environment, work collaboratively with front of house colleagues to deliver an excellent customer experience, work busy service periods to ensure food is delivered on time and to the required standard and take responsibility for kitchen operations in the absence of the Sous Chef or Junior Sous Chef. The successful candidate will someone who thrives on being part of a team to deliver the brand standards and exceed the customer’s expectations. You will demonstrate fantastic attention to detail and a true passion for food. You will currently be working in a similar role within a catering environment. It is essential to be able to work flexible shifts, including weekends and evenings. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well and impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do, therefore ask yourself: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Variety everyday Opportunities for personal development Good salary / pay rate Responsibility and autonomy This post is full time (35 hours per week). Location: Canopy Kitchen & Courtyard, Edinburgh Futures Institute (EFI), 1 Lauriston Place, Edinburgh, EH3 9EF View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14958": {
      "title": "Research Accountant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Edinburgh Research Office Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Accountant who will play a leading role in the implementation of a financial control environment to drive the project forward and meet the objectives. You will lead a project team focussed on resolving legacy grant reviews, bringing backlog to business as usual levels, providing progress reporting and implementation changes from lessons learned. Grade UE08: £50,253 - £61,759 per annum Corporate Services Group / Edinburgh Research Office Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term contract: 18 months The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. We are seeking colleagues who will embody our values of expertise, community, integrity and collaboration. The Research Grants Team work closely with the University of Edinburgh research and professional service communities, partners and funders, to maximise the research academic colleagues can undertake for the funding they receive. Our expert team delivers financial management and post award administration of the University’s 3,500 research grants. We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Accountant who will play a leading role in the implementation of a financial control environment to drive the project forward and meet the objectives. You will lead a project team focussed on resolving legacy grant reviews, bringing backlog to business as usual levels, providing progress reporting and implementation changes from lessons learned. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please apply with a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the knowledge, skills and experience required, and your motivation for applying for this post. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Working in Edinburgh Research Office you will be part of a professional, supportive and friendly team ; at the heart of University research support, working in collaboration with academics and research support professionals across the institution. The breadth of our remit means you will get an overview of research support activity while you build your skills in your specialism. Gaining new knowledge and skills to help you develop in this role and progress in your career is a priority. We offer colleagues a stimulating working environment based on our values of integrity, expertise, community, and collaboration. Our work is fast‐paced and intense; you will thrive here if you are motivated, customer‐orientated, and passionate about providing an excellent service to enable our researchers to do amazing work . "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15023": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Department: Institute of Ecology and Evolution Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a skilled and motivated 3‑year Postdoctoral Research Associate to join a NERC-funded project on adaptive introgression in European butterflies, led by Dr Konrad Lohse and Dr Simon Martin. The PDRA will lead population genomic analyses across two work packages: Grade UE07: £41,064- £ 48,822 per annum School of Biological Sciences/ College of Sciences & Engineering Full Time- 35 hours per week 36 months fixed term contract The Opportunity: We are looking for a skilled and motivated 3‑year Postdoctoral Research Associate to join a NERC-funded project on adaptive introgression in European butterflies, led by Dr Konrad Lohse and Dr Simon Martin. The PDRA will lead population genomic analyses across two work packages: Comparative scans of selective sweeps across 42 butterfly species (21 sister-species pairs) to quantify how often adaptive evolution is fuelled by introgressed variants and to identify their functional targets. Analysis of a hybrid zone system, integrating whole‑genome resequencing and long‑read sperm sequencing to find spatial population genomic signals of gene conversion and adaptive introgression. You will work with chromosome-level reference genomes, large WGS datasets and high‑performance computing, and collaborate closely with an RA responsible for lab work. The role includes opportunities for fieldwork in France, work with collaborators in Spain and Czech Republic, presenting at international conferences, and supervising project students. You will join Konrad Lohse’s lab in the dynamic Institute of Ecology and Evolution, University of Edinburgh, as part of a project funded by the Natural Environmental Research Council (NERC). Applicants must have a PhD (or to be completed in 3 months) in evolutionary/population genomics or a related field, strong skills in Python/R, computational genomics and statistics, and enthusiasm for working with large genomic datasets. The post requires creativity in problem solving, and strong communication and organisational skills. An understanding of inference methods for detecting introgression and selective sweeps is desirable. Questions should be directed to konrad.lohse @ed.ac.uk View the full Job Description A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Responsibility and autonomy Variety every day Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 13 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held online the week of 23 November 2026 . At the Institute of Evolutionary Biology we study evolution in the broadest possible sense, from virtually every angle and using a great range of organisms and techniques. Our research falls under three broad themes (Evolutionary and Quantitative Genetics, Evolutionary Ecology and Behaviour, Biodiversity and Ecology) Our interdisciplinary research and teaching benefits from strong links with members of the Institute of Immunology and Infection Research (IIIR), the Edinburgh Infectious Diseases network (EID), the Centre for Immunity, Infection and Evolution, the Institute of Genetics and Molecular Medicine, the Life Physics Group, the Roslin Institute, and the Royal Botanic Garden Edinburgh. Our funders include BBSRC, NERC, charities and government bodies. To find out more visit https://www.ed.ac.uk/biology/evolutionary-biology "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15051": {
      "title": "Guaranteed Hours Receptionist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom The School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences at the University of Edinburgh is seeking applications to join its BioQuarter Campus reception team, based across the BioQuarter campus on a permanent basis. Grade UE03: £24,729 - £25,804 per annum pro rata, plus annual leave allowance College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine Part-time: Guaranteed Hours – 100 hours per year Open-ended (permanent) Location: will vary depending on need across 5 receptions at the BioQuarter Campus Vacancies available: 3 The Opportunity: Try something new. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences at the University of Edinburgh is seeking applications to join its BioQuarter Campus reception team, based across the BioQuarter campus on a permanent basis. The postholder will provide comprehensive reception services and support to all staff, students, and visitors, serving as the primary point of contact for enquiries and visitor coordination. The role supports the smooth running of facilities, and security across the Campus, while also providing essential administrative backup during peak periods to ensure seamless day-to-day operations. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Variety every day Working within one of the world’s leading universities Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences We host research and postgraduate training in neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research. We are one of six schools in the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh and are home to the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research. Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Our institute fosters collaborative neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research to tackle some of the world's most significant health challenges. Our research explores health and disease across the body and life course – from tiny changes in cells to the lived experience of patients. Together, we work to advance the understanding, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of major health conditions including heart disease, stroke, metabolic dysfunction, mental illness, dementia and neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders. Empowered by genetics, advanced imaging, artificial intelligence and clinical trials, our research seamlessly connects discovery science with clinical care. Our work is internationally acclaimed, achieving 4* and world-leading classifications for the majority of our research, health and wealth impacts and research papers in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 assessment, the UK’s system for assessing the quality of research in UK higher education institutions. As part of driving progress in our core research areas, we host internationally leading research centres and state-of-the-art technologies: Anne Rowling Regenerative Neurology Clinic British Heart Foundation (BHF) Centre of Research Excellence Centre for Pesticide Suicide Prevention | CPSP Euan MacDonald Centre for Motor Neurone Disease Research Edinburgh Clinical Research Facility Edinburgh Imaging Jennifer Brown Research Laboratory Medical Research Council/British Hearth Foundation Centre of Research Excellence in Advanced Cardiac Therapies MS Society Edinburgh Centre for MS Research | MS Society Muir Maxwell Epilepsy Centre Patrick Wild Centre Row Fogo Centre for Research into Ageing and the Brain Simons Initiative for the Developing Brain (SIDB) UK Dementia Research Institute at Edinburgh UKRI Hub for Metabolic Psychiatry Our institute community Our researchers drive cutting-edge science, enabling our students and working hand in hand with our expert technical and professional services colleagues. We are committed to creating a collaborative, inclusive and welcoming community, where everyone feels supported, empowered and inspired to achieve their best. Training and education We offer a wide range of postgraduate training opportunities. Students learn from world-class researchers, access outstanding facilities and get support to develop. Our graduates go on to successful careers in academia, research, healthcare and industry worldwide. Our colleagues are also leading contributors to undergraduate programmes. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14930": {
      "title": "Cash Forecasting & Group Liquidity Lead",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Finance Directorate Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom In this role, you will take ownership of our cash forecasting & liquidity strategy, where you will transform how we manage cash flows across our £1.4bn global operation. You will drive a step-change in forecast accuracy, directly enabling smarter investment decisions and reducing borrowing costs to protect the funds that power our world-class research and teaching. If you are a strategic thinker ready to deliver tangible financial impact within a collaborative and inclusive environment, this is your opportunity to shape the University's financial future. Grade UE09: £63,606 - £78,469 per annum Finance & Procurement Directorate / Financial Planning & Analysis Team Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) contract Use your knowledge to drive change. Make a meaningful impact Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Opportunity In this role, you will take ownership of our cash forecasting & liquidity strategy, where you will transform how we manage cash flows across our £1.4bn global operation. You will drive a step-change in forecast accuracy, directly enabling smarter investment decisions and reducing borrowing costs to protect the funds that power our world-class research and teaching. If you are a strategic thinker ready to deliver tangible financial impact within a collaborative and inclusive environment, this is your opportunity to shape the University's financial future. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: • Responsibility and autonomy • Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. • Professional development and subject matter expertise This post is full-time; however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic candidates, as we acknowledge they are currently underrepresented in our area. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14961": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Institute of Genetics and Cancer Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Western General, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking an enthusiastic cell biologist to join our research team as a postdoctoral researcher. Funded by an ERC Advanced Grant we aim to address fundamental mechanisms controlling tissue cellularity that result in epigenetic ageing and determine size in mammals. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum, pro rata if part-time CMVM / School of Genetics and Cancer Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 3 years Where passion meets purpose. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our student’s performance and wellbeing. The Opportunity: Epigenetic regulation of stem/progenitor cells in growth and ageing We are seeking an enthusiastic cell biologist to join our research team as a postdoctoral researcher. Funded by an ERC Advanced Grant we aim to address fundamental mechanisms controlling tissue cellularity that result in epigenetic ageing and determine size in mammals. We have discovered a new accelerated ageing syndrome, that links a cell intrinsic clock with both ageing and human size (Sarni et al. Nat Genet 2026). The project team will use stem cell culture systems, targeted epigenomic engineering, CRISPR barcoding and mouse models; to define mechanisms connecting DNA methylation at lineage-determining genes to profound changes in stem cell output and tissue regeneration. The long-term goal is to identify targetable cellular pathways for future therapies, and to shed light on how this DNA methylation clock could regulate size and longevity across mammalian evolution. The post-holder will be part of a multidisciplinary team in the lab of Prof. Andrew Jackson FRS, working in a cross-disciplinary environment that addresses both fundamental cellular processes and disease mechanisms. The lab is based in the Institute of Genetics and Cancer, a highly collaborative and supportive environment. The research group is internationally diverse and strongly competitive, consistently publishing in high-level journals. For further details on the research group: https://institute-genetics-cancer.ed.ac.uk/research/research-groups-a-z/a-jackson-group People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Working within one of the world’s leading universities This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: CV Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 29 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . Interviews will be held shortly after the advert closes. The School of Genetics and Cancer is one of the six Schools that make up the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM). The School is home to the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC) whose goals are to carry out excellent genetic, genomic, cellular and clinical science, so that we contribute significantly to the understanding of human development, physiology and disease, and apply this knowledge for the benefit of patients and society. The IGC is one of the College’s five world-leading research institutes. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. The College’s undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. To find out more visit: School of Genetics and Cancer webpage ( https://medicine-vet-medicine.ed.ac.uk/school-of-genetics-and-cancer ) (opens a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14974": {
      "title": "Climbing Wall Supervisor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: University Sport and Exercise Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Sports and Exercise PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Edinburgh Sport is looking for a Climbing Wall Supervisor (UoE Grade 4) to supervise the University's Tower Climbing Wall and Rock Bouldering area. Reporting to the Climbing Wall Team Leader, you will keep the wall safe and welcoming for a community. Key duties include carrying out floor walks and stepping in when safety is at risk, leading rescues when needed, welcoming and registering members, managing equipment hire and sales, and completing daily equipment and safety checks. The role also involves running courses that teach new climbers to climb and belay safely, and setting fresh, challenging routes and problems. The ideal candidate is an active, enthusiastic climber with excellent people skills and customer service experience, and holds (or is willing to gain) a Climbing Wall Instructor or Rock Climbing Instructor qualification. This role requires PVG Scheme registration. Grade UE04: £26,093 to £28,778 per annum, pro rata (£14.30 to £15.77 per hour, plus 18.1% holiday uplift, totalling £16.89 to £18.62 per hour) Corporate Services Group / Sport & Exercise, Edinburgh Sport Guaranteed hours: 100 hours per year Open ended: Permanent Contract 3 posts available posts The Opportunity: Help us redefine what's possible. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there's a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University's research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. As Climbing Wall Supervisor, you'll provide daily supervision of the Tower Climbing Wall and Rock Bouldering area, making sure every climber can enjoy the wall safely. Reporting to the Climbing Wall Team Leader and working closely with the Technical Advisor and wider Edinburgh Sport team, you'll carry out floor walks, lead rescues when needed, welcome and register members, manage equipment hire and complete daily safety checks. You'll also run courses teaching new climbers to climb and belay safely, and set fresh, challenging routes and problems for our community of over 20,000 members. Climbing isn't just a sport, it's a community. We're looking for someone who is an active, enthusiastic climber and just as passionate about people as they are about climbing. You'll hold, or be willing to gain, a Climbing Wall Instructor or Rock Climbing Instructor qualification. This role will be based at The Pleasance Sports Complex and Gym, 46 Pleasance, Edinburgh, EH8 9TJ. Candidates must be available to work evenings and weekends. This role requires Protection of Vulnerable Groups (PVG) Scheme registration. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are part of our community. We are looking for people with drive and determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Variety every day. Responsibility and autonomy. Excellent benefits and support. Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. View the full job description here Please include the following documents in your application: CV Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 21 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browser's local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. University Sport and Exercise is a five star national and international sports facility, providing top class sporting events and sports therapy services. The University is ranked in the top 5 sporting Universities in the UK. It provides a comprehensive range of sport and exercise opportunities for students, staff and members of the local community. The range of support runs from day to day leisure and exercise, through competitive sport, to support for national and international standard athletes. Sport and Exercise website ( http://www.ed.ac.uk/schools-departments/sport-exercise ) (opens in new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15020": {
      "title": "Animal Care Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Technical JobFunction: Animal Care JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking a motivated and compassionate Animal Care Assistant (ACA) to support our clinical teams in delivering the highest standards of animal care within the Hospital for Small Animals. Grade UE04: £26,093-28,778 per annum, pro rata R(D)SVS / Hospital for Small Animals Part Time: 30 hours per week (Working 2.30pm-8.30pm- shift time TBC with successful candidate) Open ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: Be part of something bigger, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We are seeking a motivated and compassionate Animal Care Assistant (ACA) to support our clinical teams in delivering the highest standards of animal care within the Hospital for Small Animals. Working primarily in our Intermediate Care ward alongside veterinary nurses and veterinary surgeons, you will play an important role in patient care by maintaining a clean and safe environment, assisting with the handling and husbandry of animals, preparing equipment and clinical areas, and supporting the day-to-day running of the hospital. All of our Animal Care Assistants are expected to work across all areas of the hospital as needed. This is an excellent opportunity for someone who is passionate about animal welfare, enjoys working in a busy team environment, and is looking to develop experience within a leading veterinary referral hospital. Strong communication skills, a proactive approach, and a commitment to providing outstanding patient care are essential. Our Specialist Services at HfSA include: Anaesthesia Cardiology Service Dermatology Diagnostic Imaging Dick Vet Rabbit and Exotic Practice Emergency & Critical Care The Feline Clinic The Internal Medicine Service Neurology and Neurosurgery Ophthalmology Service Orthopaedic Surgery Soft Tissue Surgery REPAIR Centre The Riddell-Swan Veterinary Cancer Centre The Behaviour Clinic Our hospital is equipped with: Three canine wards Four exotics wards One feline ward Purpose-built isolation units A fully equipped emergency room Dedicated Intermediate Care and Intensive Care Wards Four theatre suites MRI and CT imaging Vital Beam linear accelerator We offer a focused training programme to help you get started in the role. During your first few weeks, you’ll shadow experienced colleagues and receive practical support to develop your knowledge and confidence. Any hours worked beyond your contracted weekly hours during training will be paid as overtime in line with university guidelines. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 21 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14969": {
      "title": "Talent Transition Rowing Coach",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: University Sport and Exercise Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Sports and Exercise JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom Join one of the UK's leading sporting universities and build a pathway that takes talented student-athletes from any sporting background into performance rowing. Where the potential is there, that pathway leads on to Scottish Rowing, British Rowing and GB Rowing programmes. Grade UE04: £26,093- £28,778 per annum Corporate Services Group, Sports & Exercise Full Time: 35 hours per week Open Ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: Use your knowledge to drive change. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. Can you spot a future international in an athlete who has never picked up an oar? Join one of the UK's leading sporting universities and build a pathway that takes talented student-athletes from any sporting background into performance rowing. Where the potential is there, that pathway leads on to Scottish Rowing, British Rowing and GB Rowing programmes. You'll be part of a high-performance culture that helps athletes thrive both academically and athletically. Finding talent (30%): Run annual recruitment campaigns and talent-transfer initiatives, working with University clubs and coaches to identify athletes with the physical, technical and behavioural potential for rowing. Coaching (40%): Plan and deliver evidence-informed programmes on land and water, build strong technical foundations, and create individual development plans. Managing the pathway (20%): Set clear progression standards from recruitment to our senior squads. Hold regular athlete reviews, and support athletes through testing, trialling and national selection. Running the programme (10%): Keep accurate records, contribute to planning and review, and support the safe use of boats, facilities and equipment. You'll work alongside strength and conditioning, physiotherapy, sport psychology and performance lifestyle colleagues, as well as Edinburgh University Boat Club and our national partners. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Variety every day Responsibility and autonomy View the full job description How to apply - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. University Sport and Exercise is a five star national and international sports facility, providing top class sporting events and sports therapy services. The University is ranked in the top 5 sporting Universities in the UK. It provides a comprehensive range of sport and exercise opportunities for students, staff and members of the local community. The range of support runs from day to day leisure and exercise, through competitive sport, to support for national and international standard athletes. Sport and Exercise website ( http://www.ed.ac.uk/schools-departments/sport-exercise ) (opens in new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14982": {
      "title": "Assistant Contracts Executive",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Enterprise Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Edinburgh Innovations Ltd is seeking a highly organised and detail-focused Assistant Contracts Executive to join our Contracts Team. Edinburgh Innovations is seeking a highly organized and detail-focused individual to provide h i g h- q u ali t y contract drafting and administration support within the EI Contracts team. As an Assistant Contracts Executive, you will play a key role in reviewing and negotiating a variety of contractual agreements, primarily Non-Disclosure Agreements and Amendments, to support collaborations with industry partners. This post is full-time (35 hours per week), we welcome applications from candidates seeking part-time hours or flexible working patterns . We also support hybrid working on a non-contractual basis, offering a blend of home and on-site working to help you balance work and life effectively. This is a fixed term post until the 31 st of July 2027 . The salary for this post is Grade 6 £32,873 to £39,233 per annum. The experience and qualities we are looking for: Educated to degree level or equivalent formal training: The candidate should have a degree or equivalent qualification. Knowledge of various legal agreements: The candidate should have knowledge of, or be able to develop knowledge of, various legal agreements. Experience drafting and negotiating contracts is desirable. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with ability to build relationships: The candidate should have excellent written and oral communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with a wide range of stakeholders, explain complex contractual matters clearly, and build strong relationships with multiple stakeholders, including internal colleagues and external clients. Demonstrable evidence of high-quality customer service skills : The candidate should have a proven track record of delivering high-quality customer service, with the ability to inspire trust and ensure a smooth and effective delivery of the EI Contracts service. Highly organised, with ability to manage a busy workload efficiently: The candidate should be highly organised, able to prioritise tasks, and manage their own busy workload efficiently to meet deadlines. Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab). About Us At Edinburgh Innovations (EI), our culture is built on three core values: Collaborative , P rogressive and I nnovative . We believe the best results come from working together, sharing ideas, and supporting one another. We’re always looking forward, embracing change, challenging assumptions, and pushing boundaries to grow. Innovation is at the heart of what we do, encouraging creativity and fresh thinking at every level of the business. If you thrive in a team-driven environment that values bold ideas and continuous improvement, you’ll fit right in. Championing Equality, Diversity and Inclusion We’re committed to building an inclusive workplace where everyone feels valued, respected, and empowered to thrive. At EI we celebrate diversity in all its forms and actively seek to create a team that reflects the communities around us. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds, experiences, and identities because different perspectives make us stronger. As a valued member of our team, you can look forward to: A competitive salary that reflects your skills and experience An exciting, positive, and creative workplace that’s as rewarding as it is challenging A generous 40 days of combined annual leave and public holiday entitlement A comprehensive benefits package, including a defined contribution pension scheme (currently 3.5/4.5% employee and 9/14.5% employer contributions), family-friendly policies, flexible working options, and more Ongoing support to develop your skills, grow your career, and recognise your achievements About You At EI, we look for people who live our core behaviours in everything they do You work well with others, build strong relationships, and believe that teamwork drives better outcomes. You're curious, creative, and not afraid to challenge the norm to find smarter solutions. You take responsibility for your work, follow through on commitments, and are driven to deliver results. You seek feedback, grow from experiences, and are always looking to improve. You genuinely care about the impact of your work on colleagues, customers, and the wider community. If this sounds like you, we’d love to hear from you. Application Information Please submit your CV along with a covering letter that tells us why you’re interested in the role and how your skills and experience make you a strong fit. Right to Work All applicants must have the right to work in the UK at the time of application. Unfortunately, we are not able to offer sponsorship for this role. You will be required to provide documentation as part of the recruitment process to confirm your eligibility to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20th of October. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:55pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14996": {
      "title": "Hotel Housekeeper",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: ACE Business Development Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We have a fantastic opportunity in our hotel housekeeping team part of the University of Edinburgh and are looking for housekeepers with great personalities who are team players and have outstanding attention to detail when it comes to cleanliness. In this role you will be working as part of our hotel housekeeping team and will ensure all guest bedrooms; public areas are cleaned on a daily basis. You will be either based at The Scholar Hotel; The Scott or KM Hotel & Apartments and service our bedrooms with an eye for detail and to our high standards. Grade UE02: £24,547 per annum pro-rata plus 18.1% Uplift for annual leave CSG / Accommodation, Catering and Events - Hotels Guaranteed hours: 20 hours guaranteed per year Open Ended: Permanent Positions: 2 The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you are looking for entry-level, mid-career or roles at the top of your chosen specialism, we can offer you a career where you can flourish and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, we are a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. We have a fantastic opportunity in our hotel housekeeping team part of the University of Edinburgh and are looking for housekeepers with great personalities who are team players and have outstanding attention to detail when it comes to cleanliness. In this role you will be working as part of our hotel housekeeping team and will ensure all guest bedrooms; public areas are cleaned on a daily basis. You will be either based at The Scholar Hotel; The Scott or KM Hotel & Apartments and service our bedrooms with an eye for detail and to our high standards. Opportunities to work in other areas of the hotel such as reception and housekeeping are available as we have a “one team ethos”. This post will present an opportunity to ensure great standards are delivered and excellent customer service is provided under the University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well and impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do, therefore ask yourself: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Good salary / pay rates. Working within one of the world’s leading universities. Variety every day. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held within 2 weeks from closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15001": {
      "title": "Deputy Director (Specialist Safety)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Health, Safety and Wellbeing Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Health and Safety JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom As Deputy Director you will be responsible for supporting the delivery of the Specialist Safety team’s work in the Health, Safety and Wellbeing Department, influencing and enabling a positive health and safety culture to support staff experience and making the University an excellent place to work. You will influence the evolution of the Specialist Safety advice team, contributing to and shaping the strategic direction of the three areas (radiation, biological and chemical safety), the relevant frameworks and practice. Grade UE09: £63,606 - £78,469 per annum Corporate Services Group: Health, Safety and Wellbeing Department Full Time: 35 hours per week Open Ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger – where your work has an impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. The Health, Safety and Wellbeing Department is part of the Corporate Services Group and provides a range of key activities that help the University to achieve its corporate objectives. This role will lead on the provision of specialist advice for the University and provide leadership to the Heads of the Radiation Protection, Biosafety and Chemical Safety Units. The role will involve implementing the strategy to deliver competent advice and services effectively for the University community. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. This post is full-time (35 hours per week) and will be based at Charles Stewart House, 9-16 Chambers Street, Edinburgh; however, we are open to considering flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) If you would like further information on this role, please contact Suzanne Thompson, Director of Health and Safety, at Suzanne.Thompson@ed.ac.uk . How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 26 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Corporate Services Group aims to support operational delivery of the University's mission, core strategic goals, and the ongoing plans of Colleges, Schools and other Support Groups. In support of the University's Strategy 2030, we support and enable the delivery of the University's mission and strategic goals, not the least of which is promotion to the staff and student experience. We liase with Colleges, Schools and other Professional Services Groups to: Develop a stimulating physical environment in which our students and staff can flourish, through our Estates, Accommodation and Catering and Events and Sports & Exercise teams; Enhancing the University's research and innovation success via the Edinburgh Innovations team: Promote responsibility for the wider environment through our Social Responsibility and Sustainability Team: Safeguard and service our wide-ranging research and other activities via our Health & Safety team, and our Bioresearch & Veterinary Services Team; Oversee and support Risk Management, Business Continuity and Emergency Management across the university;· Support Data-Driven Innovation initiatives across the University, via the DDI Programme Delivery Office; Promote commercial and other endeavours both via our Corporate Development Team, and through our portfolio of Subsidiary Companies, including scholarly publishing (Edinburgh University Press) and High-Performance Computing (UoE HPCx Ltd), venture investment (Old College Capital), and accommodation, catering and events (UoE Accommodation Ltd) We value the delivery of efficient, effective professional services in all areas, and strive for the highest standards in professional competence and best practice. Corporate Services Group (CSG) is led by Vice-Principal Corporate Services, Catherine Martin and comprises the Director's Office, professional services and specialisms within the University, and a number of subsidiary companies. For further information about the Corporate Services Group please visit the Corporate Services Group website. (opens in new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15006": {
      "title": "Catering Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: ACE Catering Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Catering Assistant position will be based at The View at the Easter Bush campus (Roslin, Midlothian). Working as part of the team, you will be responsible for delivering excellent customer service and fantastic food & drinks to our wide range of customers. The Café is open Monday to Friday, 7.45 am to 3.45 pm. Shift start and end times will vary between the hours of 7.00 am and 3.45 pm. GradeUE02: £24,547 per annum (pro-rata), £13.45 per hour, plus 18.1% Uplift CSG / Accommodation, Catering and Events - Catering Guaranteed Hours: Guaranteed 20 hours per year Open Ended: Permanent The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, this is a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. The Catering Assistant position will be based at The View at the Easter Bush campus (Roslin, Midlothian). Working as part of the team, you will be responsible for delivering excellent customer service and fantastic food & drinks to our wide range of customers. The Café is open Monday to Friday, 7.45 am to 3.45 pm. Shift start and end times will vary between the hours of 7.00 am and 3.45 pm. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Good salary / pay rates. Variety every day. Responsibility and autonomy. Excellent benefits and support. This post is part time (guaranteed 20 hours per year), 100% on the campus. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held 27 October 2026. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15012": {
      "title": "Fellowships Coordinator - EvEx",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Edinburgh Research Office Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom This is an exciting opportunity to contribute significantly to the successful delivery of Evidence Exchange (EvEx), an ESRC funded investment creating a national infrastructure to widen and diversify opportunities for public and civil servants to take up learning and exchange offers from universities and research organisations. The EvEx project brings together a consortium of universities, partners and specialist organisations across the UK. Grade UE07: £41,064 – £48,822 per annum, pro rata Corporate Services Group / Edinburgh Research Office Part-time: 21 hours per week Fixed term contract. This is a temporary post for a maximum of 6 months, starting as soon as possible from 1 st November 2026 and end date of 31 st May 2027 at the latest. The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute significantly to the successful delivery of Evidence Exchange (EvEx) , an ESRC funded investment creating a national infrastructure to widen and diversify opportunities for public and civil servants to take up learning and exchange offers from universities and research organisations. The EvEx project brings together a consortium of universities, partners and specialist organisations across the UK. This post will play an important role in the design and delivery of the EvEx Policy Fellowships programme within Scotland . The post holder will contribute their expert knowledge and brokering skills to understand the policy needs of the public and civil servants taking part in the programme, then find and match relevant academic expertise (from University of Edinburgh and beyond) for them to meet with. As the Fellowships Coordinator , you will work within a small team at Edinburgh based in the Edinburgh Research Office, as well as communicating with counterparts across the EvEx consortium. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Working within one of the world’s leading universities Interesting work Flexible/hybrid where the roles allow Responsibility and autonomy This post is part-time (21 hours per week). View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held on Thursday 29 th October. Working in Edinburgh Research Office you will be part of a professional, supportive and friendly team ; at the heart of University research support, working in collaboration with academics and research support professionals across the institution. The breadth of our remit means you will get an overview of research support activity while you build your skills in your specialism. Gaining new knowledge and skills to help you develop in this role and progress in your career is a priority. We offer colleagues a stimulating working environment based on our values of integrity, expertise, community, and collaboration. Our work is fast‐paced and intense; you will thrive here if you are motivated, customer‐orientated, and passionate about providing an excellent service to enable our researchers to do amazing work . "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14778": {
      "title": "Image Analysis Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Centre for Clinical Brain Sciences Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom The postholder will manage, process, and analyse magnetic resonance (MR) imaging data for clinical studies in multiple sclerosis (MS), primarily PrecisionMS and FutureMS. UE06: £34 ,610 - £39,906 per annum CMVM / Centre for Clinical Brain Sciences Full time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 31 July 2028 The Opportunity: Your next step starts here. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, and be recognised for your contributions. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our student’s performance and wellbeing. The postholder will manage, process, and analyse magnetic resonance (MR) imaging data for clinical studies in multiple sclerosis (MS), primarily PrecisionMS and FutureMS. The primary responsibilities will include data management, quality checking, running established pipelines and manual segmentation of brain images to assess intracranial volume, white matter lesions, and brain volumes, as well as generating parametric maps for quantitative imaging analysis pipelines. There may also be opportunities to undertake small supervised research projects within the remit of these clinical studies. The postholder will be based in Edinburgh within the MS Imaging and Anne Rowling Clinic teams at the University of Edinburgh, supporting the delivery of imaging outcomes across the Clinic’s research portfolio. To be successful in this role, you will have an undergraduate degree in a relevant subject and experience in basic MRI processing, with a good understanding of neuroanatomy and neuroimaging data. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Working within one of the world’s leading universities View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) Informal enquiries can be sent to the FutureMS study manager at future-ms@ed.ac.uk. How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 19 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences We host research and postgraduate training in neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research. We are one of six schools in the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh and are home to the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research. Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Our institute fosters collaborative neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research to tackle some of the world's most significant health challenges. Our research explores health and disease across the body and life course – from tiny changes in cells to the lived experience of patients. Together, we work to advance the understanding, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of major health conditions including heart disease, stroke, metabolic dysfunction, mental illness, dementia and neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders. Empowered by genetics, advanced imaging, artificial intelligence and clinical trials, our research seamlessly connects discovery science with clinical care. Our work is internationally acclaimed, achieving 4* and world-leading classifications for the majority of our research, health and wealth impacts and research papers in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 assessment, the UK’s system for assessing the quality of research in UK higher education institutions. As part of driving progress in our core research areas, we host internationally leading research centres and state-of-the-art technologies: Anne Rowling Regenerative Neurology Clinic British Heart Foundation (BHF) Centre of Research Excellence Centre for Pesticide Suicide Prevention | CPSP Euan MacDonald Centre for Motor Neurone Disease Research Edinburgh Clinical Research Facility Edinburgh Imaging Jennifer Brown Research Laboratory Medical Research Council/British Hearth Foundation Centre of Research Excellence in Advanced Cardiac Therapies MS Society Edinburgh Centre for MS Research | MS Society Muir Maxwell Epilepsy Centre Patrick Wild Centre Row Fogo Centre for Research into Ageing and the Brain Simons Initiative for the Developing Brain (SIDB) UK Dementia Research Institute at Edinburgh UKRI Hub for Metabolic Psychiatry Our institute community Our researchers drive cutting-edge science, enabling our students and working hand in hand with our expert technical and professional services colleagues. We are committed to creating a collaborative, inclusive and welcoming community, where everyone feels supported, empowered and inspired to achieve their best. Training and education We offer a wide range of postgraduate training opportunities. Students learn from world-class researchers, access outstanding facilities and get support to develop. Our graduates go on to successful careers in academia, research, healthcare and industry worldwide. Our colleagues are also leading contributors to undergraduate programmes. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14905": {
      "title": "Contract & Supplier Management Specialist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Procurement Office incorporating Printing Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We’re looking for great people to join our Contract & Supplier Management (CSM) team in our award-winning Procurement department. Grade UE07: £41,064 – £48,822 per annum Finance Directorate / Procurement Department Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 12 months contract Edinburgh University is a world leader. Our vision is “To become world class in everything we do, recognised as a trusted strategic partner in the successful delivery of the University’s Strategy 2030”. We’re looking for great people to join our Contract & Supplier Management (CSM) team in our award-winning Procurement department. Procurement at Edinburgh University is undergoing a major transformation as we extend the scope of our influence and impact across our £1bn world-renowned organisation. To deliver on our commitments we are now looking for people who make things happen, who challenge the status quo, are excited about working in a diverse, dynamic environment and really want to make a change. Be part of our new Procurement journey. The Opportunity: We are looking for a Contract & Supplier Management Specialist to join our Strategic Procurement team. The Department currently comprises of four ‘Category’ Teams; Information Services & IT, Professional Campus Services, Estates & Buildings, and Laboratories & Medical - of which the CSM Specialists will be allocated. Embedded within the Strategic Procurement function, the Contract & Supplier Management Specialist is responsible for supporting the delivery and development of the contract and supplier management activities within the Procurement Department, ensuring the right outcomes are delivered and the performance of contracts is maximised, as well as adding value through enhancement of supplier relationship management. Through the development of strong internal and external relationships, the post-holder will effectively manage a range of key suppliers to maximise the outcomes and benefits to the University through supplier relationship management and contract management processes, with the objective of delivering the following: Effective management and monitoring of supplier performance and supply chain risks; Commercial and operational value through its contracts with its suppliers, reducing costs and increasing value-add; Environmental and social impact through the life of the contracts, driving forward the responsible procurement agenda; and Identification of opportunities for in-life innovation and creativity to drive efficiency and consistency. The post-holder will be expected to contribute to the University’s financial wellbeing and international research profile, by delivering cash savings and other cost efficiencies; and supporting the knowledge creation and transfer which lies at the heart of the University’s Strategy 2030. Successful candidate will join a forward-thinking, ambitious and supportive procurement team, already well advanced in a service transformation programme. As part of the wider Finance Directorate, we aim to deliver world-class services to the University’s academic teaching and research staff and our professional services colleagues, in keeping with the institution’s Top-20 World Ranking. You will engage directly with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders, ensuring that Procurement delivers innovative, fit for purpose solutions which are aligned to University objectives and Government policy. Candidates will be encouraged to exercise initiative and discretion, taking ownership of CSM projects that deliver maximum benefit across the University. Your skills and attributes for success: Self-motivated and proactive, with a passion to help shape and develop the service. Good commercial competence, risk management, and strong negotiation skills. Personal credibility and ethics; capacity and confidence to manage contracts and deliver change with aptitude for implementing and improving best-practice procedures. Demonstrable experience of negotiation skills. Relevant contract and supplier management experience. Good computing literacy and numerical analysis skills with excellent attention to detail. Strong communication and negotiation skills and commercial judgement. The job description can be found here How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 19 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14983": {
      "title": "Sales and Information Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Communications and Marketing Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a Sales & Information Assistant to assist in sales and information provision within the University of Edinburgh Visitor Centre & Gift Shop at 2 Charles St, Edinburgh EH8 9AD. Grade UE03: £24,729 - £25,804 per annum pro-rata as part-time USG / Communications and Marketing / Visitor Centre & Gift Shop Part-time: 14 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) The Opportunity: To work at the University's only Visitor Centre and official gift shop in the central campus. You'll be a joining an existing team of fellow students and will be supervised by experienced retail professional colleagues. It is a lively and positive environment where the key focus is on providing help and a warm welcome to all visitors to the University. This role is part-time (14 hours per week) with a start date in October 2026. Your skills and attributes for success: Recent previous experience in retail and dealing with customers is essential. Friendly, courteous & always willing to help. Attention to detail and fully computer literate. Working knowledge of the University, its campuses and the city. Have initiative particularly in managing own workload. Flexible and adaptable. Willing to learn and develop new skills. Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens in a new link). Application Information Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 19 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Communications and Marketing (CAM) has the demanding role of promoting and enhancing the profile of the University locally, regionally and globally in various mediums. Our services and areas of expertise include public relations and press office services, media training, prospectus and promotional material production, advertising, media selection, events, video production, web publishing, corporate merchandising and information/exhibition provision via our visitor centre. As the University of Edinburgh is a well-known worldwide brand, we require the highest level of professionals to retain and enhance our reputation. To find out more, visit https://www.ed.ac.uk/communications-marketing/about-us "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14990": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: The Roslin Institute Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an experienced Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute study the investigate the genomic regions to design novel gene edits related to feed efficiency and productivity, as well as their impact in breeding programs in low- and medium-income countries. Full time, Fixed Term for 36 months or until 31/3/2030. Grade UE07- £41,064- £48,822 R(D)SVS / The Roslin Institute Full time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 36 months or until 31/03/30. The Opportunity: We are looking for an experienced Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute study the investigate the genomic regions to design novel gene edits related to feed efficiency and productivity, as well as their impact in breeding programs in low- and medium-income countries. The successful candidate will join a new recently created group in the areas of livestock hologenomics, in collaboration with other groups within the Roslin Institute. The role is ideal for a researcher with a PhD with experience on quantitative genetics or population genetics. MSc with excellent records will also be considered. The post is full-time (35 hours per week) . The University of Edinburgh is a world class organisation. We are a large organisation covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services we support the University’s research and teaching activities, offering many career and role specialisms. The salary for this post is £41,064 per annum. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter -2 reference letters As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 19 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. The Roslin Institute receives strategic investment from the Biotechnology & Biological Sciences Research Council (BBSRC) and provides national capability to achieve sustainable agriculture, control diseases and enhance health through pioneering animal bioscience. The Institute is a key part of the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework exercise, a joint submission by the School and Scotland’s Rural College placed first in the United Kingdom for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences. The Institute is home to over 400 staff, students and visitors, including around 65 Group Leaders and Tenure Track Fellows on a path to independence. It has a vibrant programme ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin/work-study/postgraduate ) (opens in a new browser tab) of postgraduate research and a thriving student community. Research at the Institute is organised across eight divisions (Quantitative Biology, Genome Biology, Functional Genetics, Translational Bioscience, Bacteriology, Virology, Immunology, and Epidemiology) and two Institute Strategic Programmes funded by the BBSRC from 2023-28 (Genes & Traits for Healthy Animals and Prevention & Control of Infectious Diseases). Over £380M has been invested in physical infrastructure at Easter Bush since 2008, including for research on key terrestrial and aquatic farmed animals. This includes the National Avian Research Facility, a £24M Large Animal Research & Imaging Facility, Aquaculture facilities for fish and shellfish, and an Innovation Centre that is now fully occupied with commercial tenants. The campus serves as a key innovation hub for the Agri-Tech, Aquaculture and Animal Bioscience (3A) sector and Roslin has extensive partnerships with businesses, including animal breeding and pharmaceutical businesses able to translate its discoveries at global scale. The Institute has a positive and inclusive culture and holds a prestigious Athena SWAN Gold award in recognition of its commitment to advance the careers of women in science. Further details of the research conducted at the Institute can be found at The Roslin Institute website ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin ) (opens in a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14921": {
      "title": "SCF Lecturer in Fashion",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Design Category: Academic JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Edinburgh College of Art is seeking to recruit an SCF Lecturer in Fashion on a part-time, open-ended basis with contract review date of January 2028. Grade UE08: £50,253 - £61,759 per annum pro rata CAHSS, Edinburgh College of Art Part-time: 28 hours per week Open-ended with contract review date of January 2028 The Opportunity: Unlocking your career. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. The post-holder will be a motivated practitioner, with in-depth knowledge of a variety of approaches to creative and innovative fashion practice. These skills will be evidenced by appropriate academic achievement and demonstrable experience of delivering teaching for the unique requirements of transnational education. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. The University is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion (EDI) for all, and these principles are at the heart of our institution’s values and strategic vision – We are diverse, inclusive and accessible to al We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 26 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14924": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Heritage",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Department: Archaeology Category: Academic JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom Archaeology, within The School of History, Classics and Archaeology , is seeking a Lecturer in Heritage Grade UE08: £50,253 to £61,759 per annum, pro rata. Please note the successful candidate will be appointed at the starting point of the grade. School of History, Classics and Archaeology / Archaeology Fixed term, part-time (0.4 FTE from 1st January 2027 to 30 June 2027 and 0.8 FTE from 1st July 2027 to 31st Dec 2027) for 12 months. Where curiosity becomes impact. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The Opportunity: The School of History, Classics and Archaeology is looking for a Lecturer, to deliver and assess courses on the Archaeology undergraduate and Cultural Heritage Futures Masters programme. Responsibilities include the role of Course Organiser for selected courses (including preparation and delivery of content and marking of assignments), contributions to co-taught courses, supervision and assessment of dissertation students, student support, contribution to the academic administration, and assisting in course and programme development. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in heritage studies, and who can contribute to the School's growing international profile in the areas of heritage and identity, the politics of the past, and digital heritage though we welcome applicants from a broad range of relevant backgrounds. Your skills and attributes for success: Degree relevant to teaching area. Demonstrable ability to apply knowledge, skills and techniques to teaching and ability to understand the needs of a group and individual students and to provide appropriate guidance. Excellent communication skills, including the ability to explain concepts in a clear and understandable manner. Specialist knowledge of the discipline/topics under discussion. Understanding of the principles of teaching, learning and assessment. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Working within one of the world’s leading universities Contributing to the work and purpose of the University View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter Applicants should provide contact details for two referees who will be contacted only if the application is successful at the shortlisting stage. Applicants who do not wish for anyone to be contacted until after an offer has been made, should indicate this on their application. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. Please note the successful candidate will be appointed at the starting point of the grade. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 16 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held shortly after the application closing date. Informal enquiries should be directed to the Head of Archaeology, Prof. Catriona Pickard, Catriona.Pickard@ed.ac.uk Archaeological research at Edinburgh is focussed geographically primarily on Europe (including Britain and Ireland, and especially Scotland) and the Middle East, and extends in time from early prehistory to the medieval period. The main teaching clusters are: prehistoric archaeology, classical and historical archaeology, and science-based archaeology, notably including osteoarchaeology and forensic anthropology. Certain research interests, e.g. landscape archaeology, are represented across these clusters. Academic staff are actively involved in field research and excavation, presently including in Turkey, Italy and over continental Europe. First established a century ago, Archaeology at Edinburgh now has a sizeable and vibrant postgraduate community which complements the academic staff, whose numbers have grown over recent years. Alongside numerous international collaborations, Archaeology also benefits from strong links with the range of archaeological institutions and applied companies based in Scotland’s capital, as well as within and beyond the School in which it is based in the University. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14968": {
      "title": "Procurement Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Procurement Office incorporating Printing Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We’re looking for self motivated, enthusiastic procurement professional to join our award-winning procurement team. Procurement at Edinburgh University has undergone a major transformation as we extended the scope of our influence and impact across our £1bn world-renowned organisation. Our vision is “To become world class in everything we do, recognised as a trusted strategic partner in the successful delivery of the University’s Strategy 2030”. To deliver on our commitments we are now looking for people who make things happen, who challenge the status quo, are excited about working in a diverse, dynamic environment and really want to make a change. Edinburgh University is a world leader. Be part of our new Procurement journey. Grade UE08: £50,253 – £61,759 per annum Procurement, University of Edinburgh (Finance Department) Full time: 35 Hours per week Open Ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: We’re looking for self motivated, enthusiastic procurement professional to join our award-winning procurement team. Procurement at Edinburgh University has undergone a major transformation as we extended the scope of our influence and impact across our £1bn world-renowned organisation. Our vision is “To become world class in everything we do, recognised as a trusted strategic partner in the successful delivery of the University’s Strategy 2030”. To deliver on our commitments we are now looking for people who make things happen, who challenge the status quo, are excited about working in a diverse, dynamic environment and really want to make a change. Edinburgh University is a world leader. Be part of our new Procurement journey. We are looking for a Procurement Manager to join one of our 4 x Category Teams; Laboratories, Medical & Veterinary Medical (Labs) Estates and Facilities (Estates) Information Services (IS) Professional & Campus Services (PCS) Reporting to the relevant Category Head, the successful candidate will be responsible for the tendering, award and contract management of a range of procurement projects; as well as strategic initiatives stemming from market engagement and spend analyses. The post-holder will be expected to contribute to the University’s financial wellbeing and international research profile, by delivering cash savings and other cost efficiencies; and supporting the knowledge creation and transfer which lies at the heart of the University’s Strategy 2030. The post-holder will join a forward-thinking, ambitious and supportive procurement team, already well advanced in a service transformation programme. As part of the wider Finance Directorate, we aim to deliver world-class services to the University’s academic teaching and research staff and our professional services colleagues, in keeping with the institution’s Top-20 World Ranking. You will engage directly with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders, ensuring that Procurement delivers innovative, fit for purpose solutions which are aligned to University objectives and Government policy. Candidates will be encouraged to exercise initiative and discretion, taking ownership of strategic procurements that deliver maximum benefit across the University. Your skills and attributes for success: Self-motivated and proactive, with a passion to help shape and develop the service. Practical experience and sound theoretical knowledge of end-to-end tendering procedures, including use of eTendering systems. A solid grounding of in-life contract and supplier relationship management. Good computing literacy and numerical analysis skills with excellent attention to detail. Strong communication and negotiation skills and commercial judgement. The job description can be found in the advert pack. Click here . How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab ) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Finance Department is responsible for the effective financial management, stewardship and long-term financial health of the University, which has a turnover in excess of £900m per year. It exercises control over the University’s finances, provides expert financial guidance particularly for key decision making, and provides an effective services and information for the financial conduct of the University’s business. It follows core values and behaviours to value and strengthen its team and delivery of its services – One Team; Expert; Innovation; Customer Focused; and Transparent. http://www.ed.ac.uk/schools-departments/finance/home "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14023": {
      "title": "Food and Beverage Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/10/2026 Department: ACE Business Development Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an enthusiastic and customer-focused individual to join our Food & Beverage team at The Scott, The Scholar, and KM Hotels & Apartments. This is a fantastic opportunity to work in a professional hospitality environment, delivering high-quality service to a wide range of guests. Grade UE02: £24,547 per annum including a 15% premium band CSG / Accommodation, Catering and Events / The Scott, The Scholar and KM Hotels & Apartments Full-time: 35 hours per week, 5 over 7 days including weekends Open-ended (permanent) Vacancies: 1 Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, this is a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. The Opportunity: We are looking for an enthusiastic and customer-focused individual to join our Food & Beverage team at The Scott, The Scholar, and KM Hotels & Apartments. This is a fantastic opportunity to work in a professional hospitality environment, delivering high-quality service to a wide range of guests. View the full job description How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Good salary / pay rates. Responsibility and autonomy. Excellent benefits and support. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary plus a share of a 12.5% service charge. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 13 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held within 2 weeks after closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14473": {
      "title": "Junior Sous Chef",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/10/2026 Department: ACE Catering Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an enthusiastic Junior Sous Chef who will support the operational service delivery to our students and commercial guests throughout the year. UE04: £26,093 to £28,778 per annum plus 15% Premium Band for flexible working Corporate Services Group / Accommodation, Catering and Events Full-time: 35 hours per week, 5 over 7 days including weekends Open-ended (permanent) contract Vacancies: 1 Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, we are a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. The Opportunity: As a Junior Sous Chef , you will support the operational service delivery to our students and commercial guests throughout the year. The role will be based at our largest catering unit; The John McIntyre Conference Centre located within Pollock Halls. The John McIntyre Conference Centre is a 500-seater buffet style restaurant that provides approximately 28,000 meals per week during our student period and breakfast, lunch and dinner to our commercial guests year-round. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: • Interesting work. • Good salary / pay rates. • Responsibility and autonomy. • Excellent benefits and support. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 13 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held 2 weeks after closing date . Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14883": {
      "title": "Data Engineer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Royal Dick Veterinary Studies Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Academic Support JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a methodological, highly organised, rigorous and service oriented individual to manage SEBI-L livestock data derived from low and middle-income countries (LMICs). The post holder will take the lead in designing and maintaining a robust high-quality database of livestock data, ensuring that data is clean, harmonized, well described and governed. It is essential that the person who fills the role has strong attention to detail, and has prior experience working with highly regulated, complex data, with experience working in the private sector. Full time (35 hours), fixed term - until end of December 2030. Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum CMVM / R(D)SVS – SEBI-Livestock Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: until end of December 2030 The Opportunity: Use your knowledge to drive change. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. Do you want to help shape the future of global livestock impact by building the trusted data foundations that power evidence-based decision making and AI-ready insights? Are you excited by the challenge of untangling complex, imperfect datasets and turning them into secure, trusted, and AI-ready information assets? Are you an experienced Data Engineer who thrives on bringing clarity, governance, and structure to complex data environments while driving best practice across an organisation? Are you motivated to you build trusted, secure, and well-governed data systems from complex and often undocumented data sources, while helping an organisation become truly AI-ready? We are looking for a methodological, highly organised, rigorous and service oriented individual to manage SEBI-L livestock data derived from low and middle-income countries (LMICs). The post holder will take the lead in designing and maintaining a robust high-quality database of livestock data, ensuring that data is clean, harmonized, well described and governed. It is essential that the person who fills the role has strong attention to detail, and has prior experience working with highly regulated, complex data, with experience working in the private sector. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Working within one of the world’s leading universities Interesting work This post is full-time(35 hours per week); We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14952": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Cognitive Evolution over Deep Time",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: GeoSciences Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Cognitive Evolution over Deep Time to join the School of Geosciences on a full-time, fixed-term contract. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum CSE / School of GeoSciences Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: from January 2027 until 31 December 2028 The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. The University is part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. The Opportunity: We seek an interdisciplinary scientist to join our international team studying the evolution of brains, intelligence, and cognition over deep time. Our Deep Time Cognition team—funded by the Swedish Research Council—combines expertise in cognitive zoology, neurobiology, and palaeontology to understand how cognition and brains have evolved in concert over eons, and what has driven this evolution. By working together, we are creating an initial atlas of how neuroanatomy correlates with cognition expressed in behaviour. Drawing on these results we will then recreate the cognition of long extinct species, by making brain models based on fossils. This will make it possible to pinpoint cognitive changes in geological time, which can be correlated with major events in the history of Earth. More information on our team can be found here: https://www.deeptimecognition.lu.se/ The University of Edinburgh component of the team specializes in palaeontology and phylogenetics, and contributes data including CT scans of fossils and reconstructions of the brains of extinct species, and large phylogenetic trees and datasets for testing trends in evolution across genealogy and time. The Edinburgh team is led by Professor Stephen Brusatte (co-PI on the research grant). The postdoctoral fellow will be a key member of the team. They will gather and synthesize data on the anatomy, size, and shape of the brains (via CT brain endocasts) for tetrapods across time (350+ million years of evolution), working closely with Brusatte and PhD students and external co-PI Lawrence Witmer (Ohio University) who have been gathering data on tetrapod subgroups (extinct dinosaurs, squamates, and amphibians). They will use Bayesian statistics to test for trends in brain size change over time and phylogeny, test for correlations between brain size and neuron counts in extant species, and make the most robust predictions for neuron counts in extinct species, including dinosaurs like Tyrannosaurus rex , contributing to an exciting current debate in the fields of cognitive zoology and palaeontology. People have always been at the heart of our work. We value colleagues with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Opportunities for publication View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV listing academic positions, degrees, publications, awards, public engagement, and other applicable information (please limit to five pages) - Cover letter outlining your interest in the position, how you can contribute to the team and its goals, and a summary of your experience working with 3D CT data and phylogenetic comparative and Bayesian statistics As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates of any demographic and particularly encourage applications from a variety of academic fields: palaeontology, anatomy, neurobiology, cognitive biology. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is Friday 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . Interviews will be held the week commencing 2 November 2026. The School of GeoSciences explores the factors and forces that shape our world. The School aims to understand the world through fundamental curiosity-driven research and to support prescient decision-making at individual to global scales. We undertake world-leading research; offer new ways of understanding natural and social drivers of change; provide inter-and trans-disciplinary solutions; and work in partnership to improve livelihoods and explore ways to manage the environment that are both sustainable and socially equitable. With over 500 academics, researchers and research students, we are the largest and most successful interdisciplinary grouping of geoscientists and geographers in the UK. Research activity is coordinated within three main Research Institutes – Global Change, Earth and Planetary Science, and Geography and the Lived Environment – and within smaller research groupings that reach across and beyond the School. A distinctive feature of the School is the combination of academic strength, intellectual breadth and societal relevance. Our interdisciplinary research and teaching builds on established core disciplines (ecology, environmental sciences, geography, geophysics, meteorology, oceanography) to provide a variety of approaches to understanding the world (including, for example, system-scale modelling, process studies and the development of urban and social theory). The School’s research covers fundamental ‘blue-skies’ questions, as well as having application to key societal challenges including inequality and vulnerability; urban precarity; nature and cultural meaning; development and sustainability; climate and environmental change; energy, food and water security; health and wellbeing; natural resources; and natural hazards. The School of GeoSciences aims to recognise and value diversity in our staff and students, and to support flexible and family-friendly working. More details about the School is available from http://www.ed.ac.uk/geosciences "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14199": {
      "title": "Security Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Security Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Estates and Facilities JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We are seeking four full time Security Officers to join the Security Department at the University of Edinburgh. The Security department provides 24/7 security cover across the University of Edinburgh. This ensures that students, staff, and visitors are kept as safe as possible and that buildings are kept secure and free from damage. Grade UE03: £24,729 - £25,804 per annum, plus 30% premium band CSG / Security Department / Estates Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) contract The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Opportunity: The Security Officer routinely patrols the University on foot and in vehicle as well as assisting in the control room. They are often the first point of contact for staff and students when a serious incident or crime occurs. They provide immediate support, guidance, and reassurance. Routinely they also respond to fire and intruder alarms. An integral part of the role is providing welfare support and the subsequent signposting to other agencies. The care of students and staff is at the forefront of the role. We are looking to appoint a candidate with excellent communication skills (both verbal and written), computer literate, able to remain calm under pressure and a good listener. We also require a full driving licence and a good attention to detail. View the full jo b description How to apply Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: CV People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Good salary/pay rates Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 26 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. The University of Edinburgh was founded in 1583 and currently occupies more than 550 academic, administrative and residential buildings throughout the city of Edinburgh across 5 campuses; Central Area, Kings Buildings, Easter Bush, Bio- Quarter and the Western General Hospital. There are also sporting facilities at Peffermill. The estate is an exciting mix of eras and architecture. Whilst many of the buildings enjoy listed status and are integral to the unique qualities and characteristics of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Edinburgh, many more have been added over the years as the University has grown. The portfolio ranges from nationally important, iconic, historical buildings through to state of the art teaching, research and sporting facilities. The University of Edinburgh has a significant programme of estates investment underway, in both capital development and also in the infrastructure and resilience of the estate. This investment will help to ensure that the university remains at the forefront of world leading teaching, learning and research. The Estates Department is part of the Corporate Services Group and provides a range of key activities that help the University to achieve its corporate objectives. It is an integrated centre of excellence responsible for implementing decisions related to the buildings, fabric and infrastructure of the University. It provides specialist advice to the University on strategic estates issues as well as its statutory obligations. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14948": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer/Lecturer in Small Animal Oncology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Veterinary Clinical JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom This is an excellent opportunity to join a busy, world-renowned oncology service, providing medical oncology expertise in the School’s Hospital for Small Animals, as well as the delivery and assessment of our BVM&S undergraduate degree programme and postgraduate training and education programmes. You will be working alongside other Diplomates in Small Animal Oncology and Radiation Oncology, five medical oncology (and one radiation oncology) residents, one oncology clinician teacher (intern), five specialised oncology nurses, and two therapeutic radiographers. Open ended, full time - 35 hours per week. Grade UE08: £50,253 to £61,759 per annum OR Grade UE09: £63,606 to £78,469 per annum (both grades will include additional Market Pay Supplement which is reviewed annually) CMVM / Royal (Dick School of Veterinary Studies / Hospital for Small Animals Full-Time: 35 hours per week Open-Ended: permanent Your next step starts here. Be part of something bigger . The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. The Opportunity: We are looking for a Senior Lecturer in Small Animal Oncology (according to experience). This is an excellent opportunity to join a busy, world-renowned oncology service, providing medical oncology expertise in the School’s Hospital for Small Animals, as well as the delivery and assessment of our BVM&S undergraduate degree programme and postgraduate training and education programmes. You will be working alongside other Diplomates in Small Animal Oncology and Radiation Oncology, five medical oncology (and one radiation oncology) residents, one oncology clinician teacher (intern), five specialised oncology nurses, and two therapeutic radiographers. Moreover, you will be working alongside specialists across numerous other disciplines such as Surgery, Internal Medicine, Emergency and Critical Care, Neurology, Cardiology, Diagnostic Imaging, Anaesthesia, Ophthalmology, Dermatology, and others, to provide the best possible care for each and every patient. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies is an ideal environment to develop or further nurture your clinical interests and research ambitions from a campus with strong collaborative links. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies is situated on the Easter Bush Campus 6 miles south of Edinburgh on the edge of the Pentland Hills and houses outstanding teaching and clinical facilities (including on site radiation therapy facilities lead by a specialist in Radiation Oncology) as well as the Roslin Institute, one of the world’s leading veterinary research centres. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). The salary for this post starts at £50,253 (UE08) or £63,606 (UE09) per annum according to experience plus Market Force Supplement (reviewed annually, currently 8%), and CPD allowance. Click here to view the copy of the full Senior Lecturer in Small Animal Oncology job description (opens in a new browser tab) Click here to view the copy of the full Lecturer in Small Animal Oncology job description (opens in a new browser tab) Your skills and attributes for success: Post-graduate experience and further qualification in Small Animal Oncology, at ECVIM or ACVIM Diploma-holder level (board certified, or board eligible) A degree registrable with the RCVS Experience in teaching, learning and assessment in a veterinary school An ability to work alone but also to supervise and instruct colleagues and students Demonstrable research output (clinical and/or basic science/translational) Appropriate English language certificate Informal enquiries should be directed to laura.blackwood@ed.ac.uk . This role is conditional on a successful Level 2 Disclosure Scotland check. How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 26 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14967": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: The Roslin Institute Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in livestock genome engineering to join a Gates Foundation-funded project aiming to improve dairy productivity in cattle in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs). Working alongside quantitative geneticists and computational biologists, you will develop and deploy genome engineering approaches in cattle cells to test genetic changes prioritised by AI models and genetic studies as candidates for improving milk production and feed efficiency. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum CMVM / Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies / Roslin Institute Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 3 years or until 31 March 2030 The Opportunity: We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in livestock genome engineering to join a Gates Foundation-funded project aiming to improve dairy productivity in cattle in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs). Working alongside quantitative geneticists and computational biologists, you will develop and deploy genome engineering approaches in cattle cells to test genetic changes prioritised by AI models and genetic studies as candidates for improving milk production and feed efficiency. You will join a team of wet-lab and computational biologists using genetic improvement to tackle challenges in livestock production, from productivity and sustainability to disease. The role offers a unique opportunity to combine genome engineering skills with understanding of gene regulation to address real-world challenges facing tropical small-holder livestock systems. You will build an international network spanning academia, industry and breeding partners, while contributing to the wider engineering biology environment at Roslin (https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin/engineering-biology/about).. For more details on the post please contact Dr Musa Hassan (Musa.Hassan@roslin.ed.ac.uk). View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply To apply, please include a cover letter stating your fit and motivation for the position and a CV detailing your academic qualifications and relevant research experience and track record. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 19 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14693": {
      "title": "Heritage Collections Digitisation Photographer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Department: Digital Library Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Library and information management JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom As a Heritage Collections Digitisation Photographer , you will be responsible for the high-quality digitisation of the library’s heritage collections, using a range of photographic equipment including medium format cameras to create images for preservation and access. This work directly supports our strategic objective to increase access to and use of our world-class collections in teaching, learning and research. Grade UE05: £29,588 - £33,951 per annum pro rata Library & University Collections / Digital Library Part Time: 31.5 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) contract The Opportunity: Your next step starts here. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. As a Heritage Collections Digitisation Photographer, you will be responsible for the high-quality digitisation of the library’s heritage collections, using a range of photographic equipment including medium format cameras to create images for preservation and access. This work directly supports our strategic objective to increase access to and use of our world-class collections in teaching, learning and research. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Excellent benefits and support This post is part time (31.5 hours per week) and we are open to considering flexible working patterns. View the full job description How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter - A portfolio of 5-10 relevant professional photographs As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 16 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Library helps people to grow their knowledge and create new connections through open services, collections and environments, sparking ideas that change the world. Library & University Collections teams (approximately 200 staff) provide leadership and oversight for the University's library and museum collections and services, and lead the University's transition to Open Scholarship. Collections comprise over 2 million print volumes, 2 million e-books, 200,000 e-journals and 100 kilometres of Heritage Collections which include rare books, archives, artworks and musical instruments. Library & University Collections deliver highly-regarded services to support teaching, learning and research across the University and wider communities. Services and access to collections are provided online and onsite through the Main Library, site libraries, the Centre for Research Collections, award-winning makerspace services and St Cecilia’s Hall Concert Room & Music Museum. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14799": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Centre for Clinical Brain Sciences Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom There is an opportunity to join Mahad lab research team, at the University of Edinburgh, that focuses basic science research on mechanisms of neurodegeneration in multiple sclerosis with a specific interest in mitochondria. UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum CMVM / Centre for Clinical Brain Sciences Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 12 months The Opportunity: There is an opportunity to join Mahad lab research team, at the University of Edinburgh, that focuses basic science research on mechanisms of neurodegeneration in multiple sclerosis with a specific interest in mitochondria. Mahad lab has developed a number of animal models to better understand the pathophysiology of progressive MS and identify potentially neuroprotective drugs. The applicant will focus on characterising mitochondrial function and mitochondrial DNA at a single cell level in an animal model with chronic neurodegeneration, in collaboration with Professor Ken Smith based at the University College London. A skill set to assess mitochondrial function and mitochondrial DNA at a single cell level in brain tissue will be highly desirable. The candidate will have the opportunity to interact closely with researches at the Dementia Research Institute, led by Professor Siddharthan Chandran. The opportunities at the University of Edinburgh will enable the candidate to be part of something bigger. For early career researchers Edinburgh University offers opportunities where the candidate can flourish and make a real difference. Academic As a post-doctoral researcher, the candidate will have a balance between supervised and prescriptive work and that stems from candidates own initiative. The work will primarily be based at the Chancellor’s building in Little France, Edinburgh. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you This post is full-time (35 hours per week); We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) . How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences We host research and postgraduate training in neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research. We are one of six schools in the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh and are home to the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research. Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Our institute fosters collaborative neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research to tackle some of the world's most significant health challenges. Our research explores health and disease across the body and life course – from tiny changes in cells to the lived experience of patients. Together, we work to advance the understanding, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of major health conditions including heart disease, stroke, metabolic dysfunction, mental illness, dementia and neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders. Empowered by genetics, advanced imaging, artificial intelligence and clinical trials, our research seamlessly connects discovery science with clinical care. Our work is internationally acclaimed, achieving 4* and world-leading classifications for the majority of our research, health and wealth impacts and research papers in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 assessment, the UK’s system for assessing the quality of research in UK higher education institutions. As part of driving progress in our core research areas, we host internationally leading research centres and state-of-the-art technologies: Anne Rowling Regenerative Neurology Clinic British Heart Foundation (BHF) Centre of Research Excellence Centre for Pesticide Suicide Prevention | CPSP Euan MacDonald Centre for Motor Neurone Disease Research Edinburgh Clinical Research Facility Edinburgh Imaging Jennifer Brown Research Laboratory Medical Research Council/British Hearth Foundation Centre of Research Excellence in Advanced Cardiac Therapies MS Society Edinburgh Centre for MS Research | MS Society Muir Maxwell Epilepsy Centre Patrick Wild Centre Row Fogo Centre for Research into Ageing and the Brain Simons Initiative for the Developing Brain (SIDB) UK Dementia Research Institute at Edinburgh UKRI Hub for Metabolic Psychiatry Our institute community Our researchers drive cutting-edge science, enabling our students and working hand in hand with our expert technical and professional services colleagues. We are committed to creating a collaborative, inclusive and welcoming community, where everyone feels supported, empowered and inspired to achieve their best. Training and education We offer a wide range of postgraduate training opportunities. Students learn from world-class researchers, access outstanding facilities and get support to develop. Our graduates go on to successful careers in academia, research, healthcare and industry worldwide. Our colleagues are also leading contributors to undergraduate programmes. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14991": {
      "title": "KTP Associate in Building Physics Automation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Institute for Infrastructure and Environment Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom The University of Edinburgh and Cosysense have been awarded a Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP) funded by Innovate UK. The KTP Associate will lead the development and deployment of advanced thermal comfort and grid demand response physics models into Cosysense’s AI control system. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum, with an additional market pay supplement bringing the total salary to up to £55,000 per annum CSE / School of Engineering / Cosysense Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 24 months Location: Primarily based at Cosysense The Opportunity: Where curiosity becomes impact. This is a KTP Associate to contribute to the Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP) project that aims to develop next-generation thermal comfort and energy optimisation models for scalable deployment. Funded by InnovateUK, the KTP project is co-led by the Institute for Infrastructure and Environment and Institute for Energy Systems at the University of Edinburgh (UoE), and Cosysense . Cosysense reduces electricity costs and carbon emissions in commercial buildings by delivering optimised comfort through AI-native climate control powered by sensors, digital twins, and autonomous control systems. The KTP Associate will lead the development and deployment of advanced thermal comfort and grid demand response physics models into Cosysense’s AI control system. You will act as the bridge between academic research and real-world application, translating cutting-edge building physics and human comfort modelling into production-ready algorithms controlling HVAC systems in live buildings. In line with the KTP model, the Associate is embedded at Cosysense day to day, engaging regularly with the University of Edinburgh for knowledge transfer and academic supervision. This role will be primarily based at Cosysense. The role combines: Research (building physics and energy systems) Applied AI / modelling Real-world deployment and validation in commercial buildings A unique employer in a vibrant city. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. Make a meaningful impact. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. Click here to view the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV (2 pages maximum, A4) - Cover letter (2 pages maximum, A4) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds and particularly encourage those from groups underrepresented in academia and STEM. Our team has significant experience in creating and delivering respectful, inclusive and supportive environments where all members can thrive and it is supported by the vision for our research, behaviours and norms. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Engineering has been taught at Edinburgh in one form or another since 1673. In 2002, the present School of Engineering was formed from the previously existing departments of Chemical Engineering, Civil Engineering, Electronics and Electrical Engineering, and Mechanical Engineering. Since then, the School has grown from strength to strength, growing rapidly in the last 5 years, now having the largest number of students among the seven Schools in the College of Science and Engineering, offering undergraduate degree programmes across all disciplines and taught Masters courses in niche areas. All of the undergraduate degree programmes are accredited by the relevant professional institutions. The School of Engineering addresses diverse complex challenges across the entire field of engineering, at the micro- and macro/global scales. At the microscopic scale, its research supports the design and deployment of novel nanotechnology and devices for biological monitoring and medical diagnosis; at the macro/global scale, it optimises the security and sustainability of the built environment and develops engineering solutions to climate change issues. In REF 2021 the joint submission from the University of Edinburgh and Heriot-Watt University to General Engineering was ranked 1st in Scotland and 3rd in the UK for quality and breadth of research. The ranking cited is based upon the breadth and quality of research which apply standard formula (as used by the Times Higher Education) to the REF 2021 results. The School has a strong track record in producing more than 50 technology spin-outs and developing industry links that enable our graduates to build career-long relationships. Current research income in the School was c.£22.0M in 2020/21. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. The School of Engineering holds a bronze Athena SWAN award, in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education & research. Further information, including details of undergraduate & postgraduate taught programmes: www.eng.ed.ac.uk "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14899": {
      "title": "Technology Transfer PhD Intern",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Enterprise Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Edinburgh Innovations Ltd. are seeking two Technology Transfer PhD Interns to join our Innovation Development & Licensing team. Technology Transfer PhD Intern Grade 5: £24,479 Fixed Term: 12-month internship Part Time: 7 hours per week (generally expected to be on the same day each week however some flexibility can be accommodated) Number of posts: 2 The Opportunity: Edinburgh Innovations is recruiting 2 interns to work for the Technology Transfer Team to support translational activities from the School of Chemistry and School of Physics and Astronomy; the School Biological Sciences and the Roslin Institute and the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies; and to the College of Arts, Humanities and Social Sciences. The Technology Transfer Intern will assist EI’s Technology Transfer team with: the identification and assessment of intellectual property (IP); due diligence and patentability assessment; market analysis and the generation of marketing materials to attract new industrial partners; development and implementation of commercialisation strategies. Your skills and attributes for success: Strong technical background in one of the following disciplines: life/medical sciences, physical sciences, engineering or business disciplines, creative industries, ideally, with hands-on experience in one of the following areas: synthetic biology, chemical biology, biotechnology, diagnostics, drug discovery, medical imaging, therapeutics, regenerative medicine, stem cell biology, digital communications, software, renewable energy, microelectronic devices, big data, sensors, new materials, process engineering, chemical engineering, catalysts, petrochemicals, animal and human health/wellbeing/nutrition; art or business copyright licensing. Strong interest in translational science, intellectual property and technology transfer; Strong interpersonal skills with an ability to communicate confidently and professionally with academic staff and industry partners. Highly fluent written and spoken English, advanced writing skills, competence in Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook; Creative and independent thinker with a demonstrable track-record of taking on new challenges; and Detail orientated, with strong organisational and time management skills Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab). As a valued member of our team you can expect: An exciting, dynamic, supportive, and rewarding place to work. An opportunity to gain hands-on experience of the University of Edinburgh technology transfer and intellectual property management process. An opportunity for you to gain direct experience of several transferable skills, including: technology and innovation evaluation, patentability assessment and due diligence, marketing and market research as well as well as experience in the decision-making process regarding IP protection and commercialisation strategy. Support to nurture your talent and interest in intellectual property and commercialisation. Applicants must have the existing and ongoing right to work in the U.K. without restrictions as sponsorship for visas is not provided. Applicants will require approval from their PhD Supervisor and should therefore ensure this is forthcoming prior to application. Job advert closes on the 30th of October at 11.55pm. Interviews will be held between 16th and 20th of November. If invited for interview you will be required to provide evidence of your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) . "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14746": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Institute of Genetics and Cancer Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Western General, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a self-motivated, enthusiastic research assistant to join the laboratory of Dr Duncan Sproul at the University of Edinburgh’s Institute of Genetics and Cancer and support research into the role of DNA methylation in gene regulation. Grade UE06: £34,610 - £39,906 per annum CMVM / Institute of Genetics and Cancer Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 18 months contract The Opportunity An exciting research assistant position is available to research the role of epigenetic marks in gene regulation. We are looking for a self-motivated, enthusiastic research assistant to join the laboratory of Dr Duncan Sproul at the University of Edinburgh’s Institute of Genetics and Cancer and support research into the role of DNA methylation in gene regulation. This project is a close collaboration with 3 other groups based at the Universities of Edinburgh and Exeter. We have recently characterised mutations that prevent the DNA methyltransferase DNMT3B from being recruited to actively transcribed gene bodies (Taglini et al . 2024, EMBO Reports 25 :1130, DOI: 10.1038/s44319-024-00061-5). Our new project will use this knowledge combined with recently developed synthetic approaches as an entry point to probe the non-canonical regulation of transcription by DNA methylation within gene bodies and its functional impact on cellular phenotypes. The successful candidate will work with the Sproul and collaborating laboratories as part of a successful multidisciplinary research team. This role will have a particular emphasis on the use of synthetic vector libraries combined with cellular landing pads high throughput sequencing data of variant libraries from mammalian cells. Candidates who have a degree in a relevant subject and have experience in laboratory research in the areas of molecular biology, cellular biology or biochemistry are invited to apply. Applicants must possess the ability to work with others as part of an interdisciplinary research team to support research projects towards publication. Candidates with prior experience of research in the fields of epigenomics, gene regulation and/or the use of synthetic approaches in these fields are particularly welcome. The post is full-time (35 hours per week) and funding is available from 1 st January 2027 for 1.5 years pending review after 6 months. Further details of work from the Sproul research group are available at: https://institute-genetics-cancer.ed.ac.uk/research/research-groups-a-z/sproul-group Click to view a copy of the full Job Description. How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter - Details of 2 referees The cover letter and CV should clearly describe how you meet each of the selection criteria listed in the job description. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 rd October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We aim to hold interviews during the period 9 th November 2026. The School of Genetics and Cancer is one of the six Schools that make up the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM). The School is home to the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC) whose goals are to carry out excellent genetic, genomic, cellular and clinical science, so that we contribute significantly to the understanding of human development, physiology and disease, and apply this knowledge for the benefit of patients and society. The IGC is one of the College’s five world-leading research institutes. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. The College’s undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. To find out more visit: School of Genetics and Cancer webpage ( https://medicine-vet-medicine.ed.ac.uk/school-of-genetics-and-cancer ) (opens a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14845": {
      "title": "Cleaner",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/11/2026 Department: Cleaning/Servitorial/Central Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Estates and Facilities JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We are looking for Cleaners to join our team. Grade UE02: £24.547 per annum, pro-rata Estates Department / Support Services Part-time: 15 hours per week Open-ended (Permanent) Vacancies available: 5 The Opportunity: We are looking for Cleaners to join our team. There is an excellent working environment along with good terms and conditions of service. Applications are invited for the post of cleaner at the University’s Central Campus Edinburgh, EH1 1LT Your skills and attributes for success: Cleaning experience preferred although training will be provided and the ability to work to a high standard as part of a team are required. Please click Here for a copy of the full job description. How to apply Please include CV in your application. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 18 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. The University of Edinburgh was founded in 1583 and currently occupies more than 550 academic, administrative and residential buildings throughout the city of Edinburgh across 5 campuses; Central Area, Kings Buildings, Easter Bush, Bio- Quarter and the Western General Hospital. There are also sporting facilities at Peffermill. The estate is an exciting mix of eras and architecture. Whilst many of the buildings enjoy listed status and are integral to the unique qualities and characteristics of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Edinburgh, many more have been added over the years as the University has grown. The portfolio ranges from nationally important, iconic, historical buildings through to state of the art teaching, research and sporting facilities. The University of Edinburgh has a significant programme of estates investment underway, in both capital development and also in the infrastructure and resilience of the estate. This investment will help to ensure that the university remains at the forefront of world leading teaching, learning and research. The Estates Department is part of the Corporate Services Group and provides a range of key activities that help the University to achieve its corporate objectives. It is an integrated centre of excellence responsible for implementing decisions related to the buildings, fabric and infrastructure of the University. It provides specialist advice to the University on strategic estates issues as well as its statutory obligations. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14877": {
      "title": "BR-UK Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Centre for Population Health Sciences Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Behavioural Research UK Leadership Hub (BR-UK) Administrator will provide high level administrative, communications and general secretarial support to the BR-UK team. This will involve undertaking a range of duties including administration, financial processing, communication functions and meeting arrangements. Grade UE05: £29,588 to £33,951 per annum, pro rata School of Population Health Sciences / Centre for Population Health Sciences Part Time: 21 hours per week Fixed term: from November 2026 until 31st October 2028 Location: Usher Building, Bioquarter, Edinburgh The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. Professional Services The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Behavioural Research UK Leadership Hub (BR-UK) Administrator will provide high level administrative, communications and general secretarial support to the BR-UK team. This will involve undertaking a range of duties including administration, financial processing, communication functions and meeting arrangements. Informal enquiries may be directed to Sancha Martin, Sancha.martin@ed.ac.uk (Consortium Manager). A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Interesting work Good salary/pay rates Excellent benefits and support View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: • A competitive salary. • An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. • To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. • Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. We draw together researchers, clinicians and practitioners from public health, primary care, biomedical and social sciences - with roots going back to the first Chair of Public Health in the UK, established in 1898. For more information, visit the Population Health Sciences website ( https://usher.ed.ac.uk/research/population-health-sciences ) (opens in a new browser tab). School of Population Health Sciences Transforming health in society by working with people, populations and data. The School of Population Health Sciences sits within the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at The University of Edinburgh. The School of Population Health Sciences is a key applied and translational arm of the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh; offering significant expertise in health services research, health informatics, data science and social science. We are also the Health and Social Care Data-Driven Innovation Hub, part of the Edinburgh and South East Scotland City Region Deal. We strive for and work towards the data-enabled transformation of health. About the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine The College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) is home to six Schools which in turn host five world-leading research institutes; the Institute of Genetics and Cancer, the Usher Institute, the Institute for Regeneration and Repair, the Roslin Institute and the new Institute for Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. Our undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14760": {
      "title": "Reader in Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Informatics Category: Academic JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We are seeking current and emerging leading researchers in the field. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or be close to completing one), a strong research agenda, and the enthusiasm and ability to undertake original research, teach courses, and supervise student projects. UE09: £63,606 - £78,469 per annum College of Science and Engineering / School of Informatics Full-Time: 35 hours per week Open-Ended (permanent) The Opportunity: We are seeking current and emerging leading researchers in the field. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or be close to completing one), a strong research agenda, and the enthusiasm and ability to undertake original research, teach courses, and supervise student projects. We seek candidates with research interests in the development of cutting-edge Artificial Intelligence methods and models, particularly in the joint space of perception (e.g., computer vision) and action (e.g., interactive decision-making systems). We are interested in candidates motivated to establish and expand the principles underlying such methods and models in the context of high-impact application domains (e.g., robotics, health, environment, society, etc.). The candidate's research focus may align with the following, non-exhaustive, list of topics (in no particular order): Continual, open-ended, self-improving AI Agentic AI, world models, and generative models Embodied perception and action Learning from interaction and reinforcement Decision-making under uncertainty Long-duration autonomy Candidates should have a strong research track record. They should also demonstrate interest and ability to teach undergraduate (UG) and master's (MSc) level courses, and to supervise projects for UG, MSc, and PhD in areas related to their research. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Applicants must provide: A cover letter. A recent CV. A research statement (2-3 pages, excluding references) A teaching statement (1-2 pages, excluding references) The research statement should describe research interests and vision, prior work, and future plans. The teaching statement should specify the candidate's teaching philosophy and experience, the speciality and introductory courses they would be qualified to teach, and the types of topics/projects they would propose for undergraduate and masters-level student projects. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Informatics is the study of how natural and artificial systems store, process and communicate information. Research in Informatics promises to take information technology to a new level, and to place information at the heart of 21st century science, technology and society. The School enjoys collaborations across many disciplines in the University, spanning all three College, and also participates as a strategic partner in the Alan Turing Institute and is home to a number of Centres for Doctoral Training. The School provides a fertile environment for a wide range of studies focused on understanding computation in both artificial and natural systems. It attracts students around the world to study in our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Informatics is one of seven schools in the College of Science and Engineering, at the University of Edinburgh. It is recognised for the employability of its graduates, its contributions to entrepreneurship, and the excellence of its research. Since the first Research Assessment Exercise in 1986, Informatics at Edinburgh has consistently been assessed to have more internationally excellent and world-class research than any other submission in Computer Science and Informatics. The latest REF 2021 results have again confirmed that ours is the largest concentration of internationally excellent research in the UK. This contributes to our ranking of consistently being in the top 30 world-wide. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14761": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Informatics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We are seeking current and emerging leading researchers in the field. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or be close to completing one), a strong research agenda, and the enthusiasm and ability to undertake original research, teach courses, and supervise student projects. We seek candidates with research interests in the development of cutting-edge Artificial Intelligence methods and models, particularly in the joint space of perception (e.g., computer vision) and action (e.g., interactive decision-making systems). We are interested in candidates motivated to establish and expand the principles underlying such methods and models in the context of high-impact application domains (e.g., robotics, health, environment, society, etc.). Grade UE08: £50,253 - £61,759 per annum School of Informatics / College of Science and Engineering Full-Time: 35 hours per week Open-Ended: permanent The Opportunity: We are seeking current and emerging leading researchers in the field. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or be close to completing one), a strong research agenda, and the enthusiasm and ability to undertake original research, teach courses, and supervise student projects. We look for candidates with research interests in the development of cutting-edge Artificial Intelligence methods and models, particularly in the joint space of perception (e.g., computer vision) and action (e.g., interactive decision-making systems). We are interested in candidates motivated to establish and expand the principles underlying such methods and models in the context of high-impact application domains (e.g., robotics, health, environment, society, etc.). The candidate's research focus may align with the following, non-exhaustive, list of topics (in no particular order): Continual, open-ended, self-improving AI Agentic AI, world models, and generative models Embodied perception and action Learning from interaction and reinforcement Decision-making under uncertainty Long-duration autonomy Applicants should have a strong research track record. They should also demonstrate interest and ability to teach undergraduate (UG) and master's (MSc) level courses, and to supervise projects for UG, MSc, and PhD in areas related to their research. The School of Informatics The School of Informatics was ranked #1 in the UK for research power in Computer Science and Informatics Times Higher Education ranking, based on the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF). We are the highest-ranked UK institution and one of the top two in Europe for AI, according to the CSRankings website (csrankings.org). The School of Informatics currently hosts multiple doctoral training programmes related to the field of AI (including Machine Learning Systems, AI for Dependable and Deployable Robotics, Responsible NLP, and Biomedical AI Innovation), through which a successful candidate may recruit funded PhD students, in addition to scholarships available through the School. The successful candidate will join a dynamic environment with both junior and senior faculty members and dedicated professional service staff. The professional services available in the School include excellent support for national and international research funding applications, as well as support for commercial engagement and entrepreneurial initiatives. The University of Edinburgh The University of Edinburgh is a research-intensive university. The University is an active Unit in the European Laboratory for Learning and Intelligent Systems (ELLIS); these units are recognised centres of excellence in AI research across Europe. In addition, it is one of the five founding partners of the UK's Alan Turing Institute for Data Science and AI. There are also many opportunities within the University to engage in collaborative activities with researchers in other areas of computer science and mathematics, and across the physical, psychological, social, and biomedical sciences. This includes becoming a fellow of the University-wide Generative AI Lab ( gail.ed.ac.uk ). The University has also been recently announced as the home of the UK’s next national supercomputer . The School of Informatics holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. The City Edinburgh is an attractive city to live in; it is walkable, enjoys proximity to nature, and features a world-class cultural scene that includes attractions such as the Edinburgh International Festival or the Edinburgh Fringe . Edinburgh was ranked 1 st in the 2022 Time-Out Index of the best cities in the world, which considered everything from scenic beauty, community spirit, public transport, walk-ability, restaurants, through to cleanliness. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Applicants must provide: A cover letter. A recent CV. A research statement (2-3 pages, excluding references) A teaching statement (1-2 pages, excluding references) The research statement should describe research interests and vision, prior work, and future plans. The teaching statement should specify the candidate's teaching philosophy and experience, the speciality and introductory courses they would be qualified to teach, and the types of topics/projects they would propose for undergraduate and masters-level student projects. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Informatics is the study of how natural and artificial systems store, process and communicate information. Research in Informatics promises to take information technology to a new level, and to place information at the heart of 21st century science, technology and society. The School enjoys collaborations across many disciplines in the University, spanning all three College, and also participates as a strategic partner in the Alan Turing Institute and is home to a number of Centres for Doctoral Training. The School provides a fertile environment for a wide range of studies focused on understanding computation in both artificial and natural systems. It attracts students around the world to study in our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Informatics is one of seven schools in the College of Science and Engineering, at the University of Edinburgh. It is recognised for the employability of its graduates, its contributions to entrepreneurship, and the excellence of its research. Since the first Research Assessment Exercise in 1986, Informatics at Edinburgh has consistently been assessed to have more internationally excellent and world-class research than any other submission in Computer Science and Informatics. The latest REF 2021 results have again confirmed that ours is the largest concentration of internationally excellent research in the UK. This contributes to our ranking of consistently being in the top 30 world-wide. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14885": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer/Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Veterinary Clinical JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies is seeking a Senior Lecturer/Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia based within the Hospital for Small Animals on Easter Bush Campus, just outside Edinburgh. As part of a team covering all aspects of small animal anaesthesia, you will assist in the provision of a referral level anaesthesia at the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies within normal University hours; contribute to the on call rota, and contribute to the teaching efforts of the Service. Fixed Term 9 months (Maternity Cover), 35 hours per week, plus on call (additional payment). Grade UE08: £50,253 - £61,759 per annum OR Grade UE09: £63.606 - £78,469 per annum (according to experience) plus Market Force Supplement and additional on-call payment Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies / Hospital for Small Animals Fixed-Term: 9 months Maternity Cover Full-Time: 35 hours per week Appointment to Grade 8 or Grade 9 is based on fulfilment of University of Edinburgh job grading criteria. Your next step starts here. Be part of something bigger . We are looking for a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. The Opportunity: The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies is seeking a Senior Lecturer/Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia based within the Hospital for Small Animals on Easter Bush Campus, just outside Edinburgh. As part of a team covering all aspects of small animal anaesthesia, you will assist in the provision of a referral level anaesthesia at the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies within normal University hours; contribute to the on-call rota, and contribute to the teaching efforts of the Service. You will assist in the development of the Anaesthesia Service. Working in a multidisciplinary environment where collaboration is actively encouraged, you will be supported by teams of specialists in internal medicine, oncology, diagnostic imaging, soft tissue surgery, orthopaedics, neurology, emergency and critical care among others. This post is full-time (35 hours per week) for a fixed term of 9 months. The salary for this post starts at £50,253 (UE08) or £63,606 (UE09) per annum according to experience plus Market Force Supplement (reviewed annually, currently 8%). The majority of your time will be spent on the clinic processing specialist referral cases. This includes clinical teaching of final year undergraduate students. Click here to view the copy of the full Senior Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia job description (opens in a new browser tab) Click here to view the copy of the full Lecturer in Veterinary Anaesthesia job description (opens in a new browser tab) Your skills and attributes for success: A degree in veterinary medicine and, at the time of appointment, be registered with the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons Boarded (UE09) or board eligible (UE08) for ECVAA or ACVAA Extensive experience within a multi-disciplinary referral and teaching centre Extensive experience in referral anaesthesia Appropriate English language certificate Informal enquires should be directed to Prof Gudrun Schoeffmann, email Gudrun.Schoeffmann@ed.ac.uk This role is conditional on a successful Level 2 Disclosure Scotland check. How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 20 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14160": {
      "title": "The Ella McGregor Senior Lecturer / Lecturer in Equine Sport Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/12/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Veterinary Clinical JobFunction: Lectureships JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an experienced equine clinician with a demonstrable focus on sports medicine (particularly lameness diagnostics and advanced imaging) to join our clinical team and take a leading role in the provision and development of the sports medicine service provided by our equine hospital and practice. Open-ended, full time - 35 hours per week. Grade UE08: £50,253 to £61,759 per annum OR Grade UE09: £63.606 to £78,469 per annum (Both grades will include additional Market Pay Supplement which is reviewed annually) College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine / Royal (Dick School of Veterinary Studies / Division of Equine Sciences Full Time: 35 hours per week (Clinicians will contribute to the on-call rota depending on area of clinical expertise) Open Ended: permanent Appointment to Grade 8 or Grade 9 is based on fulfilment of University of Edinburgh job grading criteria. The Opportunity: From local roots to global impact. Be part of something bigger. Join the University of Edinburgh and you’ll be making a difference to everything around you. Be part of something bigger — where you’ll do meaningful work, grow and progress, be rewarded and recognised, and benefit from our strong commitment to your wellbeing. There are so many reasons to join us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our student’s performance and wellbeing. We are looking for an experienced equine clinician with a demonstrable focus on sports medicine (particularly lameness diagnostics and advanced imaging) to join our clinical team and take a leading role in the provision and development of the sports medicine service provided by our equine hospital and practice. This is a unique opportunity to join or lead (depending on experience) the team growing the discipline of equine sports medicine within a top-ranking UK veterinary school. In conjunction with our talented and dedicated equine team, the post holder will work within a highly-equipped equine hospital where they will contribute to the continued development of our clinical caseload and take responsibility for cases at referral level. Beyond the standard medical and surgical caseload, the hospital has well-developed cardiology, respiratory, orthopaedic, diagnostic imaging and behavioural medicine services, with significant clinical and research expertise in each area. We are now in a position to recruit a dedicated person capable of driving an integrated “performance driven sports medicine” service. We have the necessary breadth of clinical expertise, research reputational standing and advanced imaging modalities to fully support growth in this service. Current/recent complimentary research activities within the division include fracture risk assessment (multimodal imaging and biomarkers), osteoarthritis biomarker discovery, T2 cartilage mapping, advanced cardiac imaging and cardiac mapping (arrhythmias) and multi-omics assessment of mild equine asthma and training-associated immune dysregulation. The post-holder will also have the opportunity to work in conjunction with our behavioural medicine team to develop a better understanding of the relationship between pain and behavioural changes with a view to collectively developing a more comprehensive and formulated approach to the investigation and management of such cases. They will be involved in teaching and practical training of veterinary undergraduate students and postgraduate clinical scholars (residents) and will be encouraged to undertake clinical research in their field of expertise, thus enhancing the professional reputation of the equine service and contributing to the research output of the division and the school. Collaborative research is encouraged and there is extensive opportunity for cross-campus collaboration. In addition, the post holder will assist with initiatives aimed at the promotion of equine welfare and will have the opportunity to contribute towards the efforts of the Division of General Practice in supporting the equine industry in remote and rural parts of Scotland. There will also be the option to undertake racecourse veterinary duties at 2 of Scotland’s racecourses as part of our existing team. Your skills and attributes for success: A Veterinary Degree registered with the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons as a practising member of the profession. General equine sports medicine skills, with a particular focus on/interest in orthopaedics and diagnostic imaging. Further specialist qualification, preferably ECVSMR/ACVSMR; however, ECVS/ACVS Diploma-holders with a demonstrable sports medicine interest are very much encouraged to apply. Residency trained board eligible candidates in the appropriate speciality are encouraged to contact us in the first instance – please see contact details below. Interest in fulfilling the remit of a clinical academic working within a veterinary school setting (i.e., engagement with teaching, learning and assessment) Demonstrable commitment to develop their own, and contribute to the School’s clinical research output. Desirable (not essential) : Higher degree (Masters /PhD) in a relevant discipline For further information or informal discussion please contact Scott Pirie on scott.pirie@ed.ac.uk Depending on the personal circumstances of the successful candidate, this role may be eligible for the University’s standard relocation allowance. Further details can be found here: https://www.ed.ac.uk/human-resources/job/relocation ’. The post holder will join a dynamic team who are committed to delivering gold standard patient and client care whilst also training the next generation of primary care veterinarians. The academic environment provides many opportunities for CPD and career progression. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Working within one of the world’s leading universities Professional development and subject matter expertise Variety every day Excellent benefits and support Click to view a copy of the full Senior Lecturer in Equine Sport Medicine job description Click to view a copy of the full Lecturer in Equine Sport Medicine job description How to apply Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 17 December 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14916": {
      "title": "Patient Public Involvement Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Centre for Cancer Research Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Western General, Midlothian, United Kingdom How can we meaningfully integrate the lived experiences of cancer patients and the public into our scientific research to ensure our discoveries are relevant, ethical, and focused on what matters most to patients? We are looking for a specialist in the field of Patient Public Involvement (PPI) in research to develop, influence, lead and deliver local and national PPI strategy for the Cancer Research UK Scotland Centre. The focus of the role is to embed the use of PPI in fundamental and translational cancer research to improve the quality and impact of our research, and to provide specialist expertise and knowledge and support to enhance the development of research and funding applications. You would work collaboratively, providing expertise and support across the Cancer Research UK Scotland centre and its partners. We are looking for someone who ideally has a minimum of 4 years’ experience within Patient and Public Involvement in Research. Grade UE08: £50,253 to £61,759 per annum, pro rata if part time School of Genetics and Cancer / CMVM Full time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: for 24 months with potential for extension The Opportunity: How can we meaningfully integrate the lived experiences of cancer patients and the public into our scientific research to ensure our discoveries are relevant, ethical, and focused on what matters most to patients? We are looking for a specialist in the field of Patient Public Involvement (PPI) in research to develop, influence, lead and deliver local and national PPI strategy for the Cancer Research UK Scotland Centre. The focus of the role is to embed the use of PPI in fundamental and translational cancer research to improve the quality and impact of our research, and to provide specialist expertise and knowledge and support to enhance the development of research and funding applications. You would work collaboratively, providing expertise and support across the Cancer Research UK Scotland centre and its partners. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. This role is conditional on a successful PVG and Disclosure Scotland check. View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held on Tuesday 1 December 2026, at the Institute of Genetics and Cancer, The University of Edinburgh, Western General Hospital, Crewe Road South, Edinburgh EH4 2XU The School of Genetics and Cancer is one of the six Schools that make up the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM). The School is home to the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC) whose goals are to carry out excellent genetic, genomic, cellular and clinical science, so that we contribute significantly to the understanding of human development, physiology and disease, and apply this knowledge for the benefit of patients and society. The IGC is one of the College’s five world-leading research institutes. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. The College’s undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. To find out more visit: School of Genetics and Cancer webpage ( https://medicine-vet-medicine.ed.ac.uk/school-of-genetics-and-cancer ) (opens a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14879": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom The appointed PDRA will work in the group of Prof. Marialuisa Aliotta (PI) to conduct research in nuclear astrophysics for the UKRI-funded ERC Advanced Grant “NUCLEAR: NUclear Clustering Effects in Astrophysical Reactions”. Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum, pro rata if part time School of Physics and Astronomy / Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics /College of Science & Engineering Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: initially for 12 months, with possibility for extension The Opportunity: The appointed PDRA will work in the group of Prof. Marialuisa Aliotta (PI) to conduct research in nuclear astrophysics for the UKRI-funded ERC Advanced Grant “NUCLEAR: NUclear Clustering Effects in Astrophysical Reactions” The appointed PDRA will develop and apply R-matrix evaluations of experimental data on nuclear reactions cross sections of astrophysical interest, to support the research within the group and contirbute to a vibrant and exciting research area. Working closely with the PI and collaborator DR James deBoer (Notre Dame University, US), the PDRA will contribute to, and utilize, a set of tools designed for these purposes. The PDRA will maintain close ties with the PI's research team, providing R-matrix global fits of experimental data and contributing to the evaluation of astrophysical reason rates. The ideal candidate will have a strong background in experimental nuclear astrophysics, especially in underground laboratories, as well as an excellent academic track record of strong publications and leadership in the field. In addition, s/he will be expected to co-supervise research students and to contribute to project provision and supervision of graduate and undergraduate students. The successful candidate is expected to engage in frequent field trips to overseas laboratories, present their work at collaboration meetings as required, and maintain a good collaborative attitude. The Edinburgh Nuclear Physics Group has an outstanding reputation for scientific leadership and innovative instrumentation in diverse areas of nuclear physics including nuclear astrophysics and properties of exotic nuclei. The Group’s research is performed at world-leading laboratories, using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the Group. The School of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Edinburgh is committed to encouraging equality and diversity among its workforce and eliminating discrimination. The School strives to create a more diverse, equitable and inclusive workplace for all, and we are looking to actively diversify our staff. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and encourage applications from people of colour, women, people with disabilities, LGBTQ+ people and other minority and under-represented groups. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working, as evidenced by our Juno Champion and Athena SWAN Silver awards. The level of appointment for the PDRA post will be determined by the selection panel depending on the experience of the successful candidate. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Responsibility and autonomy. Excellent benefits and support. Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Your application should include a motivation letter detailing skills and experience relevant for the post, together with a full CV and an up-to-date list of peer-reviewed publications. The names of two referees should also be provided. For informal enquiries contact Prof. Marialuisa Aliotta ( m.aliotta@ed.ac.uk ) or Dr James deBoer ( Richard.J.deBoer.12@nd.edu ) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We anticipate that interviews will be held on Zoom in late November. Further details will be communicated in good time to shortlisted candidates. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14741": {
      "title": "Cleaner",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Department: Cleaning/Servitorial/Non-Central Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Estates and Facilities PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom Applications are invited for the posts of part time cleaners at the University’s Kings Building EH9 3FD, Bioquarter EH16 4UU, Easter Bush EH25 9RG and Western General EH4 2XU campus. Grade UE02: £24,547 per annum pro rata CSG / Estates Department Part Time: 15 hours per week (6am to 9am Monday to Friday) Open Ended: permanent Various vacancies The Opportunity: There is an excellent working environment along with good terms and conditions of service. Cleaning experience preferred although training will be provided and the ability to work to a high standard as part of a team are required. Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab) Application Information Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: CV As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The University of Edinburgh was founded in 1583 and currently occupies more than 550 academic, administrative and residential buildings throughout the city of Edinburgh across 5 campuses; Central Area, Kings Buildings, Easter Bush, Bio- Quarter and the Western General Hospital. There are also sporting facilities at Peffermill. The estate is an exciting mix of eras and architecture. Whilst many of the buildings enjoy listed status and are integral to the unique qualities and characteristics of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Edinburgh, many more have been added over the years as the University has grown. The portfolio ranges from nationally important, iconic, historical buildings through to state of the art teaching, research and sporting facilities. The University of Edinburgh has a significant programme of estates investment underway, in both capital development and also in the infrastructure and resilience of the estate. This investment will help to ensure that the university remains at the forefront of world leading teaching, learning and research. The Estates Department is part of the Corporate Services Group and provides a range of key activities that help the University to achieve its corporate objectives. It is an integrated centre of excellence responsible for implementing decisions related to the buildings, fabric and infrastructure of the University. It provides specialist advice to the University on strategic estates issues as well as its statutory obligations. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14716": {
      "title": "R(D)SVS Student Ambassador",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Royal Dick Veterinary Studies Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Student Services JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies is currently has an opening for 9 Student Ambassadors. Student Ambassadors are an essential part of The University’s recruitment and pre-arrival activity. UE02: £24,547 per annum pro rata CMVM / Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies Guaranteed hours Vacancies: 9 The Opportunity: Student Ambassadors are an essential part of The University’s recruitment and pre-arrival activity. They will have opportunities to represent the University, their School and the student body to internal and external audiences. The main purpose of the student ambassadors is to promote their own student experience through a variety of activities, including but not limited to vlogging/blogging, campus tours and participation in open days, in order to share the authentic student experience and engage with prospective students. Please note: you must be a current matriculated student on one of the veterinary medicine programmes to be eligible to apply for this position. Your skills and attributes for success: Planning and Organising Problem Solving Communication Click to view a copy of the full Student Ambassador job description (opens new browser tab) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community Flexible working hours of your choice How to apply Along with your application you will must include: An up-to-date CV Answers to the supplemental questions. The questions can be found here . Championing equality, diversity and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) Key dates to note: The closing date for applications is 18th October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15033": {
      "title": "Senior Climate Data Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: GeoSciences Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Academic Support JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a Senior Climate Data Analyst to work in the Scottish Climate Intelligence Service (SCIS) Programme at the Edinburgh Climate Change Institute. UE07: £41,064.00 - £48,822.00 Per Annum. CSE / School of Geosciences / ECCI. Fixed Term Contract – Until 31 March 2027. Full Time – 35 Hours Per Week. The Opportunity: We are looking for a Senior Climate Data Analyst to work in the Scottish Climate Intelligence Service (SCIS) Programme at the Edinburgh Climate Change Institute. Interested in being part of a project that supports delivery of climate action in Scotland? Join the Climate Partnerships team at the Edinburgh Climate Change Institute to work on the SCIS programme where you will provide technical expertise and support to Scottish Local Authorities, and to local and national partners, to scale up and deliver climate action. The role includes providing support to shape and track progress on delivery of local and national plans using an online platform (ClimateView); identifying and sharing best practice and helping to coordinate delivery across climate delivery partners; analysing Scotland-wide datasets to provide new insights for local and national government policy; and leading workstreams and helping to develop data services which improve delivery of climate action. We'd also be interested in hearing from people with skills or experience in using data to capture economic and social impact, to help us to further embed the wider benefits of climate action into the SCIS programme. You’ll be joining the Climate Partnerships team within ECCI; we are a team of 12+ working on various programmes to support delivery of climate action by private and public stakeholders across Scotland. SCIS is a partnership between ECCI and the Improvement Service. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We’re a hybrid working team, with an expectation of working at least two days a week in the office in Edinburgh, and with the option to work in the office more frequently. Your skills and attributes for success: Strong data analysis and presentation skills Knowledge of public sector climate policy and/or delivery Adaptable in applying diverse problem-solving strategies Proven written and verbal communication skills Confident working independently View the full Job Description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply To apply, please submit your CV and a cover letter. The closing date for applications is 20 th October As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community Comprehensive Staff Benefits, such as a generous holiday entitlement, competitive pension schemes, staff discounts, and family-friendly initiatives. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the total value of your pay and benefits Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We expect interviews to be held virtually within the first two weeks of November. The School of GeoSciences explores the factors and forces that shape our world. The School aims to understand the world through fundamental curiosity-driven research and to support prescient decision-making at individual to global scales. We undertake world-leading research; offer new ways of understanding natural and social drivers of change; provide inter-and trans-disciplinary solutions; and work in partnership to improve livelihoods and explore ways to manage the environment that are both sustainable and socially equitable. With over 500 academics, researchers and research students, we are the largest and most successful interdisciplinary grouping of geoscientists and geographers in the UK. Research activity is coordinated within three main Research Institutes – Global Change, Earth and Planetary Science, and Geography and the Lived Environment – and within smaller research groupings that reach across and beyond the School. A distinctive feature of the School is the combination of academic strength, intellectual breadth and societal relevance. Our interdisciplinary research and teaching builds on established core disciplines (ecology, environmental sciences, geography, geophysics, meteorology, oceanography) to provide a variety of approaches to understanding the world (including, for example, system-scale modelling, process studies and the development of urban and social theory). The School’s research covers fundamental ‘blue-skies’ questions, as well as having application to key societal challenges including inequality and vulnerability; urban precarity; nature and cultural meaning; development and sustainability; climate and environmental change; energy, food and water security; health and wellbeing; natural resources; and natural hazards. The School of GeoSciences aims to recognise and value diversity in our staff and students, and to support flexible and family-friendly working. More details about the School is available from http://www.ed.ac.uk/geosciences "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15081": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in disease transmission genetics and modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Department: The Roslin Institute Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We would like to recruit a driven and enthusiastic colleague to join the group as Postdoctoral Research Fellow, funded by a prestigious research grant on the genetic and immune determinants and evolutionary constraints underlying individual variation in host infectiousness. In this project we harness data from the fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster as a model system of infection, immunity, and behaviour to disentangle the genetic and immune determinants, and evolutionary constraints underlying pathogen transmission. Full Time - 35 Hours Per Week, or part-time 80%. Fixed term: 24 months full time, or potentially longer if part-time until contract end of 31.5.2029. UE07: £41,064.00 - £48,822.00 Per Annum. CMVM / The Roslin Institute / Quantitative Biology. Full Time - 35 Hours Per Week, or part-time 80%. Fixed term: 24 months full time, or potentially longer if part-time until contract end of 31.5.2029 The Opportunity: We would like to recruit a driven and enthusiastic colleague to join the group as Postdoctoral Research Fellow, funded by a prestigious research grant on the genetic and immune determinants and evolutionary constraints underlying individual variation in host infectiousness. In this project we harness data from the fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster as a model system of infection, immunity, and behaviour to disentangle the genetic and immune determinants, and evolutionary constraints underlying pathogen transmission. The project requires a set of advanced analytical and computational modelling skills to apply genetic-epidemiological inference and prediction models to the project data. By applying these skills you will generate important new scientific insights on the diverse drivers of disease transmission for more effective disease control. Your skills and attributes for success: We welcome applications from all over the world that meet the following qualifications: PhD, or near completion of a PhD (within 3 months), in a discipline with a substantial quantitative component (e.g. quantitative genetics, statistics, bioinformatics, theoretical biology or physics, applied mathematics). Proven research track record in quantitative genetics and genomics, generalised linear mixed models, Bayesian statistics/ MCMC and mathematical modelling of disease or other dynamic biological processes*. Experience in high performance computing, in particular writing efficient computational code for genetic and statistical data analyses and simulation studies. Excellent verbal and written communication skills evidenced through a track record of research outputs, including publications in peer reviewed scientific journals and presentations at scientific conferences. Excellent organisation and time management skills and ability to work independently and in a team. Knowledge of Drosophila or other model species genetics is desirable. *We do not expect a single candidate to fulfil all these criteria, and trained and support will be provided to acquire new skills. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) The successful candidate will be based at the Roslin Institute of the University of Edinburgh, Scotland, UK under the primary supervision of Prof. Andrea Doeschl-Wilson https://www.ed.ac.uk/profile/andrea-wilson ). There will also be extensive interaction with Dr. Pedro Vale’s group (https://biology.ed.ac.uk/pedrovale) based in the School of Biological Sciences at the University of Edinburgh and Dr. Chris Pooley at Biomathematics & Statistics Scotland ( https://www.bioss.ac.uk/people/cpooley ). We are open to considering flexible working patterns (on a contractual basis) but expect that the majority of the work will include regular on-campus working. As a valued member of The Roslin Institute and the wider University of Edinburgh, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community of researchers. To contribute to world-class research with real world impact. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. How to apply For informal enquiries please contact Professor Andrea Doeschl-Wilson ( andrea.wilson@roslin.ed.ac.uk ). Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter - Full contact details of 2 referees Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 13 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Roslin Institute receives strategic investment from the Biotechnology & Biological Sciences Research Council (BBSRC) and provides national capability to achieve sustainable agriculture, control diseases and enhance health through pioneering animal bioscience. The Institute is a key part of the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework exercise, a joint submission by the School and Scotland’s Rural College placed first in the United Kingdom for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences. The Institute is home to over 400 staff, students and visitors, including around 65 Group Leaders and Tenure Track Fellows on a path to independence. It has a vibrant programme ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin/work-study/postgraduate ) (opens in a new browser tab) of postgraduate research and a thriving student community. Research at the Institute is organised across eight divisions (Quantitative Biology, Genome Biology, Functional Genetics, Translational Bioscience, Bacteriology, Virology, Immunology, and Epidemiology) and two Institute Strategic Programmes funded by the BBSRC from 2023-28 (Genes & Traits for Healthy Animals and Prevention & Control of Infectious Diseases). Over £380M has been invested in physical infrastructure at Easter Bush since 2008, including for research on key terrestrial and aquatic farmed animals. This includes the National Avian Research Facility, a £24M Large Animal Research & Imaging Facility, Aquaculture facilities for fish and shellfish, and an Innovation Centre that is now fully occupied with commercial tenants. The campus serves as a key innovation hub for the Agri-Tech, Aquaculture and Animal Bioscience (3A) sector and Roslin has extensive partnerships with businesses, including animal breeding and pharmaceutical businesses able to translate its discoveries at global scale. The Institute has a positive and inclusive culture and holds a prestigious Athena SWAN Gold award in recognition of its commitment to advance the careers of women in science. Further details of the research conducted at the Institute can be found at The Roslin Institute website ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin ) (opens in a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15089": {
      "title": "Examinations Invigilator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Academic Planning (Registry Services) Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom Registry Services are looking for Examinations Invigilators to support the University’s in-person examination service. The three main examination diets fall in December (2 weeks) April/May (4 weeks) and August (2 weeks) each year. UE03: £24,729.00 to £25,804.00 per annum pro-rata. USG / Academic Planning (Registry Services). Guaranteed Hours – minimum 20 hours per year. Open Ended (permanent). We are looking for Examinations Invigilators to support the University’s in-person examination service. The Opportunity: Your next step starts here. Be part of something bigger. The University offers roles in just about every area you can think of. If you are looking for your next step, we can offer you employment opportunities where you can flourish. In Registry Services our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation. ​ With around 1,200 exams and 100,000 student sittings across three main examination diets, December (2 weeks) April/May (4 weeks) and August (2 weeks), the role of invigilator is crucial as part of ensuring the successful and secure delivery of the examination diet. Supporting the designated Senior Invigilator, you will be able to demonstrate: Experience of working within a team environment Able to communicate effectively and professionally with other invigilators and students Good customer service experience is required, to assist in dealing with students and staff Experience of working within guidelines and following procedures Demonstrate good organisational and administrative skills Click to view a copy of the full job description As a valued member of our team, you can expect A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community Comprehensive Staff Benefits, such as a generous holiday entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the total value of your pay and benefits Championing equality, diversity and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note Unless stated otherwise the closing date for applications is 30 October 2026 at 11:59pm. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We anticipate interviews may be held during w/c 2 nd November. As a world-leading research-intensive University, we are here to address tomorrow’s greatest challenges. Between now and 2030 we will do that with a values-led approach to teaching, research and innovation, and through the strength of our relationships, both locally and globally. Academic Services is a team of professional services staff responsible for: the University’s academic governance (Senate and its committees); educational policy and regulations which underpin and support the student experience and enhance learning and teaching; quality assurance and enhancement; and case-work (student conduct, student appeals, and complaints.) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15093": {
      "title": "Clinical Cleaning Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Technical JobFunction: Animal Care JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are seeking a dedicated and reliable Clinical Cleaner to join our hospital team. This post plays a vital role in maintaining a safe, clean, and hygienic clinical environment. 15 hours/week working Thursday and Friday each week, open-ended contract. UE02: £24,547.00 Per Annum, Pro Rata. CMVM / The Royal Dick School of Veterinary Studies. 15 hours per week working Thursday and Friday each week. Open ended contract – Permanent. Shifts time 1.30pm-10pm with one hour lunch (shift times TBC with successful candidate) Be part of something bigger, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. The Opportunity: We are seeking a dedicated and reliable Clinical Cleaner to join our hospital team. This post plays a vital role in maintaining a safe, clean, and hygienic clinical environment. Working within a busy referral hospital setting, the successful candidate will be responsible for delivering high quality cleaning across clinical and non-clinical areas, ensuring standards are consistently met in line with infection control policies. This is an excellent opportunity for someone who is passionate about animal welfare, enjoys working in a busy team environment, and is looking to develop experience within a leading veterinary referral hospital. Key Responsibilities Carry out routine tidying and deep cleaning of clinical areas including wards, treatment rooms, isolation areas, and shared spaces Follow infection prevention and control procedures to minimise the risk of cross‑contamination and hospital‑acquired infections Clean and maintain equipment and high‑touch surfaces to a high standard Respond flexibly to urgent cleaning requirements as clinical activity fluctuates Safely use cleaning chemicals and equipment in accordance with COSHH guidance Work collaboratively with clinical and non‑clinical staff to support safe hospital operations Maintain accurate cleaning records where required Training & Support We offer a focused training programme to help you get started in the role. You’ll normally start shadowing our friendly and experienced team during standard daytime hours (Monday–Friday, 8:30am–5:00pm and 6am-2.30pm) to build your confidence and skills. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14101": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/11/2026 Department: Institute of Genetics and Cancer Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Western General, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking to employ a post-doctoral researcher to join the group of Professor Martin Taylor at the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC), working to understand the mechanisms that drive mutagenesis. In this post you will be a key member of the international Cancer Grand Challenges CAUSE team mechanistically relating DNA damage to the mutations it generates. This is pioneering research in a highly collaborative international team embedded within a well supported, collegiate and enthusiastic research group. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum pro-rata if part-time Institute of Genetics and Cancer Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: for 24 months in the first instance The Opportunity: Cancer Grand Challenges – from DNA damage to the mechanisms of mutagenesis We are looking to employ a post-doctoral researcher to join the group of Professor Martin Taylor at the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC), working to understand the mechanisms that drive mutagenesis. In this post you will be a key member of the international Cancer Grand Challenges CAUSE team mechanistically relating DNA damage to the mutations it generates. This is pioneering research in a highly collaborative international team embedded within a well-supported, collegiate and enthusiastic research group. This specific role is will drive the development and application of technologies to enrich for and map DNA damage and modifications within the human genome. You will explore how that damage is processed leading to repair, mutagenesis or damage tolerance. Within the IGC you will be co-supervised by biochemist Dr Tom Deegan. The successful candidate will have experience in molecular biology and nucleic acid manipulation along with core biochemistry techniques including protein purification. You will have ownership of your own projects and work closely with experts in the analysis of high-throughput sequencing and mass-spectrometry data, familiarity with such platforms would be an advantage. The post holder will have many opportunities for multidisciplinary collaborative working both within the IGC and with CAUSE partners based at the Sanger Institute, Hubrecht Institute, Harvard University, University of San Diego and University of Minnesota. The wider Cancer Grand Challenge community will also provide excellent opportunities for training, professional and network development. Applicants must have a PhD in molecular biology, biochemistry or a related discipline for appointment at Research Associate level. Applicants close to completion of their PhDs will also be considered where experience is directly relevant. Please see the job description and person specification document associated with this advert for the full list of criteria. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. Initially a 24-month contract is offered, with the possibility of extension. View full Job Description How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV – either narrative or structured. - Cover letter explaining your fit for the role, the science that excites you how you think this role could help realise your aspirations. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and aim to make appointments only on the criteria of merit and evidence. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 18 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. The School of Genetics and Cancer is one of the six Schools that make up the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM). The School is home to the Institute of Genetics and Cancer (IGC) whose goals are to carry out excellent genetic, genomic, cellular and clinical science, so that we contribute significantly to the understanding of human development, physiology and disease, and apply this knowledge for the benefit of patients and society. The IGC is one of the College’s five world-leading research institutes. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. The College’s undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. To find out more visit: School of Genetics and Cancer webpage ( https://medicine-vet-medicine.ed.ac.uk/school-of-genetics-and-cancer ) (opens a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14938": {
      "title": "Research Grants Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Edinburgh Research Office Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Research Grants Team are looking for a Research Grants Administrator to join our expert team in delivering financial management and post award administration. Grade UE06: £34,610 to £39,906 per annum Corporate Services Group / Edinburgh Research Office Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) The Opportunity The Research Grants Team are looking for Research Grants Administrators to join our expert team in delivering financial management and post award administration. You will work collaboratively across the Research Grants team and with colleagues across a range of departments to ensure effective stewardship of research finances and provide specialist knowledge on all aspects of research grant management and reporting. We are seeking colleagues who will embody our values of expertise, community, integrity and collaboration. Colleagues in Edinburgh Research Office work in partnership with researchers to develop and deliver world-leading research. We work across the research lifecycle – growing ideas into proposals, projects and impact. We are enablers and connectors, networked across the University, able to provide the best advice to researchers and research leaders in all disciplines. We are part of a wider ecosystem of over 300 colleagues providing research support across the University of Edinburgh – linking through our Schools, Colleges and Service Groups. The Research Grants Team work closely with the University of Edinburgh research and professional service communities, partners and funders, to maximise the research academic colleagues can undertake for the funding they receive. Our expert team delivers financial management and post award administration of the University’s 3,500 research grants. The University offers hybrid working; in Edinburgh Research Office we expect colleagues to be on campus for a minimum of team anchor days, team meetings, all-ERO meetings and other in-person meetings and events required in your role. The Research Operations Directorate is responsible for delivery of a high quality, integrated, user-focused and resilient service which covers applications, contracting and awards. We provide the research operations services that underpin the University Research and Innovation Strategy and associated growth plans. The Research Grants Team is responsible for ensuring the effective recovery of research income from funders and partners. Our work is predominately deadline driven, and fast paced. Working in the Research Grants Team, you can expect to support the maximisation of research income through ensuring compliance with funder terms and conditions and completing of financial reporting. You will work directly with University of Edinburgh research and professional service communities, as well as external stakeholders. A Research Grants Administrator will: manage the delivery of their portfolio of grants in a fast paced, deadline driven environment; provide a high quality of service through compliance with funder terms and conditions and University policy; work with colleagues to find solutions, manage issues and risks, enabling academic colleagues to maximise their research funding; help us continually improve our processes and procedures and train others in new ways of working; The successful candidate will: be passionate about using finance as an enabler to support colleagues in their goals; have strong problem-solving skills and a can-do attitude; be highly self-motivated and thrive in constantly changing environments; excellent organisational and time management skills, with attention to detail; Development Opportunities Edinburgh Research Office is a supportive and encouraging environment for those new to research professional services and those wishing to further develop their University professional service careers. We have a longstanding commitment to training and staff development and a great track record of internal promotions for ambitious colleagues. We actively encourage all colleagues in ERO to think about their development in role. We encourage applicants to think not just about their suitability now, but how they might grow in the role in the future. Click here for a copy of the full job description How to Apply Please apply with a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the knowledge, skills and experience required, and your motivation for applying for this post. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 29 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Working in Edinburgh Research Office you will be part of a professional, supportive and friendly team ; at the heart of University research support, working in collaboration with academics and research support professionals across the institution. The breadth of our remit means you will get an overview of research support activity while you build your skills in your specialism. Gaining new knowledge and skills to help you develop in this role and progress in your career is a priority. We offer colleagues a stimulating working environment based on our values of integrity, expertise, community, and collaboration. Our work is fast‐paced and intense; you will thrive here if you are motivated, customer‐orientated, and passionate about providing an excellent service to enable our researchers to do amazing work . "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14981": {
      "title": "Junior Sous Chef",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: ACE Business Development Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an enthusiastic and motivated Junior Sous Chef to join our kitchen team within the University of Edinburgh’s Hospitality and Events Collection. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced Chef de Partie or current Junior Sous Chef ready to take the next step in their career. Working within a busy hotel and restaurant environment, you will support the Head Chef and Sous Chef with the day-to-day running of the kitchen while developing your leadership and management skills. Our operation covers breakfast, lunch and dinner service alongside functions, events and hotel guests, so we are looking for someone who is organised, reliable and comfortable working in a fast-paced environment. Grade UE04: £26,093 to £28,778 per annum plus 15% Premium band for flexible working School/Department: Accommodation, Catering and Events - Hotels Full Time: 35 hours per week, 5 over 7 days including weekends Open Ended: Permanent The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, this is a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. We are looking for an enthusiastic and motivated Junior Sous Chef to join our kitchen team within the University of Edinburgh’s Hospitality and Events Collection. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced Chef de Partie or current Junior Sous Chef ready to take the next step in their career. Working within a busy hotel and restaurant environment, you will support the Head Chef and Sous Chef with the day-to-day running of the kitchen while developing your leadership and management skills. Our operation covers breakfast, lunch and dinner service alongside functions, events and hotel guests, so we are looking for someone who is organised, reliable and comfortable working in a fast-paced environment. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise. Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience. Working within one of the world’s leading universities. Good salary / pay rates. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 29 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held within 2 weeks after closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15049": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards formal developments and precision lattice QCD calculations, including (semi-)leptonic decays with QED corrections, with both heavy and light quark flavours. The position is fixed-term for two years, with a possible extension contingent on additional funding. Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 2 years, October 1, 2027 – September 30, 2029 The Opportunity: We are looking for a Po stdoctoral Research Associate in the Higgs Centre for Theoretical Physics to work on numerical calculations and formal developments in the context of lattice field theory applied to theoretical particle physics. This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards formal developments and precision lattice QCD calculations, including (semi-)leptonic decays with QED corrections, with both heavy and light quark flavours. The position is fixed-term for two years, with a possible extension contingent on additional funding. The calculations are to be performed, in part, using resources from the national supercomputing facility DiRAC. Your skills and attributes for success: PhD (awarded or near completion) in Physics , including Theoretical or Mathematical Physics Strong knowledge of the lattice formulation of quantum field theories and the Standard Model of particle physics Experience in C++ programming in UNIX environments Experience in delivering research results in an academic context; e.g. a record of peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations Capability to work independently, exercising a high degree of initiative and demonstrating a proactive and flexible approach to work Please view full Job Description How to apply Applications should be submitted by 11:59pm UK time on 27 November 2026. Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (fewer than three pages) and provide the names and contact details of at least three referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Direct enquiries about the position to Antonin Portelli ( antonin.portelli@ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and Stonewall's Proud Employer programme, supporting LGBTQ+ inclusion. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab ) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15065": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Department: Chemistry Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position to work under the supervision of Dr Emma King-Smith on a project funded by The Royal Society. Grade UE07: £41,064-48,822 per annum School of Chemistry, College of Science & Engineering Full Time: 35 hours per week, however we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. Fixed term contract: 24 months The Opportunity: Postdoctoral Research Associate (King-Smith Group) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position to work under the supervision of Dr Emma King-Smith on a project funded by The Royal Society. The successful candidate will work on syntheses to advance the next generation of AI-guided organic chemistry. You can find out more about the King-Smith Group at our website (https://www.kingsmithgroup.com/). Applicants should have: A PhD in chemistry, computer science, or related discipline (or have submitted a PhD thesis prior to taking up the appointment). Possess a deep interest in chemical synthesis and the application of machine learning to synthesis. A proven track record of successfully completing research projects. Excellent written, oral and interpersonal communication skills are essential. Click to view a copy of the full Job Description (opens new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - Up to date CV with details of two referees - Cover letter (max 1 page) - Research summary (max 2 pages) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 12 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. The School of Chemistry at the University of Edinburgh has a long and distinguished history. The first Chemistry Professor was appointed to the Chair in 1713 and the Edinburgh University student-led Chemical Society is regarded as the oldest chemical society in the world. The School is run as a single academic unit with approximately 160 staff (made up of academic, postdoctoral researchers and professional services staff) and 800 students (undergraduate, taught postgraduate and research students). Our vision is for all students and staff to harness their curiosity, utilising outstanding and diverse talents to pursue knowledge that can be used to tackle global challenges and improve people’s lives. S ustainability, collaboration and inclusion are at the heart of all our school activities. School of Chemistry researchers deliver advances in both core and interdisciplinary chemistry across five overlapping themes: Catalysis & Synthesis, the Chemistry-Biology Interface, Functional Materials, Structural Chemistry & Chemical Dynamics, and Energy, Environment & Sustainable Chemistry. Our research underpins solutions to major global challenges including health, climate change, and sustainability, and we collaborate extensively with other disciplines and industry partners to take our research towards use. Our 55 group leaders work with a similar number of postdoctoral researchers and approx.155 research students to deliver £32M of current research grants across government, charity and industry funders, as well as a wide variety of public engagement activities. Along with the University of St Andrews we formed EaStCHEM in 2004 to combine the research activities of two of Scotland’s leading schools of chemistry. Combined, we are the largest chemistry unit research in the UK. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework (REF 2021), we were one of only three chemistry submissions to achieve a 100% “world-leading” score for our research environment. We hold a Silver Athena SWAN award, in recognition of excellence in science, engineering and technology employment in higher education relating to supporting women in Science. We are particularly proud of establishing the Christina Miller Fellowships, which have enabled seven talented postdoctoral researchers from under-represented groups to establish themselves as independent group leaders. For further information about our school is available on the School of Chemistry Website ( https://chem.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/13762": {
      "title": "Epidemiologist/Data Scientist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Centre for Cardiovascular Science Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom Applications are invited for an Epidemiologist/ Health Data Scientist, to work with Dr Peter Gallacher and Prof. Neeraj Dhaun (Bean) (Cardiovascular and Renal Healthcare Data Research Group) on an ambitious research programme utilising high-fidelity, routinely-collected NHS data to improve cardiovascular risk prediction and management in patients with kidney disease. Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum CMVM / School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences / Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: for 2 years The Opportunity: With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you are looking for entry-level, mid-career or roles at the top of your chosen specialism, we can offer you a career where you can flourish and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation. ​ The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. Join the University of Edinburgh and you’ll be making a difference to everything around you. Be part of something bigger — where you’ll do meaningful work, grow and progress, be rewarded and recognised, and benefit from our strong commitment to your wellbeing. There are so many reasons to join us. Applications are invited for an Epidemiologist/ Health Data Scientist, to work with Dr Peter Gallacher and Prof. Neeraj Dhaun ( Bean ) (Cardiovascular and Renal Healthcare Data Research Group) on an ambitious research programme utilising high-fidelity, routinely-collected NHS data to improve cardiovascular risk prediction and management in patients with kidney disease. The postholder will work at the interface between cardio-renal epidemiology, data science, medical statistics, and clinical medicine. They will contribute to original, high-impact quantitative research by accessing, curating and interrogating large-scale and complex regional and national datasets, comprising linked administrative, biochemistry, diabetes, hospitalisation, mortality, prescribing, renal and transplantation data. The postholder will work with clinical researchers to transform and curate linked datasets, design novel and exciting data-linkage studies, and perform advanced data analytics and statistical modelling. They will be embedded within our growing research team, comprising clinical research nurses and clinician researchers from a variety of public health, primary and secondary care backgrounds. The postholder will also provide guidance to other members of the research group, including undergraduate or postgraduate students. As our research team grows, they will help to recruit and lead a team of epidemiologists and data scientists. Our research team is based within the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research – a BHF-funded Centre of Research Excellence in Edinburgh – and works closely and collaboratively with a variety of research teams under the auspices of the Centre’s ‘ Heart Diseases ’ and ‘ Data-Driven Innovation’ Research Themes ( https://cardiovascular-science.ed.ac.uk/research/cvs-bhf-centres/bhf-centre-of-research-excellence ). This role would suit an ambitious individual who is either looking to develop their skills and career in high-impact health data research, or a more experienced epidemiologist/data scientist looking to expand their role within a dynamic and specialist team. You will have: An undergraduate qualification or equivalent in a mathematics, statistics, computing science, scientific or health-related field; Experience of clinical epidemiology or health data science; Experience of data management (e.g. data cleaning and manipulation); Experience of statistical modelling; Proficiency in programming (e.g. statistical programming in R ); A strong level of attention-to-detail that is well-suited to handling and analysing large-scale, complex linked datasets. You will be based in the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research, Queen’s Medical Research Institute, University of Edinburgh, 47 Little France Crescent, Edinburgh EH16 4TJ. The position is funded through an award from the British Heart Foundation and is available immediately for 2 years in the first instance. View the full job description How to apply Potential applicants who would like to discuss the post are welcome to contact: Dr Peter Gallacher at: pgallach@ed.ac.uk or Prof. Neeraj Dhaun (Bean) at: bean.dhaun@ed.ac.uk Please include the following documents in your application: - Cover letter - Curriculum vitae As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from minority ethnic candidates, as we acknowledge they are currently underrepresented in our team. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 28 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK, the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browser’s local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences We host research and postgraduate training in neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research. We are one of six schools in the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh and are home to the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research. Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Our institute fosters collaborative neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research to tackle some of the world's most significant health challenges. Our research explores health and disease across the body and life course – from tiny changes in cells to the lived experience of patients. Together, we work to advance the understanding, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of major health conditions including heart disease, stroke, metabolic dysfunction, mental illness, dementia and neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders. Empowered by genetics, advanced imaging, artificial intelligence and clinical trials, our research seamlessly connects discovery science with clinical care. Our work is internationally acclaimed, achieving 4* and world-leading classifications for the majority of our research, health and wealth impacts and research papers in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 assessment, the UK’s system for assessing the quality of research in UK higher education institutions. As part of driving progress in our core research areas, we host internationally leading research centres and state-of-the-art technologies: Anne Rowling Regenerative Neurology Clinic British Heart Foundation (BHF) Centre of Research Excellence Centre for Pesticide Suicide Prevention | CPSP Euan MacDonald Centre for Motor Neurone Disease Research Edinburgh Clinical Research Facility Edinburgh Imaging Jennifer Brown Research Laboratory Medical Research Council/British Hearth Foundation Centre of Research Excellence in Advanced Cardiac Therapies MS Society Edinburgh Centre for MS Research | MS Society Muir Maxwell Epilepsy Centre Patrick Wild Centre Row Fogo Centre for Research into Ageing and the Brain Simons Initiative for the Developing Brain (SIDB) UK Dementia Research Institute at Edinburgh UKRI Hub for Metabolic Psychiatry Our institute community Our researchers drive cutting-edge science, enabling our students and working hand in hand with our expert technical and professional services colleagues. We are committed to creating a collaborative, inclusive and welcoming community, where everyone feels supported, empowered and inspired to achieve their best. Training and education We offer a wide range of postgraduate training opportunities. Students learn from world-class researchers, access outstanding facilities and get support to develop. Our graduates go on to successful careers in academia, research, healthcare and industry worldwide. Our colleagues are also leading contributors to undergraduate programmes. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15038": {
      "title": "Innovation Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Department: Laboratory for Foundations of Computer Science Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom As part of a strategic investment supporting the UK’s National Quantum Strategy, the University of Edinburgh is recruiting up to three Innovation Fellows to develop high-impact applications of quantum computing across a range of scientific domains. This is a rare opportunity to help shape a new field: to work at the frontier of quantum computing while delivering results that matter to science, industry, and society. The Innovation Fellows will be recruited as a cohort and pursue collaborative research within the Quantum Advantage Turbo Charger (QATCH) programme. Over the course of their fellowships they will work alongside research teams at the Quantum Software Lab, domain experts from across the College of Science and Engineering, the National Quantum Computing Centre, and QATCH’s industry partners to deliver applications of quantum computing in one or more of the following target sectors: Grade UE08: £50,253 - £61,759 per annum College of Science and Engineering / School of Informatics Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 24 months The Opportunity: As part of a strategic investment supporting the UK’s National Quantum Strategy, the University of Edinburgh is recruiting up to three Innovation Fellows to develop high-impact applications of quantum computing across a range of scientific domains. This is a rare opportunity to help shape a new field: to work at the frontier of quantum computing while delivering results that matter to science, industry, and society. The Innovation Fellows will be recruited as a cohort and pursue collaborative research within the Quantum Advantage Turbo Charger (QATCH) programme. Over the course of their fellowships they will work alongside research teams at the Quantum Software Lab, domain experts from across the College of Science and Engineering, the National Quantum Computing Centre, and QATCH’s industry partners to deliver applications of quantum computing in one or more of the following target sectors: S1) Healthcare and Life Sciences. S2) Materials and Manufacturing . S3) Finance. S4) Energy . S5) AI and Data Science . S6) Cybersecurity. These fellowships are designed for ambitious early-career researchers who want to turn frontier quantum research into real-world impact. We welcome applicants approaching quantum computing from either direction. If your background is in quantum algorithms, information science, or hardware, this is your opportunity to apply that expertise to concrete, high-value scientific problems and to build end-to-end software pipelines that run on cutting-edge hardware. If you are an expert in one of our application domains (life sciences, materials, energy, finance, AI, or cybersecurity) this is your opportunity to unlock long-standing problems in your field with quantum computing, working side by side with leading algorithm and hardware specialists to translate your challenges into quantum solutions. Applicants should have a demonstrable research track record in quantum computing and/or a relevant application domain and will ideally have at least two years of postdoctoral (or equivalent industry) experience. Above all, we are looking for researchers who combine scientific rigour with pragmatic execution, and who are energised by turning ideas into working solutions. We're building a research environment that combines the intellectual freedom of Bell Labs with the urgency and impact of a focused start-up. We offer the opportunity to work on hard, long-term problems that matter—with the autonomy to follow promising directions. We value researchers who can balance scientific rigour with pragmatic execution: people who want to ship real solutions to real problems, energised by turning research into impact. QSL provides a tight-knit team of exceptional researchers, the freedom to publish your work, and the rare opportunity to see your ideas go from concept to demonstration, all within a highly stimulating intellectual environment. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab ) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: CV Cover letter, mentioning the target sector(s) you feel most keen to contribute to . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 11 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Informal enquiries can be directed to Professor Julien Michel, QATCH Deputy Director of Impact, julien.michel@ed.ac.uk The Laboratory for Foundations of Computer Science (LFCS) is one of seven research institutes in the School of Informatics at the University of Edinburgh. It was founded in 1987 and is a community of theoretical computer scientists with interests in concurrency, semantics, categories, algebra, types, logic, algorithms, complexity, databases and modelling. The mainspring of research in LFCS is the study of theories which underlie, or should in future underlie, the analysis and design of computing systems. Our work has a core of theoretical research and a practical component which explores application and implementation of the theory. Several research groups exist within LFCS. Find out more at http://wcms.inf.ed.ac.uk/lfcs "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15047": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Research Associate will contribute to the European Research Council Advanced Grant project “Classical and Semiclassical Aspects of Amplitudes”. Research activities include studying the interplay of large gauge transformations, amplitudes and observables; computing (semi)classical observables both in scattering and bound domains, and investigating amplitudes in time-dependent spacetimes. The ERC grant is held by Donal O’Connell but there will be ample opportunity to work with others including Tim Adamo, Anton Ilderton, Mao Zeng and t Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hour per week Fixed Term: 3 years, September 1, 2027 – August 31, 2030 The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. Applications are invited for two Postdoctoral Research Associate at the Higgs Centre for Theoretical Physics, to work on applications of scattering amplitudes and the double copy to gauge theory and gravity. The Research Associate will contribute to the European Research Council Advanced Grant project “Classical and Semiclassical Aspects of Amplitudes”. Research activities include studying the interplay of large gauge transformations, amplitudes and observables; computing (semi)classical observables both in scattering and bound domains, and investigating amplitudes in time-dependent spacetimes. The ERC grant is held by Donal O’Connell but there will be ample opportunity to work with others including Tim Adamo, Anton Ilderton, Mao Zeng and the wider group. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). View the full Job Description How to apply Applications should be submitted before the deadline of 11:59pm UK time on 27 November 2026. Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (strictly less than 3 pages including references) and provide the names and contact details of at least two referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Please feel free to address any enquiries regarding the position to Tim Adamo ( t.adamo@ed.ac.uk ), Anton Ilderton ( anton.ilderton@ed.ac.uk ), Donal O’Connell ( donal@ed.ac.uk ) or Mao Zeng ( mao.zeng@ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from people of colour, women, people with disabilities, LGBTQ+ people and other minority and under-represented groups. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held after the closing date. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15050": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards scattering amplitudes in gauge theory, Feynman integrals, kinematic expansions, infrared divergences, and Regge limits, working with Einan Gardi, Franz Herzog, Andrew McLeod, Mao Zeng and the wider group. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum College of Science & Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 3 years, October 1, 2027 – September 30, 2030 The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards scattering amplitudes in gauge theory, Feynman integrals, kinematic expansions, infrared divergences, and Regge limits, working with Einan Gardi, Franz Herzog, Andrew McLeod, Mao Zeng and the wider group. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). Please view full Job Description How to apply Applications should be submitted before the deadline of 11:59pm UK time on 27 November 2026. Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (strictly less than 3 pages including references) and provide the names and contact details of at least two referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Please address any enquiries regarding the position to Einan Gardi ( Einan.Gardi@ed.ac.uk ), Franz Herzog ( fherzog@exseed.ed.ac.uk ), Andrew McLeod ( andrew.mcleod@ed.ac.uk ) or Mao Zeng ( mao.zeng@ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from people of colour, women, people with disabilities, LGBTQ+ people and other minority and under-represented groups. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held after the closing date. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15068": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Centre for Inflammation Research Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an inventive and enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to work within the lab of Professor Will Wood at the University of Edinburgh. This represents a unique opportunity to be part of a multi-disciplinary team undertaking research in inflammation and innate immunity, utilising a powerful live imaging approach to understanding how efferocytosis regulates the fate and function of macrophages during health and disease. The post-holder will undertake research using Drosophila, but will interface with mammalian-focused PDRAs within the wider Cell Death group to enable rapid identification of the key, evolutionarily conserved mechanisms of efferocytosis and its consequences. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum School/Department: School of Regeneration and Repair / Centre for Inflammation Research Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 12-month contract The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. Make a meaningful impact Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. We are looking for an inventive and enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to work within the lab of Professor Will Wood at the University of Edinburgh. This represents a unique opportunity to be part of a multi-disciplinary team undertaking research in inflammation and innate immunity, utilising a powerful live imaging approach to understanding how efferocytosis regulates the fate and function of macrophages during health and disease. The post-holder will undertake research using Drosophila, but will interface with mammalian-focused PDRAs within the wider Cell Death group to enable rapid identification of the key, evolutionarily conserved mechanisms of efferocytosis and its consequences. Your skills and attributes for success: Experience with Drosophila or C.elegans Experience with confocal live imaging of efferocytosis in vivo using either of the models above Self-motivated, professional, highly organized, and goal-oriented. Excellent ability to work within a bigger team and exceptional communication skills. Knowledge/interest in mammalian models or a motivation to learn. Please address informal enquiries to Professor Will Wood ( w.wood@ed.ac.uk ). A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Opportunities for publication This post is full-time (35 hours per week) View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply As part of this application, you are required to upload a cover letter/statement of not more than one page detailing how you meet each of the essential criteria as outlined on the job description and a CV . These items should be uploaded as one single combined document in either PDF or Word format. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 28 October 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We anticipate that interviews will be held within 2-3 weeks of the advert closing date. The Centre for Inflammation Research (CIR) is a world-leading centre dedicated to understanding the mechanisms of inflammation and translating these insights into new therapies for inflammatory diseases. Part of The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR), CIR brings together discovery science, clinical expertise, and cross-disciplinary innovation to improve human health across the lifespan. Our mission is to transform the understanding and treatment of inflammation through cutting-edge research, inclusive collaboration, and impactful translation. By uniting discovery scientists, clinicians, chemists, engineers, and industry partners, we aim to develop innovative tools, therapies, and strategies that address pressing global health challenges. Our research is structured across three interconnected themes - Infection and Immunity, Damage and Repair, and Experimental Medicine - providing a powerful framework for scientific collaboration and translational excellence. Together, these themes investigate how inflammation starts, progresses, and resolves, and how it contributes to both health and disease. Through advanced experimental models, patient-focused research, and state-of-the-art technologies, we accelerate discovery and ensure our work leads to meaningful, real-world impact. Centre for Inflammation Research website ( https://inflammation-research.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) The Institute for Regeneration and Repair The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR) is cross-College with staff and students from the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) School of Regeneration and Repair (SRR) and the College of Science and Engineering (CSE), School of Biological Sciences and School of Chemistry. The School of Regeneration and Repair is one of six schools in CMVM. At IRR our scientists and clinicians study tissue regeneration and repair to advance human health and reproductive outcomes. The Institute incorporates three leading research centres with a focus on regenerative medicine, inflammation and reproductive health. Our Director is Professor Stuart Forbes. IRR has a 5-year income of over £200M and is a collaborative hub for commercial, clinical and academic endeavour. Based at Edinburgh BioQuarter we bring together world leading research expertise to drive understanding and deliver clinical and translational outcomes. IRR has a work environment built on trust and encourages the very best talent from all backgrounds and at all levels to join us. A diverse team strengthens our ability to innovate and support cutting-edge world leading research. Supporting and empowering our team at all levels is central to our approach. Our team is ambitious, acts with integrity and demonstrates commitment to progress and impact. We aim to achieve excellence in all that we do, always being principled, considerate and respectful. Institute for Regeneration and Repair website ( https://regeneration-repair.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14970": {
      "title": "Senior Research Finance Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Edinburgh Research Office Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We have an exciting opportunity for 2 x Senior Research Finance Analysts who will part of a project team focussed on resolving legacy grant reviews. You will support the Research Accountant by preparing detailed financial award reconciliations to support financial closure and any associated remedial actions on the ledger. Grade UE06: £34,610 - £39,906 per annum Corporate Services Group / Edinburgh Research Office Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 18 months Vacancies available: 2 Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms . The Opportunity: We are seeking colleagues who will embody our values of expertise, community, integrity and collaboration. The Research Grants Team work closely with the University of Edinburgh research and professional service communities, partners and funders, to maximise the research academic colleagues can undertake for the funding they receive. Our expert team delivers financial management and post award administration of the University’s 3,500 research grants. We have an exciting opportunity for 2 x Senior Research Finance Analysts who will part of a project team focussed on resolving legacy grant reviews. You will support the Research Accountant by preparing detailed financial award reconciliations to support financial closure and any associated remedial actions on the ledger. Ideal candidates will demonstrate a strong ability to tackle complex problems head-on. You should enjoy analysing issues, identifying root causes, and implementing effective solutions with creativity and determination. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please apply with a CV and a supporting statement explaining how you meet the knowledge, skills and experience required, and your motivation for applying for this post. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 October 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14971": {
      "title": "Digital Marketing Executive",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Department: INTERFACE Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Interface are seeking a Digital Marketing Executive to join our team. Digital Marketing Executive This highly collaborative role delivers marketing activity that supports Interface’s strategic objectives by raising the profile of Interface, our innovation support services, academic-led initiatives and funding programmes. The role is focussed on implementing digital marketing strategies, managing digital assets and social platforms, and supporting the creation of engaging, evidence-based content. This post is full-time (35 hours per week), we welcome applications from candidates seeking flexible working patterns . We also support hybrid working on a non-contractual basis, offering a blend of home and on-site working to help you balance work and life effectively. This role is fixed term until 31 July 2028 . The salary for this post is Grade 6 £32,873 to £39,233 per annum. The experience and qualities we are looking for: A good standard of formal education or equivalent work experience in digital marketing, communications, or B2B marketing Experience managing CMS platforms (e.g. WordPress), social media platforms, email tools and design tools such as Canva Experience developing and implementing digital marketing campaigns across channels Familiarity with search engines, AI tools, SEO, Google Analytics and other web analysis tools Strong content creation and copywriting skills for digital and offline channels with the ability to write, design, or co-produce case studies, newsletters, videos, or infographics Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab). About Us At Edinburgh Innovations (EI), our culture is built on three core values: Collaborative , P rogressive and I nnovative . We believe the best results come from working together, sharing ideas, and supporting one another. We’re always looking forward, embracing change, challenging assumptions, and pushing boundaries to grow. Innovation is at the heart of what we do, encouraging creativity and fresh thinking at every level of the business. Interface’s vision is to help unlock and accelerate the full potential of Scotland’s innovation ecosystem by fostering strong, collaborative partnerships between academia and industry that drive sustained growth and global competitiveness. Interface has been at the heart of Scotland’s innovation ecosystem since 2005, bridging the gap between businesses—particularly SMEs—and academic expertise to drive inclusive, regionally balanced growth. We play a key role in ensuring businesses of all sizes and from all parts of Scotland can access world-class academic resources to innovate and thrive. But our work is not just about access; it’s about capacity and collaboration. That’s why we support both sides of the innovation partnership—helping businesses solve real-world challenges while equipping academic institutions to engage confidently and effectively with industry. Through this dual focus, we help drive innovation, accelerate research impact, and strengthen regional economies. Interface is a national programme and a core part of Scotland’s Innovation Infrastructure. Hosted by Edinburgh Innovations Ltd (University of Edinburgh), we benefit from a stable operational base while operating impartially across the entire academic landscape. We are well-connected and embedded in the wider innovation ecosystem—working closely with colleges, universities, enterprise agencies, funders, innovation centres, and industry partners. Collaboration is central to everything we do. To find out more about Interface please visit their website here . Championing Equality, Diversity and Inclusion We’re committed to building an inclusive workplace where everyone feels valued, respected, and empowered to thrive. At EI we celebrate diversity in all its forms and actively seek to create a team that reflects the communities around us. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds, experiences, and identities because different perspectives make us stronger. As a valued member of our team, you can look forward to: A competitive salary that reflects your skills and experience An exciting, positive, and creative workplace that’s as rewarding as it is challenging A generous 40 days of combined annual leave and public holiday entitlement A comprehensive benefits package, including a defined contribution pension scheme (currently 3.5/4.5% employee and 9/14.5% employer contributions), family-friendly policies, flexible working options, and more Ongoing support to develop your skills, grow your career, and recognise your achievements About You At EI, we look for people who live our core behaviours in everything they do You work well with others, build strong relationships, and believe that teamwork drives better outcomes. You're curious, creative, and not afraid to challenge the norm to find smarter solutions. You take responsibility for your work, follow through on commitments, and are driven to deliver results. You seek feedback, grow from experiences, and are always looking to improve. You genuinely care about the impact of your work on colleagues, customers, and the wider community. If this sounds like you, we’d love to hear from you. Application Information Please submit your CV along with a covering letter that tells us why you’re interested in the role and how your skills and experience make you a strong fit. Right to Work All applicants must have the right to work in the UK at the time of application. Unfortunately, we are not able to offer sponsorship for this role. You will be required to provide documentation as part of the recruitment process to confirm your eligibility to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 30th of October. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:55pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15011": {
      "title": "Communications Executive",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Marketing & Engagement Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Marketing and Communications JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Edinburgh Innovations Ltd are seeking a Communications Executive to join our team. We are seeking a Communications Executive to join our Communications & Marketing team to publicise Edinburgh Innovations and the commercialisation activity of the University of Edinburgh to our target audiences internally and externally. This post is full-time (35 hours per week), we welcome applications from candidates seeking part-time hours or flexible working patterns . We also support hybrid working on a non-contractual basis, offering a blend of home and on-site working to help you balance work and life effectively. This role is fixed term until 31st of July 2027. The salary for this post is Grade 5 £26,513 to £32,872 per annum. The experience and qualities we are looking for: Ability to work across the University in a highly proactive and diplomatic manner to build quick and effective collaborations. The communications executive will curate, draft and deliver engaging and accurate content across all channels. Proficient in drafting web stories, profiles and campaign content. Click to view a copy of the full job description ( opens new browser tab). About Us At Edinburgh Innovations (EI), our culture is built on three core values: Collaborative , P rogressive and I nnovative . We believe the best results come from working together, sharing ideas, and supporting one another. We’re always looking forward, embracing change, challenging assumptions, and pushing boundaries to grow. Innovation is at the heart of what we do, encouraging creativity and fresh thinking at every level of the business. If you thrive in a team-driven environment that values bold ideas and continuous improvement, you’ll fit right in. Championing Equality, Diversity and Inclusion We’re committed to building an inclusive workplace where everyone feels valued, respected, and empowered to thrive. At EI we celebrate diversity in all its forms and actively seek to create a team that reflects the communities around us. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds, experiences, and identities because different perspectives make us stronger. As a valued member of our team, you can look forward to: A competitive salary that reflects your skills and experience An exciting, positive, and creative workplace that’s as rewarding as it is challenging A generous 40 days of combined annual leave and public holiday entitlement A comprehensive benefits package, including a defined contribution pension scheme (currently 3.5/4.5% employee and 9/14.5% employer contributions), family-friendly policies, flexible working options, and more Ongoing support to develop your skills, grow your career, and recognise your achievements About You At EI, we look for people who live our core behaviours in everything they do You work well with others, build strong relationships, and believe that teamwork drives better outcomes. You're curious, creative, and not afraid to challenge the norm to find smarter solutions. You take responsibility for your work, follow through on commitments, and are driven to deliver results. You seek feedback, grow from experiences, and are always looking to improve. You genuinely care about the impact of your work on colleagues, customers, and the wider community. If this sounds like you, we’d love to hear from you. Application Information Please submit your CV along with a covering letter that tells us why you’re interested in the role and how your skills and experience make you a strong fit. Right to Work All applicants must have the right to work in the UK at the time of application. Unfortunately, we are not able to offer sponsorship for this role. You will be required to provide documentation as part of the recruitment process to confirm your eligibility to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27th of October . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:55pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15039": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards LHC phenomenology, and in particular the extraction of Standard Model parameters, the development of Machine Learning tools, and the calculation of approximate N3LO corrections to top and jet cross-sections. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work with the NNPDF collaboration. The position is initially for two years with possible extension to three depending on funding constraints. Grade UE07: £41,064- £48,822 per annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 2 years October 1, 2027 - September 30, 2029 The Opportunity: We are looking for a P ostdoctoral R esearch A ssociate in the Higgs Centre for Theoretical Physics to work on LHC physics. This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards LHC phenomenology, and in particular the extraction of Standard Model parameters, the development of Machine Learning tools, and the calculation of approximate N3LO corrections to top and jet cross-sections. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work with the NNPDF collaboration. The position is initially for two years with possible extension to three depending on funding constraints. Your skills and attributes for success: PhD (or near completion) in Physics, Theoretical Physics or Mathematical Physics. Experience of working in Collider Physics. Knowledge of standard statistical data analysis techniques. Experience in delivering research results in an academic context; e.g. a record of peer-reviewed journal and conference presentations. Capability to work independently, exercising a high degree of initiative and demonstrating a proactive and flexible approach to work Click to view a copy of the full Job Description How to Apply Applications should be submitted before 27 November 2026 . Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (less than 3 pages) and provide the names and contact details of at least three referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Please feel free to address any enquiries regarding the position to Richard Ball ( r.d.ball@ed.ac.uk ), Luigi Del Debbio ( luigi.del.debbio@ed.ac.uk ) or Franz Herzog ( fherzog@exseed.ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15041": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards formal developments and precision lattice QCD calculations, e.g. the determination of multi-hadron scattering and decay amplitudes, the extraction of long-distance matrix elements involving flavour-changing neutral currents, and the constraint of spectral quantities related to (semi-)inclusive rates, amplitudes and distribu Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hour per week Fixed Term: 2 years October 1, 2027 – September 30, 2029 The Opportunity: We are looking for a Po stdoctoral Research Associate in the Higgs Centre for Theoretical Physics to work on numerical calculations and formal developments in the context of lattice field theory applied to theoretical particle physics. This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards formal developments and precision lattice QCD calculations, e.g. the determination of multi-hadron scattering and decay amplitudes, the extraction of long-distance matrix elements involving flavour-changing neutral currents, and the constraint of spectral quantities related to (semi-)inclusive rates, amplitudes and distribution functions. The position is fixed-term for two years, with a possible extension contingent on additional funding. The calculations are to be performed, in part, using resources from the national supercomputing facility DiRAC. Your skills and attributes for success: PhD (awarded or near completion) in Physics , including Theoretical or Mathematical Physics Strong knowledge of the lattice formulation of quantum field theories and the Standard Model of particle physics Experience in C++ programming in UNIX environments Experience in delivering research results in an academic context; e.g. a record of peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations Capability to work independently, exercising a high degree of initiative and demonstrating a proactive and flexible approach to work View full Job Description (opens in new browser tab) How to apply Applications should be submitted by 27 November 2026. Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (fewer than three pages) and provide the names and contact details of at least three referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Direct enquiries about the position to Maxwell Hansen ( maxwell.hansen@ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15042": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards scattering amplitudes in gravitational physics, motivated by gravitational waves, cosmology, and formal theory, working with Tim Adamo, Anton Ilderton, Donal O’Connell, Mao Zeng and the wider group. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). Grade UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 3 years October 1, 2027 – September 30, 2030 The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. This position will contribute to the STFC Consolidated Grant UKRI4228 ‘Particle Theory at the Higgs Centre’. Research activities will be oriented towards scattering amplitudes in gravitational physics, motivated by gravitational waves, cosmology, and formal theory, working with Tim Adamo, Anton Ilderton, Donal O’Connell, Mao Zeng and the wider group. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). This post is full time 35 hours per week. We are open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. Please view the full Job Description How to apply Applications should be submitted before the deadline of 11:59pm UK time on 27 November 2026. Please upload a CV and a brief research statement (strictly less than 3 pages including references) and provide the names and contact details of at least two referees. The referees will be approached for letters after the long-listing process. Address any enquiries regarding the position to Tim Adamo ( t.adamo@ed.ac.uk ), Anton Ilderton ( anton.ilderton@ed.ac.uk ), Donal O’Connell ( donal@ed.ac.uk ) or Mao Zeng ( mao.zeng@ed.ac.uk ). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from people of colour, women, people with disabilities, LGBTQ+ people and other minority and under-represented groups. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 27 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held after the closing date. Interview dates to be confirmed. The Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics is composed of three research groups. The Particle Physics Experiments group seeks understanding of the fundamental particles of nature and the interactions governing their behaviour. In particular, we aim to explain the dominance of matter over anti-matter through the study of CP violation with the LHCb experiment: to understand the mechanisms of electroweak symmetry breaking that lead to the creation of mass, and to search for new particles at ATLAS and future colliders; to discover and characterise particle dark matter with the LUX-ZEPLIN and DarkSide-20k experiments; and to explore neutrino oscillations, and neutrinos of astrophysical origin with experiments distributed grid computing (GridPP), to store and analyse the vast quantities of data that are produced in these endeavours. The Particle Physics Theory Group is interested in fundamental physics at all energy scales, from the hadronic binding energy scale, to the scale of present and future particle colliders, up to the energy scales of the very early universe at its first fractional second of existence. We have interest and theoretical involvement in most current and upcoming particle physics experiments, high energy colliders, and in observations made by the WMAP and Planck satellites. We pursue the very latest developments in both perturbative and nonperturbative field theory, renormalization theory, and the application of quantum field theory to other branches of physics such as turbulence theory and condensed matter systems. The Nuclear Physics Group has a broad and diverse range of research interests aimed at understanding the properties and structure of nuclei, the origin of the chemical elements in the universe, and the way in which nuclear reactions power some of the most spectacular stellar explosions, such as novae, supernovaea and X-ray bursts. Our research is performed at world-leading oversea laboratories (TRIUMF, RIKEN, GSI, LNGS, CERN and others), using a variety of experimental techniques and state-of-the-art instrumentation developed by the group. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15004": {
      "title": "Fintech Sector Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: FinTech Scotland Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Development JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom FinTech Scotland are seeking a Fintech Sector Analyst to join our team. The Fintech Sector Analyst role within FinTech Scotland is a very varied role and includes a combination of sector research and analysis and hands-on engagement with the fintechs and Strategic Partners in our community. It supports all the functions within Fintech Scotland: marketing and comms, ecosystem engagement, impact reporting, events and strategic programmes. This post is full-time, but we welcome applications from candidates seeking part-time hours or flexible working patterns . We also support hybrid working on a non-contractual basis, offering a blend of home and on-site working to help you balance work and life effectively. The salary for this post is Grade 6 £32,873 to £39,233 per annum. The experience and qualities we are looking for: An understanding of the fintech sector Basic data analysis and reporting skills mainly using the Microsoft product suite Strong communication skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a small and busy team Willingness to get involved across the organisation and support where needed Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab). About Us At Edinburgh Innovations (EI), our culture is built on three core values: Collaborative , P rogressive and I nnovative . We believe the best results come from working together, sharing ideas, and supporting one another. We’re always looking forward, embracing change, challenging assumptions, and pushing boundaries to grow. Innovation is at the heart of what we do, encouraging creativity and fresh thinking at every level of the business. FinTech Scotland was founded in January 2018 as a joint initiative by University of Edinburgh, Lloyds Banking Group, HSBC and Scottish Enterprise. Market and strategy led, the organisation has grown and is now supported by a broad range of global financial services, technology and professional services firms as well as major universities and the public sector. We act as a strategic enabler and cluster management body and are focused on leveraging the potential economic and social benefits that arise from being a leading global centre of fintech research and innovation. FinTech Scotland is a hosted organisation within Edinburgh Innovations. If you thrive in a team-driven environment that values bold ideas and continuous improvement, you’ll fit right in. Championing Equality, Diversity and Inclusion We’re committed to building an inclusive workplace where everyone feels valued, respected, and empowered to thrive. At EI we celebrate diversity in all its forms and actively seek to create a team that reflects the communities around us. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds, experiences, and identities because different perspectives make us stronger. As a valued member of our team, you can look forward to: A competitive salary that reflects your skills and experience An exciting, positive, and creative workplace that’s as rewarding as it is challenging A generous 40 days of combined annual leave and public holiday entitlement A comprehensive benefits package, including a defined contribution pension scheme (currently 3.5/4.5% employee and 9/14.5% employer contributions), family-friendly policies, flexible working options, and more Ongoing support to develop your skills, grow your career, and recognise your achievements About You At EI, we look for people who live our core behaviours in everything they do You work well with others, build strong relationships, and believe that teamwork drives better outcomes. You're curious, creative, and not afraid to challenge the norm to find smarter solutions. You take responsibility for your work, follow through on commitments, and are driven to deliver results. You seek feedback, grow from experiences, and are always looking to improve. You genuinely care about the impact of your work on colleagues, customers, and the wider community. If this sounds like you, we’d love to hear from you. Application Information Please submit your CV along with a covering letter that tells us why you’re interested in the role and how your skills and experience make you a strong fit. Right to Work All applicants must have the right to work in the UK at the time of application. Unfortunately, we are not able to offer sponsorship for this role. You will be required to provide documentation as part of the recruitment process to confirm your eligibility to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is Friday 6th November Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will most likely be held on Wednesday 25 th November. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15096": {
      "title": "Registered Veterinary Nurse - Equine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Department: Veterinary Clinical Services Category: Technical JobFunction: Nursing JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom Our highly-skilled team provide nursing support for a wide and varied caseload, with advanced equipment ranging from a 3T MRI to the latest and most advanced cardiac ultrasound machines. The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary hospital team supporting the clinical, teaching and research activities within the hospital. Part-time (28 hours per week), open-ended. Grade UE06: £35,302 - £40,704 per annum Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies, MVM 28 hours per week (4 days per week, 0.8FTE) Open ended Unlocking your career. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. The Dick Vet Equine Hospital is looking for an experienced and reliable Registered Veterinary Nurse (RVN). The post-holder should have a strong work ethic, and the ability to work as part of a dedicated team across our equine Medicine, Orthopaedic and Soft Tissue Services. RVN is essential but not necessarily in equine, we require a competent horse handler, along with excellent equine nursing and interpersonal skills. The post holder will join a team of 10 friendly nurses, with responsibilities including theatre nursing for both standing and surgeries performed under general anaesthesia, support in lameness investigations, advanced diagnostic imaging and stock control. The post-holder will work independently to organise and prioritise daily/weekly workload and will contribute to teaching our undergraduate veterinary students. Out of hours working may be required. The Opportunity: Our highly-skilled team provide nursing support for a wide and varied caseload, with advanced equipment ranging from a 3T MRI to the latest and most advanced cardiac ultrasound machines. The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary hospital team supporting the clinical, teaching and research activities within the hospital. The hospital is located on the beautiful Easter Bush Campus, sitting at the foot of the iconic Pentland Hills, and only a ten-minute commute from Edinburgh city centre. Edinburgh is consistently voted one of the most beautiful cities in the world to live and visit, with amenities to suit all interests. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in a world class equine hospital within our outstanding veterinary medicine and biomedical campus. Your skills and attributes for success: Registered Veterinary Nurse Right to work in the UK Confident horse handler Prior experience in equine practice Excellent communication skills Click here for a copy of the full job description. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 13 November Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/about/what-we-do ) (opens in a new browser tab) is a one-of-a-kind centre of excellence in clinical activity, teaching and research. Our purpose-built campus, set against the Pentland Hills Regional Park, is home to over 800 staff and a thriving student communit y ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/education ) (opens in a new browser tab). The School comprises the Roslin Institute, three veterinary hospitals, a pathology unit, innovation centre and a range of research centres and services. We represent one of the largest concentrations of animal science expertise in Europe, impacting local, regional, national and international communities in terms of economic growth, the provision of clinical services and the advancement of scientific knowledge. Education The School is widely accredited, enabling our graduates to practise across the globe. We are ranked among the best vet schools in the UK and internationally, evidencing our leadership in veterinary education, research and clinical practice. The Roslin Institute collaborates closely in delivering research-led teaching to undergraduate veterinary students, and promoting clinical veterinary research and translation. The School’s position as part of the College of Medicine & Veterinary Medicine enables the translation of expertise and knowledge into human health and vice-versa. Research Impact In a joint submission to the latest UK Research Excellence Framework exercise, research at the School and Scotland’s Rural College (SRUC) ranked number one in the UK for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences by combining ratings for each institution. The research environment was rated 100 per cent world leading or internationally excellent, as was the impact of our research on society. Community The School holds Athena SWAN Silver, and the Roslin Institute Gold. The campus is home to a thriving and diverse community of staff and students, with access to facilities including allotments, cycle facilities, fitness trails, restaurants and a gym. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15104": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in quantitative disease genetics and modelling in aquaculture",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Department: The Roslin Institute Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Easter Bush Campus, Midlothian, United Kingdom We have a fully funded 3-year position for a highly motivated and skilled post-doctoral researcher in quantitative genetics and epidemiological modelling of infectious diseases in aquaculture. The successful candidate will join a multi-disciplinary international team of aquaculture, genomic and modelling specialists from academia and industry seeking to make scientific breakthroughs in reducing disease transmission and improving disease resilience in farmed fish. This is full-time fixed term role, currently available until 31/10/2029, grade UE07 £41,885 - £49,798 CMVM/The Roslin Institute/Quantitative Biology Grade UE07 – £41,885 - £49,798 Full Time - 35 Hours Per Week Fixed term: Position is available from 1.1.2027 to 31.10.2029 with potential for extension The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. This post is full time, 35 hours per week. We have a fully funded 3-year position for a highly motivated and skilled post-doctoral researcher in quantitative genetics and epidemiological modelling of infectious diseases in aquaculture. The successful candidate will join a multi-disciplinary international team of aquaculture, genomic and modelling specialists from academia and industry seeking to make scientific breakthroughs in reducing disease transmission and improving disease resilience in farmed fish. As successful candidate you will have the opportunity take a lead role in building and applying the next generation of computational tools to transform future experimental and field study designs, and genetic analyses of infectious disease data to tackle the most pressing disease challenges in aquaculture. Your skills and attributes for success: We welcome applications from all over the world that meet the following qualifications: PhD, or near completion of a PhD, in a discipline with a substantial quantitative component (e.g. quantitative genetics, statistics, bioinformatics, theoretical biology or physics, applied mathematics). Proven research track record in quantitative genetics and genomics, Bayesian statistics/ MCMC and mathematical modelling of disease or other dynamic biological processes. Experience in high performance computing, in particular writing efficient computational code for genetic and statistical data analyses and simulation studies. Evidenced proficient use of Generative Artificial Intelligence tools in research. Excellent verbal and written communication skills evidenced through a track record of research outputs, including publications in peer reviewed scientific journals and presentations at scientific conferences. Excellent organisation and time management skills and ability to work independently and in a team. Knowledge of aquaculture breeding or farming practices is desirable. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) The successful candidate will be based at the Roslin Institute of the University of Edinburgh, Scotland, UK under the primary supervision of Prof. Andrea Doeschl-Wilson https://www.ed.ac.uk/profile/andrea-wilson ). We are open to considering flexible working patterns (on a contractual basis) but expect that the majority of the work will include regular on-campus working. As a valued member of The Roslin Institute and the wider University of Edinburgh, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community of researchers. To contribute to world-class research with real world impact. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. How to apply For informal enquiries please contact Professor Andrea Doeschl-Wilson ( andrea.wilson@roslin.ed.ac.uk ). Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter - Full contact details of 2 referees Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 23 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Roslin Institute receives strategic investment from the Biotechnology & Biological Sciences Research Council (BBSRC) and provides national capability to achieve sustainable agriculture, control diseases and enhance health through pioneering animal bioscience. The Institute is a key part of the Royal (Dick) School of Veterinary Studies. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework exercise, a joint submission by the School and Scotland’s Rural College placed first in the United Kingdom for agriculture, food and veterinary sciences. The Institute is home to over 400 staff, students and visitors, including around 65 Group Leaders and Tenure Track Fellows on a path to independence. It has a vibrant programme ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin/work-study/postgraduate ) (opens in a new browser tab) of postgraduate research and a thriving student community. Research at the Institute is organised across eight divisions (Quantitative Biology, Genome Biology, Functional Genetics, Translational Bioscience, Bacteriology, Virology, Immunology, and Epidemiology) and two Institute Strategic Programmes funded by the BBSRC from 2023-28 (Genes & Traits for Healthy Animals and Prevention & Control of Infectious Diseases). Over £380M has been invested in physical infrastructure at Easter Bush since 2008, including for research on key terrestrial and aquatic farmed animals. This includes the National Avian Research Facility, a £24M Large Animal Research & Imaging Facility, Aquaculture facilities for fish and shellfish, and an Innovation Centre that is now fully occupied with commercial tenants. The campus serves as a key innovation hub for the Agri-Tech, Aquaculture and Animal Bioscience (3A) sector and Roslin has extensive partnerships with businesses, including animal breeding and pharmaceutical businesses able to translate its discoveries at global scale. The Institute has a positive and inclusive culture and holds a prestigious Athena SWAN Gold award in recognition of its commitment to advance the careers of women in science. Further details of the research conducted at the Institute can be found at The Roslin Institute website ( https://vet.ed.ac.uk/roslin ) (opens in a new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15108": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: Centre for Population Health Sciences Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom As a post-doctoral researcher or epidemiologist, you will be involved in work aimed at improving the use of real world evidence to support regulatory and health technology decision making for medicines and medical devices. You will work closely with different stakeholders within and outside of the University on standalone projects involving data analysis and as part of wider programmes of work. Grade and salary scale: UE07 £41,885 to £49,798 School of Population Health Sciences / Usher Institute / Centre for Population Health Sciences Full Time - 35 hours per week Fixed term contract available from November 2026 until 31st October 2028 Location – Usher Building, Bioquarter, Edinburgh The Opportunity: From local roots to global impact. Be part of something bigger Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. As a post-doctoral researcher or epidemiologist, you will be involved in work aimed at improving the use of real world evidence to support regulatory and health technology decision making for medicines and medical devices. You will work closely with different stakeholders within and outside of the University on standalone projects involving data analysis and as part of wider programmes of work. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: • Professional development and subject matter expertise • Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience • Responsibility and autonomy • Working within one of the world’s leading universities • Excellent benefits and support This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns providing they are compatible with the needs of the project work. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter For informal enquires please contact Professor Daniel Morales (daniel.morales@ed.ac.uk) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: • A competitive salary. • An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. • To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. • Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The Usher Institute sits within the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at The University of Edinburgh. We are also the Health and Social Care Data-Driven Innovation Hub, part of the Edinburgh and South East Scotland City Region Deal. The Usher Institute is a key applied and translational arm of the Edinburgh Medical School, within the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh; offering significant expertise in health services research, health informatics, data science and social science. We strive for and work towards the data-enabled transformation of health. For more information, visit the Usher Institute website ( https://usher.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) School of Population Health Sciences Transforming health in society by working with people, populations and data. The School of Population Health Sciences sits within the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at The University of Edinburgh. The School of Population Health Sciences is a key applied and translational arm of the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh; offering significant expertise in health services research, health informatics, data science and social science. We are also the Health and Social Care Data-Driven Innovation Hub, part of the Edinburgh and South East Scotland City Region Deal. We strive for and work towards the data-enabled transformation of health. About the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine The College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) is home to six Schools which in turn host five world-leading research institutes; the Institute of Genetics and Cancer, the Usher Institute, the Institute for Regeneration and Repair, the Roslin Institute and the new Institute for Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences. The teams in the six CMVM Schools work together to build outstanding discovery and translational research programmes designed to answer the major challenges facing humanity, animal and planetary health, now and in the future. Our undergraduate and postgraduate teaching is delivered through Edinburgh Medical School and the Royal (Dick) School of Medical Studies with contributions from all six schools. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14329": {
      "title": "Commis Chef",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: ACE Catering Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom An opportunity has arisen for a Commis chef to join the University of Edinburgh’s Conference & Events Catering Department, based at Pollock Hills in the South Hall Kitchen. UE02: £24,547 per annum, plus 15% Premium Band for flexible working CSG / Accommodation, Catering and Events Full Time: 35 hours per week, 5 over 7 days including weekends Open-Ended (permanent) 1 Vacancy Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, this is a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. The Opportunity: An opportunity has arisen for a Commis chef to join the University of Edinburgh’s Conference & Events Catering Department, based at Pollock Hills in the South Hall Kitchen. We cater for everything from small meetings and private dinners to large, high-profile conferences. We also run an extensive Outside and Delivered Hospitality service, supplying food to over 180 University buildings as well as private and corporate venues across the city. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Good salary/pay rates Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 5 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held 2 weeks from closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14965": {
      "title": "Research Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Department: Physics and Astronomy - School Management and Services Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Finance JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom This post will deliver an expert service of research grant administration both pre and post award, supporting the School’s research staff in grant application and management. Grade UE05: £29,588 to £33,951 per annum College of Science and Engineering, School of Physics and Astronomy Full Time: 35 Hours per week Open-ended: Permanent Contract The Opportunity: This post will deliver an expert service of research grant administration both pre and post award, supporting the School’s research staff in grant application and management. This includes: Advising on and providing costs for grant applications and submission. Supporting grant holders in managing awards and providing advice on funder regulations. Preparing for end of grant processes and the submission of final expenditure statements. Use your knowledge to drive change. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. Your skills and attributes for success: Well developed customer service approach. Good oral and written communication skills. High levels of accuracy and numeracy. Good planning and organisational skills. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Variety every day This post is full-time (35 hours per week). View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 12 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. The School of Physics and Astronomy is in the College of Science and Engineering and comprises the Institute for Particle and Nuclear Physics (IPNP), the Institute for Condensed Matter and Complex Systems (ICMCS) and the Institute for Astronomy (IfA). We have around 100 academic staff, over 120 research staff and around 65 professional services staff. The School of Physics and Astronomy was ranked 4th in the UK and 1st in Scotland in the Research Excellence Framework (REF) 2021 listing for the quality, scale and breadth of its research by Times Higher Education. Consistently ranked within the top 20 physics departments worldwide, these results confirm the exceptional performance of our staff, our excellent facilities, and our world-leading research. The School runs undergraduate programmes at BSc and MPhys level in Physics, Mathematical Physics, Theoretical Physics, Computational Physics, Astrophysics and (jointly with the School of Chemistry) Chemical Physics. The undergraduate programme has flexible entry and exit points, creating courses of variable duration and level. The School accepts around 230 new undergraduates into its programmes each year and has current student populations of over 800 undergraduates, 50 taught postgraduates and 220 research postgraduates. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. The School of Physics and Astronomy holds Athena SWAN Silver and IoP Juno Champion awards, in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14997": {
      "title": "University Website Support Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Digital Skills Design and Training Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Administration JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We're looking for a University Website Support Assistant who will be working with the University Website and Web Strategy teams to improve the quality of the University’s web presence and estate Grade UE05: £29,588- £33,951 per annum Information Services Group/Learning, Teaching and Web Services/Website and Communications Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 2-year contract Shape what happens next. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Opportunity We're looking for a University Website Support Assistant who will be working with the University Website and Web Strategy teams to improve the quality of the University’s web presence and estate. In this role, you will support and enable the University web publishing community in creating, maintaining, and improving University-owned websites, by providing expert guidance to a diverse range of users. You will be managing and monitoring the University’s web estate registry, helping website owners ensure their websites’ regulatory compliance and performance. This role will ensure that web content meets University standards, accessibility requirements, and legal obligations, while delivering training, support, and quality assurance. It also contributes to continuous improvement by gathering insights, supporting the centrally managed platform’s development, and promoting best practice across the community. You'll join the Website and Communications (WAC) team, a highly collaborative and supportive part of Information Services. We work in the open: EdWeb 2 and the University's Student & Staff Portal (MyEd) are both built on open-source software, reflecting the University's commitment to open software and data across its services. You will work very closely with the Web Strategy team, actively contributing to the compliance, performance and effectiveness of the University’s large website estate. We'd love to hear from someone with strong communications skills, having the ability to engage with non-specialist audiences and support them to make the best use of the University’s website services to communicate with their audiences. A key skill is experience working with data, from collection to management and their visualisation for diverse audiences, to support insightful actions. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Responsibility and autonomy Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns, such as compressed hours or longer breaks in the working day. The team office location is at the west edge of the University’s central campus, in 5 Forrest Hill, Edinburgh, EH1 2QL. The role follows a hybrid working pattern, combining time working from home with regular time on campus: we ask that colleagues spend at least two days a week on-site, one of which is our shared team anchor day, so the team can collaborate, support one another and stay connected in person. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab ) How to apply We welcome informal enquiries about the role, to find out more, please email Jennifer Smith (University Website Team Manager) at Jen.Smith@ed.ac.uk . Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. The University is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion. If you have the right skills for the job, we want to hear from you. We encourage applications from the right candidates regardless of age, gender identity, disability, sexual orientation, religion, belief or race. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 5 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK , the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held at Forrest Hill (In-person) on the 17th and 18th November 2026. The Learning, Teaching & Web Services Directorate (LTW) brings together technology enhanced learning, e-learning, the University website, web services, graphics, multimedia, digital skills training and classroom technology teams to support learning, teaching and outreach for an enhanced student experience. We provide a wide range of technology and support services for innovative learning and teaching, including classroom tools, virtual learning environment (VLE), blended learning, bespoke development, online video, open educational resources (OER), taught online Masters programmes, massive open online courses (MOOCs), feedback and assessment. We manage the continuing expansion and diversification of the University website, student portal, and provide digital technology services in 400 teaching rooms and study spaces across the campus. Every year we host student internships, projects and events which promote innovation and align with the University strategic aims to promote new ways of working in learning, teaching and research. More information can be viewed at: http://www.ed.ac.uk/information-services/about/organisation/learning-teaching-web "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15005": {
      "title": "Project Archivist (Milestones in Care)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Heritage Collections (Research and Curatorial) Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Library and information management JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom As the Project Archivist (Milestones in Care), you will catalogue, preserve and share the archive of Milestone House, Scotland’s first purpose-built AIDS hospice, ensuring its important history and the experiences of those connected to it are accessible for future generations. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £ £48,822 per annum Information Services Group, Library & University Collections, Heritage Collections, Lothian Health Services Archive Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: January 2027 – December 2028 Location: the role will require working on site in both Edinburgh and Glasgow The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. As the Project Archivist (Milestones in Care), you will catalogue, preserve and share the archive of Milestone House, Scotland’s first purpose-built AIDS hospice, ensuring its important history and the experiences of those connected to it are accessible for future generations. Opened in 1991, Milestone House was a place of care, compassion and community for people living with HIV at a time when effective treatments were unavailable, and stigma was widespread. Funding from the Archives Revealed Consortium Grant will support a partnership between Lothian Health Services Archive at the University of Edinburgh, Waverley Care, Scotland’s HIV and hepatitis C charity and the National Library of Scotland Moving Image Archive, giving you the unique opportunity to work collaboratively to capture a vital chapter in Scotland’s response to HIV and the pioneering work of Waverley Care. The role will require you to work on site in both Edinburgh and in Glasgow. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise. Interesting work Variety every day Responsibility and autonomy This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) Informal enquiries may be directed to Amy Cawood, Lothian Health Services Archive Manager (LHSA), amy.cawood@ed.ac.uk How to apply: Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion: The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. The University is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion. If you have the right skills for the job, we want to hear from you. We encourage applications from the right candidates regardless of age, gender identity, disability, sexual orientation, religion, belief or race. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note: The closing date for internal applications is 9 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held Wednesday 3 December 2026. The Library helps people to grow their knowledge and create new connections through open services, collections and environments, sparking ideas that change the world. Library & University Collections teams (approximately 200 staff) provide leadership and oversight for the University's library and museum collections and services, and lead the University's transition to Open Scholarship. Collections comprise over 2 million print volumes, 2 million e-books, 200,000 e-journals and 100 kilometres of Heritage Collections which include rare books, archives, artworks and musical instruments. Library & University Collections deliver highly-regarded services to support teaching, learning and research across the University and wider communities. Services and access to collections are provided online and onsite through the Main Library, site libraries, the Centre for Research Collections, award-winning makerspace services and St Cecilia’s Hall Concert Room & Music Museum. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15008": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Institute of Molecular Plant Science Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for an experienced Research Assistant to become a key part of our newly established group working in the field of plant immunity and the proteins involved in it. Grade UE06: £34,610- £39,906 per annum CSE, School of Biological Sciences Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: 12 months The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. Our technical staff are essential to supporting teaching, research, and the smooth operation of our facilities. Whether you're maintaining cutting-edge lab equipment, facilitating ground-breaking research, developing new techniques, or enhancing student and staff experience with top-notch facilities, your work will have a lasting impact that stretches far beyond our academic community. We are looking for an experienced Research Assistant to become a key part of our newly established group working in the field of plant immunity and the proteins involved in it. You will support research across the whole lab, helping keep our plant materials thriving, our labs running smoothly, and our science moving forward. You will also play a valued role in welcoming and training new lab members. We seek candidates with experience in plant molecular biology laboratory and enjoy the organisational side of research as much as the science itself. You will join Institute of Molecular Plant Sciences (IMPS) at the University of Edinburgh, which is a vibrant scientific community within an exciting and beautiful city. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Flexible/hybrid where the roles allow This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter - Email contacts of two references As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from underrepresented groups. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 5 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. The Institute of Molecular Plant Sciences provides a stimulating environment for studies on a broad range of plant-based topics. IMPS is one of few plant-based research and teaching Institutes within the UK. Our research uses genetics, biochemistry, cell biology and modelling to examine how plants develop, function and evolve. Main research themes include developmental biology, plant cell signalling, speciation and molecular plant pathology. Research is funded by a diversity of organisations including BBSRC and commercial companies, and is supported by state-of the art facilities; an IMPS research forum facilitates inter-laboratory collaborations. IMPS has close links with SynthSys (the centre for Synthetic and Systems Biology at Edinburgh and the Royal Botanic Gardens, Edinburgh (RBGE), providing further breadth to our research base. In addition, researchers within IMPS have strong links with the James Hutton Institute. To find out more visit https://www.ed.ac.uk/biology/imps "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15071": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: Institute of Ecology and Evolution Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a postdoctoral research assistant as part of the UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship: ‘FORESIGHT: predicting population responses to climate change’. Grade UE07: £41,064-48,822 per annum School of Biological Sciences, Institute of Ecology and Evolution Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: Until 31 October 2030 The Opportunity: We are looking for a postdoctoral research assistant as part of the UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship: ‘ FORESIGHT: predicting population responses to climate change’ . This postdoctoral position will focus primarily on establishing ‘how changes in climate cause population change in birds and mammals ’. A proportion of the position’s time will be spent contributing to Open Science initiatives and databases through the SPI-Birds network and database ( https://spibirds.org/en ). There will also be opportunities to explore drivers of predictability in ecology and to contribute to cross-sector workshops with conservation and policy organisations. The role will involve conducting research to identify weather drivers, population modelling, demography, and ecological forecasting, as well as producing and testing statistical tools. The post holder will also be expected to contribute to the development of new ideas and approaches, to contribute to the overall direction of the research project, and to contribute to publishing or otherwise disseminating the outputs of research, likely including R package development as well as academic publications. The postdoctoral research assistant will work as part of various teams, including Dr Emily Simmonds’ group at the University of Edinburgh and the SPI-Birds development team based in the Netherlands but with contributors across Europe. This role combines methodological development and research on a pressing societal challenge, with Open Science, and the potential for applied impact and would suit someone whose interests span this spectrum. The successful candidate will benefit from individual training and travel funding and will join a growing research group in a highly supportive environment. This post is full-time (35 hours per week). View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and all backgrounds. We are committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workplace. If you need any reasonable adjustments during the application or interview process, please let us know. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 6 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. As the modern inheritors of our 400-year-old reputation for excellence, our staff and students contribute to a vibrant academic community whose visions are shaping tomorrow’s world. The School of Biological Sciences is at the leading edge in teaching, research and innovation. Independent rankings such as the Times Higher Education consistently place us amongst the top 25 universities in the world for biological sciences. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and to recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development opportunities for all staff. We place a particular focus on development for early-career researchers (postgraduate and postdoctoral research staff and personal fellowship holders), offering mentoring and an active ‘BioDocSoc’ organisation that brings together researchers for networking, events and training opportunities. The School of Biological Sciences holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance the representation of women in science, mathematics, engineering and technology. Our website contains more information about the School. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14596": {
      "title": "Software Engineer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: DCC and EDINA Products Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: IT JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom We are looking for a talented software engineering to join the ELM software engineering team within DCC and EDINA, contributing across the full software development lifecycle. The role involves engineering and maintaining full-stack features using Java, the Java Spring Framework, TypeScript and Angular, alongside database, API, integration and deployment work. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum, pro rata if part-time Information Services Group / DCC and EDINA Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) The Opportunity ELM is the University of Edinburgh’s secure gateway to generative artificial intelligence, providing safer access to Large Language Models for the University and wider UK tertiary education sector. We are looking for a skilled and collaborative Software Engineer to help design, build, test and support the ELM platform as it develops into a reliable, secure and accessible AI innovation service for education. You will join the ELM software engineering team within DCC and EDINA, contributing across the full software development life cycle. The role involves engineering and maintaining full-stack features using Java, the Java Spring Framework, TypeScript and Angular, alongside database, API, integration and deployment work. You will work with colleagues across product, service, testing, systems administration, security and support teams to deliver robust and user-focused functionality. This is an opportunity to work on a high-profile platform at the intersection of software engineering, education and responsible use of generative AI. You will contribute to the continued development of ELM as a trusted service, helping institutions and users access AI tools in a safer, more managed and effective way. The role will suit a software engineer with strong practical experience in modern web application development, relational databases, containerised deployment and continuous integration / continuous deployment practices. You should be comfortable working collaboratively in an agile or iterative environment and able to communicate effectively with both technical and non-technical colleagues. Your skills and attributes for success: Substantial hands-on experience engineering software using Java and the Java Spring Framework, including Spring Boot or equivalent Spring technologies. Practical experience developing web applications using TypeScript and Angular. Experience working with relational databases and SQL, including schema changes, queries, data integrity and troubleshooting. Experience building or working with RESTful APIs and software integrations. Experience using Docker, Kubernetes and containerised deployment approaches. Experience with continuous integration and deployment pipelines, automated builds, automated testing and Git-based workflows. Understanding of software testing, code review, secure coding, maintainability and technical documentation. Experience working in an agile, iterative or collaborative software delivery environment. Working knowledge of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines and good general knowledge of coding for accessibility. Good communication skills and the ability to work effectively with technical and non-technical colleagues. Desirable experience includes: Experience with additional programming languages such as Python or Ruby. Experience with cloud platforms such as AWS, Azure or Google Cloud. Experience with micro services, messaging, event-driven systems or API gateways. Experience supporting production services or working within ITIL-style support processes. Experience mentoring junior engineers or contributing to team standards. Experience in higher education, research, public sector or other complex organisational environments. Experience integrating with AI/LLM platforms, model provider APIs, authentication systems or usage-monitoring services. Awareness of data protection, privacy, security and responsible-use considerations relevant to generative AI services. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. The opportunity to be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive staff benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives and flexible work options. This post is full-time, 35 hours per week; however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working, on a non-contractual basis, that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter Your cover letter should set out how your skills and experience meet the requirements of the role, with particular reference to your experience in software engineering, full-stack web development, Java/Spring, TypeScript/Angular, databases, APIs, containerisation and CI/CD practices. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and particularly encourage applications from candidates from groups that are underrepresented in software engineering and digital technology roles. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 4 Novembe r 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews dates to be confirmed. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14907": {
      "title": "Kitchen Porter",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: ACE Catering Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Hospitality JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom The role will be based at our South Hall C&E kitchen, located within Pollock Halls. As one of our Kitchen Porters, you will support the operational service delivery to our students and commercial guests throughout the year. Grade UE02 - £24,547 per annum (pro-rata), £13,45 per hour, plus 18.1% Uplift CSG /Accommodation, Catering and Events Part-time: Guaranteed Hours - 20 hours per year Open-ended (permanent) Location: Pollock Halls, 18 Holyrood Park Road, Edinburgh, EH16 5AY The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, we are a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. The role will be based at our South Hall C&E kitchen, located within Pollock Halls. As one of our Kitchen Porters , you will support the operational service delivery to our students and commercial guests throughout the year. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well and impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do, therefore ask yourself: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Interesting work. Good salary / pay rates. Responsibility and autonomy. Excellent benefits and support. This role is guaranteed hours position with 20 hours guaranteed per year and fully based on the campus. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 4 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone Interviews will be held within 2 weeks after closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15015": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Centre for Cardiovascular Science Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are currently looking to recruit a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to perform scientific research to investigate the characteristics of indoor air pollution and its potential to affect health. The project forms part of a wider multi-disciplinary UKRI Hub of research organisations in the UK called \"INHABIT\", which looks at how building net-zero measures may affect indoor air quality. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences/Centre for Cardiovascular Science Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 18 months The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you’re looking to take the next step in your current specialism, explore a new challenge, or broaden your experience, we offer opportunities where you can flourish and make a real difference. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you We are currently looking to recruit a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to perform scientific research to investigate the characteristics of indoor air pollution and its potential to affect health. The project forms part of a wider multi-disciplinary UKRI Hub of research organisations in the UK called \" INHABIT \", which looks at how building net-zero measures may affect indoor air quality. This project will be predominantly lab based, using oxidative potential assays to assess the reactivity of air pollution particles and in vivo models to assess the potential toxicological effects of these particles. The role holder will contribute to the development of new ideas, approaches, concepts or techniques, as well as take an active role in the direction of the research project and dissemination of research outputs A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week) and fully on-campus at the Edinburgh BioQuarter (next to the Royal Infirmary of Edinburgh). The post is a fixed term appointment for 18 months with the possibility of extension depending on funding. Your skills and attributes for success: A PhD, or equivalent, in a related biomedical background, e.g. air pollution or particle research, toxicology, physiology. Experience of some of the laboratory techniques involved in the research, e.g. particle characterisation, acellular assays of oxidative potential, in vivo rodent models, assays to measure signalling pathways, (e.g ELISAs for inflammatory cytokines, fluorescence assays, flow cytometry), organ bath preparations, histology. Experience of recruitment of volunteers or public interaction for research studies, would be advantageous Ability to work independently and as part of a team, contributing to both the practical work but also assisting with the project management, e.g. organising provision or purchase of research consumables, liaising with collaborating labs. Enthusiasm to develop the research area and programme through manuscript writing and supporting grant applications. Click to view a copy of the full job description . Application Information Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team you can expect: A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community Comprehensive Staff Benefits, such as a generous holiday entitlement, competitive pension schemes, staff discounts, and family-friendly initiatives. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the total value of your pay and benefits Championing equality, diversity and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 4 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. School of Neurological and Cardiovascular Sciences We host research and postgraduate training in neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research. We are one of six schools in the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine at the University of Edinburgh and are home to the Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research. Institute for Neuroscience and Cardiovascular Research Our institute fosters collaborative neuroscience, cardiovascular, metabolic and psychiatric research to tackle some of the world's most significant health challenges. Our research explores health and disease across the body and life course – from tiny changes in cells to the lived experience of patients. Together, we work to advance the understanding, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of major health conditions including heart disease, stroke, metabolic dysfunction, mental illness, dementia and neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders. Empowered by genetics, advanced imaging, artificial intelligence and clinical trials, our research seamlessly connects discovery science with clinical care. Our work is internationally acclaimed, achieving 4* and world-leading classifications for the majority of our research, health and wealth impacts and research papers in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 assessment, the UK’s system for assessing the quality of research in UK higher education institutions. As part of driving progress in our core research areas, we host internationally leading research centres and state-of-the-art technologies: Anne Rowling Regenerative Neurology Clinic British Heart Foundation (BHF) Centre of Research Excellence Centre for Pesticide Suicide Prevention | CPSP Euan MacDonald Centre for Motor Neurone Disease Research Edinburgh Clinical Research Facility Edinburgh Imaging Jennifer Brown Research Laboratory Medical Research Council/British Hearth Foundation Centre of Research Excellence in Advanced Cardiac Therapies MS Society Edinburgh Centre for MS Research | MS Society Muir Maxwell Epilepsy Centre Patrick Wild Centre Row Fogo Centre for Research into Ageing and the Brain Simons Initiative for the Developing Brain (SIDB) UK Dementia Research Institute at Edinburgh UKRI Hub for Metabolic Psychiatry Our institute community Our researchers drive cutting-edge science, enabling our students and working hand in hand with our expert technical and professional services colleagues. We are committed to creating a collaborative, inclusive and welcoming community, where everyone feels supported, empowered and inspired to achieve their best. Training and education We offer a wide range of postgraduate training opportunities. Students learn from world-class researchers, access outstanding facilities and get support to develop. Our graduates go on to successful careers in academia, research, healthcare and industry worldwide. Our colleagues are also leading contributors to undergraduate programmes. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15016": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Centre for Inflammation Research Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Cellular Senescence in Lung Injury. A postdoctoral research position defining the contribution of cellular senescence to lung injury and inflammation in translationally relevant human models will be funded by the Baillie Gifford Pandemic Science Hub (BG PSH) which was created with philanthropic support from Baillie Gifford and comprises scientists, clinicians, professional services staff and students united in a common goal to use human genetics and experimental medicine to rapidly find and test the effects of drugs. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum School/Department: School of Regeneration and Repair, Centre for Inflammation Research Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 6 months The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Cellular Senescence in Lung Injury. A postdoctoral research position defining the contribution of cellular senescence to lung injury and inflammation in translationally relevant human models will be funded by the Baillie Gifford Pandemic Science Hub (BG PSH) which was created with philanthropic support from Baillie Gifford and comprises scientists, clinicians, professional services staff and students united in a common goal to use human genetics and experimental medicine to rapidly find and test the effects of drugs. The successful candidate will use a novel, high content, image analysis-based cell painting assay to define characteristics of senescent cells and their response to known senolytic drugs. They will optimise this process for use as a high throughput screen to identify novel senolytic / senomorphic drugs from available drug libraries. The senolytic potential of lead candidate molecules will be tested in a clinically relevant human precision cut lung slice model of irradiation-induced senescence. Your skills and attributes for success: Experience of preparing and working with working with human Precision Cut Lung Slices (PCLS). Extensive tissue culture experience, including established cell lines and primary cell culture and characterisation of cellular senescence. Imaging techniques including multiplex - IHC and ICC, live cell imaging and confocal microscopy. Assay development (including high throughput application) and standardisation Expertise in qRT-PCR and immunoassays including Flow Cytometry, ELISA/ cytokine bead array and Western blotting People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Opportunities for publication This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time working patterns. This role requires presence onsite for lab work and therefore hybrid work cannot be offered for this role. View the full job description ( opens in a new browser tab) How to apply As part of this application, you are required to upload a cover letter/statement of not more than one page detailing how you meet each of the essential criteria as outlined on the job description and a CV . These items should be uploaded as one single combined document in either PDF or Word format. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We’re committed to building an inclusive workplace where everyone feels valued, respected, and empowered to thrive. At IRR we celebrate diversity in all its forms and actively seek to create a team that reflects the communities around us. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds, experiences, and identities. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key notes for applicants The closing date for applications is 4 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We anticipate that interviews will be held within 2-3 weeks of the advert closing date. The Centre for Inflammation Research (CIR) is a world-leading centre dedicated to understanding the mechanisms of inflammation and translating these insights into new therapies for inflammatory diseases. Part of The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR), CIR brings together discovery science, clinical expertise, and cross-disciplinary innovation to improve human health across the lifespan. Our mission is to transform the understanding and treatment of inflammation through cutting-edge research, inclusive collaboration, and impactful translation. By uniting discovery scientists, clinicians, chemists, engineers, and industry partners, we aim to develop innovative tools, therapies, and strategies that address pressing global health challenges. Our research is structured across three interconnected themes - Infection and Immunity, Damage and Repair, and Experimental Medicine - providing a powerful framework for scientific collaboration and translational excellence. Together, these themes investigate how inflammation starts, progresses, and resolves, and how it contributes to both health and disease. Through advanced experimental models, patient-focused research, and state-of-the-art technologies, we accelerate discovery and ensure our work leads to meaningful, real-world impact. Centre for Inflammation Research website ( https://inflammation-research.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) The Institute for Regeneration and Repair The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR) is cross-College with staff and students from the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) School of Regeneration and Repair (SRR) and the College of Science and Engineering (CSE), School of Biological Sciences and School of Chemistry. The School of Regeneration and Repair is one of six schools in CMVM. At IRR our scientists and clinicians study tissue regeneration and repair to advance human health and reproductive outcomes. The Institute incorporates three leading research centres with a focus on regenerative medicine, inflammation and reproductive health. Our Director is Professor Stuart Forbes. IRR has a 5-year income of over £200M and is a collaborative hub for commercial, clinical and academic endeavour. Based at Edinburgh BioQuarter we bring together world leading research expertise to drive understanding and deliver clinical and translational outcomes. IRR has a work environment built on trust and encourages the very best talent from all backgrounds and at all levels to join us. A diverse team strengthens our ability to innovate and support cutting-edge world leading research. Supporting and empowering our team at all levels is central to our approach. Our team is ambitious, acts with integrity and demonstrates commitment to progress and impact. We aim to achieve excellence in all that we do, always being principled, considerate and respectful. Institute for Regeneration and Repair website ( https://regeneration-repair.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15021": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Centre for Inflammation Research Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - BioQuarter, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Baillie Gifford Pandemic Science Hub (https://psh.ac.uk/) seeks a postdoctoral researcher experienced with pharmaceutical formulation and drug delivery to develop next-generation inhaled therapies to join our dynamic multidisciplinary team. In this role, the postdoctoral researcher will conduct research using analytical techniques to investigate nanoparticle-based drug delivery and nebulisation technologies. They will analyse data, lead experiments, and contribute to research publications, while also supervising students and engaging in collaborative projects. Grade UE07: £41,064 - £48,822 per annum School of Regeneration and Repair / Centre for Inflammation Research Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed term: 24-month contract The Opportunity: Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger Make a meaningful impact Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing. The Baillie Gifford Pandemic Science Hub (https://psh.ac.uk/) seeks a postdoctoral researcher experienced with pharmaceutical formulation and drug delivery to develop next-generation inhaled therapies to join our dynamic multidisciplinary team. In this role, the postdoctoral researcher will conduct research using analytical techniques to investigate nanoparticle-based drug delivery and nebulisation technologies. They will analyse data, lead experiments, and contribute to research publications, while also supervising students and engaging in collaborative projects. Ideal candidates will have a PhD in chemistry, chemical engineering, pharmaceutical or biomedical sciences, along with strong communication and teamwork skills. Experience with inhalation and formulation development is a plus. This role involves participation in innovative ARIA-funded projects within an inclusive and supportive work environment, with opportunities for professional development. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Leading-edge research Opportunities for publication This post is full-time (35 hours per week) View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply As part of this application, you are required to upload a cover letter/statement of not more than one page detailing how you meet each of the essential criteria as outlined on the job description and a CV . These items should be uploaded as one single combined document in either PDF or Word format. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Key notes for applicants The closing date for applications is 4 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. We anticipate that interviews will be held within 2-3 weeks of the advert closing date. The Centre for Inflammation Research (CIR) is a world-leading centre dedicated to understanding the mechanisms of inflammation and translating these insights into new therapies for inflammatory diseases. Part of The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR), CIR brings together discovery science, clinical expertise, and cross-disciplinary innovation to improve human health across the lifespan. Our mission is to transform the understanding and treatment of inflammation through cutting-edge research, inclusive collaboration, and impactful translation. By uniting discovery scientists, clinicians, chemists, engineers, and industry partners, we aim to develop innovative tools, therapies, and strategies that address pressing global health challenges. Our research is structured across three interconnected themes - Infection and Immunity, Damage and Repair, and Experimental Medicine - providing a powerful framework for scientific collaboration and translational excellence. Together, these themes investigate how inflammation starts, progresses, and resolves, and how it contributes to both health and disease. Through advanced experimental models, patient-focused research, and state-of-the-art technologies, we accelerate discovery and ensure our work leads to meaningful, real-world impact. Centre for Inflammation Research website ( https://inflammation-research.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) The Institute for Regeneration and Repair The Institute for Regeneration and Repair (IRR) is cross-College with staff and students from the College of Medicine and Veterinary Medicine (CMVM) School of Regeneration and Repair (SRR) and the College of Science and Engineering (CSE), School of Biological Sciences and School of Chemistry. The School of Regeneration and Repair is one of six schools in CMVM. At IRR our scientists and clinicians study tissue regeneration and repair to advance human health and reproductive outcomes. The Institute incorporates three leading research centres with a focus on regenerative medicine, inflammation and reproductive health. Our Director is Professor Stuart Forbes. IRR has a 5-year income of over £200M and is a collaborative hub for commercial, clinical and academic endeavour. Based at Edinburgh BioQuarter we bring together world leading research expertise to drive understanding and deliver clinical and translational outcomes. IRR has a work environment built on trust and encourages the very best talent from all backgrounds and at all levels to join us. A diverse team strengthens our ability to innovate and support cutting-edge world leading research. Supporting and empowering our team at all levels is central to our approach. Our team is ambitious, acts with integrity and demonstrates commitment to progress and impact. We aim to achieve excellence in all that we do, always being principled, considerate and respectful. Institute for Regeneration and Repair website ( https://regeneration-repair.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab) "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15102": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Laboratory for Foundations of Computer Science Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom The successful candidate will work on the RubberDUQ project within the Quantum Software Lab, developing mathematical foundations and practical tools for hybrid quantum-classical programming. The project uses categorical semantics to give quantum programs, including their interaction with classical control and measurement, a precise meaning, and turns that meaning into compilers and optimisations that are correct by constructio Grade UE07: £41,064.00 - £48,822.00 Per Annum College of Science and Engineering/ School of Informatics Full Time: 35 hours per week. Fixed term contract: 12 months, Until 31/12/2027. The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger.With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our student’s performance and wellbeing. The successful candidate will work on the RubberDUQ project within the Quantum Software Lab, developing mathematical foundations and practical tools for hybrid quantum-classical programming. The project uses categorical semantics to give quantum programs, including their interaction with classical control and measurement, a precise meaning, and turns that meaning into compilers and optimisations that are correct by construction. Depending on the candidate's background, the work may range from category theory and programming language semantics to compiler design and implementation. The Research Associate will collaborate with the project team and with QSL's academic and industrial partners. This post is advertised as full-time. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. Your skills and attributes for success: Degree in Computer Science, Mathematics, or Physics). The following criteria are not strict yes/no requirements; rather, candidates will be assessed on the strength of their experience and evidence across these areas. Recruitment will aim to select the candidates demonstrating the strongest overall profile. Experience conducting research in one or more of: categorical semantics, programming language theory, compilers, or quantum computing. Proven expertise in the mathematical or software techniques required to undertake the proposed research, such as category theory, formal semantics, or compiler implementation. Evidence of high-quality research performance (e.g., publications, technical reports, software contributions, or comparable outputs). Ability to communicate complex information clearly, both verbally and in writing. Contact details for enquiries (name and email address): Prof. Chris Heunen, chris.heunen@ed.ac.uk A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Leading-edge research Contributing to the work and purpose of the University. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: CV Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab). The University may be able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. This will depend on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant. Please note if the role is offered on a part-time basis, it may result in sponsorship being dependent on a number of factors specific to the successful applicant or the role no longer meeting the Home Office’s criteria for sponsorship. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 4 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Informatics is the study of how natural and artificial systems store, process and communicate information. Research in Informatics promises to take information technology to a new level, and to place information at the heart of 21st century science, technology and society. The School enjoys collaborations across many disciplines in the University, spanning all three College, and also participates as a strategic partner in the Alan Turing Institute and is home to a number of Centres for Doctoral Training. The School provides a fertile environment for a wide range of studies focused on understanding computation in both artificial and natural systems. It attracts students around the world to study in our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Informatics is one of seven schools in the College of Science and Engineering, at the University of Edinburgh. It is recognised for the employability of its graduates, its contributions to entrepreneurship, and the excellence of its research. Since the first Research Assessment Exercise in 1986, Informatics at Edinburgh has consistently been assessed to have more internationally excellent and world-class research than any other submission in Computer Science and Informatics. The latest REF 2021 results have again confirmed that ours is the largest concentration of internationally excellent research in the UK. This contributes to our ranking of consistently being in the top 30 world-wide. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14951": {
      "title": "University Teacher in Design Informatics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Informatics Category: Academic JobFunction: Teacher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom The School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh invites applications for a University Teacher, to deliver teaching and student support on Informatics courses and undertake other teaching, student support and administrative duties. This post is fixed term until 30 June 2026 It is advertised as full-time (35 hours per week), however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. Grade UE07: £41,064 - 48,822 per annum CSE / School of Informatics Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: contract ending 30 June 2027 Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger - where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. The School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh invites applications for a University Teacher in Design Informatics, to deliver teaching and student support on Design Informatics courses and undertake other teaching, student support and administrative duties. Candidates should have expertise in data vizualisation and/or in building and implementing data representations , experience in teaching, and be interested and able to teach undergraduate and Masters level courses in Design Informatics. In particular, we are very interested in applicants who already have experience in teaching (including imparting knowledge, motivating and inspiring students, developing teaching materials, methods and approaches and assessment of student performance ) in courses of relevance to Design informatics / data visualization. This post is fixed term until 31 st June 2026. It is advertised as full-time (35 hours per week), however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. The salary for this post is UE07: £41,064 to £48,822 per annum A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Working within one of the world’s leading universities Responsibility and autonomy Excellent benefits and support This post is associated with the Institute for Design Informatics (IDI) ( designinformatics.org ), a joint research initiative between the Schools of Informatics and Design. IDI draws upon Edinburgh’s unique combination of strengths in Informatics and Design, to inspire, equip and nurture a new generation of researchers, practitioners and entrepreneurs at the intersection of data, design and technology. IDI researchers engage in a wide-range of projects, with strengths around digital health and care, creative industries, fintech, data visualisation and analysis, digital humanities, co-creative and speculative methods, human-computer interaction, human-centered security and privacy, and the societal and ethical dimensions of data and AI. The successful candidate will join IDI’s dynamic team of academics, researchers, PhD students, software research engineers and professional services staff and their dedicated fully equipped lab spaces for data visualization, digital fabrication, and public and student engagement. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students performance and wellbeing. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab). How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 3 Novmeber 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interview dates to be confirmed. Informatics is the study of how natural and artificial systems store, process and communicate information. Research in Informatics promises to take information technology to a new level, and to place information at the heart of 21st century science, technology and society. The School enjoys collaborations across many disciplines in the University, spanning all three College, and also participates as a strategic partner in the Alan Turing Institute and is home to a number of Centres for Doctoral Training. The School provides a fertile environment for a wide range of studies focused on understanding computation in both artificial and natural systems. It attracts students around the world to study in our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Informatics is one of seven schools in the College of Science and Engineering, at the University of Edinburgh. It is recognised for the employability of its graduates, its contributions to entrepreneurship, and the excellence of its research. Since the first Research Assessment Exercise in 1986, Informatics at Edinburgh has consistently been assessed to have more internationally excellent and world-class research than any other submission in Computer Science and Informatics. The latest REF 2021 results have again confirmed that ours is the largest concentration of internationally excellent research in the UK. This contributes to our ranking of consistently being in the top 30 world-wide. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15044": {
      "title": "Head of HR",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: HR - Directors Office Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Human Resources JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom This leadership position requires an experienced HR professional with a deep understanding of the unique opportunities and challenges in a multi-stakeholder and complex organisation. With a successful track record of delivering complex change at scale, you will partner with stakeholders across the University on a range of strategic initiatives, leading on all matters relating to HR. You will have regular engagement with trade union representatives and work collaboratively on our plans. Grade UE09: £63,606 - £78,469 per annum Corporate Services Group – Human Resources Full Time: 35 hours per week Open Ended: Permanent contract The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. The University of Edinburgh isn’t just any employer. We are a world class organisation and our people make an ongoing contribution to global thinking, progress and research. You will be working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. As part of the University of Edinburgh, you’ll be making a difference to everything around you. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. As Head of HR Partnering, you will lead the delivery of HR within an aligned area, leading and developing a team of Business Partners to provide a comprehensive professional service supporting both strategic and operational activity. This leadership position requires an experienced HR professional with a deep understanding of the unique opportunities and challenges in a multi-stakeholder and complex organisation. With a successful track record of delivering complex change at scale, you will partner with stakeholders across the University on a range of strategic initiatives, leading on all matters relating to HR. You will have regular engagement with trade union representatives and work collaboratively on our plans. People have always been at the heart of our work. As part of the University, you are a part of our community. We are looking for people with drive, determination, and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful/personalised flexibility for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Working within one of the world’s leading universities Interesting work which offers variety every day Responsibility and autonomy to make a difference Contributing to the work and purpose of the University This post is full-time however, we are open to considering flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter explaining how your experience fits well with this specific role and why you want it. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 3 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews are likely to be held in early November . Human Resources is a strategic partner in delivering the University's strategy and values. We have an important role in shaping inspirational and innovative long-term people strategies that attract, develop, reward and retain a highly motivated and diverse workforce. We are a community of over 100 HR professionals who strive for a positive supportive culture and working environment. We influence change initiatives, define talent and development opportunities and deliver quality life cycle services that help the University and its Academic and Professional Service staff succeed. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/14280": {
      "title": "Out of Hours Response Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: ACE Property Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Estates and Facilities JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for confident, proactive individuals to join our Out of Hours Response Team, delivering a professional and responsive front-line service across our estate. Grade UE03: £24,729 to £26,320 per annum plus a 30% Premium Band for flexible working CSG / Accommodation, Catering and Events - Property Part Time: 23.5 hours per week, 4 on, 4 off Night Shift (7pm - 2am and can be adjusted to suite business needs) Open Ended (permanent) Vacancies: 1 Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. Here, you can continue to make a difference in everything around you. Take on new challenges, grow your career, be recognised for your contributions, and benefit from our commitment to your wellbeing. Be part of something bigger — where your work has real impact and your development matters. There are so many reasons to take your next step with us. From student accommodation to café’s restaurants and hotels, this is a 24/7 organisation that embraces the traditional and the modern in equal measure. From historical buildings to state of the art technical and research hubs our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities’ and our values drive everything we do. We are looking for confident, proactive individuals to join our Out of Hours Response Team, delivering a professional and responsive front-line service across our estate. The Opportunity: As an Out of Hours Response Officer, you will be at the centre of operations overnight, responding to incidents, supporting residents and maintaining a safe, inclusive and well managed environment. This includes welfare concerns, fire alarm activations, security issues and urgent maintenance matters, often as the first point of contact. This is a highly visible, fast paced role where no two shifts are the same. You will carry out patrols, respond to calls, provide first aid and work closely with internal teams and external services to resolve situations effectively. We are looking for individuals who are calm under pressure, confident in dealing with people and able to make sound decisions in dynamic situations. Having a valid UK driver’s license is essential. At ACE our mission is to ‘enrich student learning and growth as well as impact our local communities.’ Our values drive everything we do: Are you PASSIONATE about making a difference? Do you like to COLLABORATE and achieve things as a team? Is INCLUSION at the forefront of all you do? Do you thrive when given OWNERSHIP ? Are you inspired by INNOVATION and excited to bring new ideas to life? A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Good salary/pay rates Excellent benefits and support Contributing to the work and purpose of the University Location: Pollock Halls, 18 Holyrood Park Road, Edinburgh, EH16 5AY This post is part time (23.5 hours per week) and is campus based. Click here to view the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab ) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 10 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone. Interviews will be held 2 weeks after closing date. Accommodation, Catering and Events provides catered and self-catered residential accommodation for over 10,000 students, including community and welfare support through its Residence Life service. We also operate 17 cafes and a range of other catering products to students and staff across the University estate. Under our commercial brand, The University of Edinburgh Hospitality and Events Collection, we deliver hotel accommodation options to around 200,000 guests each year plus an extensive selection of conference, catering and event services to over 2,000 events. Please visit our website for further information. "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15019": {
      "title": "Deputy Facilities Operations Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Facilities Category: Professional, Administration and Operational JobFunction: Estates and Facilities JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom The Deputy Facilities Operations Manager within the Information Services Group (ISG), leads a team responsible for maintaining safe, compliant, and high-performance environments that support critical University operations Grade UE07: £41,885 - £49,798 per annum Information Services Group / IS Corporate Full-time: 35 hours per week Open-ended (permanent) Where passion meets purpose. Be part of something bigger. The University isn’t just any employer. We are part of a local community and a contributor to global thinking, progress and research. In Edinburgh, you are in one of the world’s most attractive cities with active arts and social sectors, while working in a University that has made significant contributions to society, medicine, physics and teaching for over four centuries. Our people have helped create the modern world and are working on more ground-breaking technologies and practices. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation, helping us thrive and innovate together. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Opportunity: The Deputy Facilities Operations Manager within the Information Services Group (ISG), leads a team responsible for maintaining safe, compliant, and high-performance environments that support critical University operations. This is a pivotal leadership role for a professional who understands that exceptional management is about enabling people to thrive. As a key member of the ISG Facilities senior management team, you will not just maintain buildings; you will shape the environment where research and teaching happen. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. This is a 100% on-campus role and not suitable for hybrid working People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. Discover more about working at the University of Edinburgh on our Jobs website . A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Working within one of the world’s leading universities Good salary/pay rates Responsibility and autonomy Note: While we champion flexibility, this role requires a 100% on-campus presence to effectively lead frontline operations and engage directly with the campus community. View the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply To apply, please submit your CV and a Cover Letter. In your cover letter, we ask that you specifically address: Your experience in leading and developing facilities teams within a complex organisation. An example of how you have successfully driven a culture change or service improvement initiative. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. We believe that diverse teams build better estates. We welcome applications from all backgrounds and particularly encourage candidates who can bring varied perspectives to our leadership team. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages . On this occasion the University will not consider applicants requiring sponsorship for this role. International workers will therefore only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 10 November 2026. Unless stated otherwise, the closing time for applications is 11:59 pm (UK time). If you are applying from outside the UK, the closing time shown on our adverts will automatically adjust to your browser’s local time zone . Interviews will be held week commencing 30 November 2026. Information Services Corporate comprises Business Administration, IS Facilities, Disability Information Services, IS HR, Strategy Management and Special Projects. Its main role is supporting the CIO, Head of Knowledge Management and Planning, and the Directorates, to manage the smooth running of IS and its interactions within the wider University community. For further information visit the IS Corporate Office webpage (Opens new browser tab). "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15109": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Biological Sciences Category: Technical JobFunction: Laboratory Services JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom We are looking for a highly motivated research technician to work on epigenetic transposon silencing in the germline. Grade UE05 (Salary Range: £30,180-£34,630) School of Biological Sciences, Institute for Stem Cell Research Full time, 35 hours per week Fixed-Term Contract up to 12 months The Opportunity: We are looking for a highly motivated research technician to work on epigenetic transposon silencing in the germline. Specifically, we seek to build upon our recent advancements in understanding the mechanism of piRNA-directed transposon methylation (1-4). A major goal of the laboratory is to explore these mechanisms in vivo and through biochemical as well as structural approaches. You will be part of the O’Carroll group located the Centre for Cell Biology, University of Edinburgh. Applicants must have, or shortly obtain a bachelor’s degree. The post requires experience in germ cell research and molecular biology. A working knowledge of the germline is essential. An overview of the laboratory can be found at https://edwebprofiles.ed.ac.uk/profile/donal-o-carroll . Please address all enquires to donal.ocarroll@ed.ac.uk References Nature . 2024 doi: 10.1038/s41586-024-07963-3. PMID: 39294378. Mol Cell . 2024 doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2024.01.014. PMID: 38359823. Nat Commun. 2020 doi: 10.1038/s41467-020-17372-5. PMID: 32719317. Nature. 2020 doi: 10.1038/s41586-020-2557-5. PMID: 32674113. This post is advertised as full-time (35 hours per week), however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. Click to view a copy of the full job description (opens new browser tab) Application Information Please ensure you include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter As a valued member of our team you can expect: A competitive salary An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community Comprehensive Staff Benefits, such as a generous holiday entitlement, competitive pension schemes, staff discounts, and family-friendly initiatives. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the total value of your pay and benefits Championing equality, diversity and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter and we are also Stonewall Scotland Diversity Champions, actively promoting LGBT equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is unable to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. International applicants will therefore be unable to apply for and secure a Skilled Worker visa. They will only be able to take up this role if they can demonstrate an alternative right to work in the UK. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 10 November 2026 . Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm GMT. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Interviews will be held TBC "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15124": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Global hazard hotspot identification",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Department: Institute for Infrastructure and Environment Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom The School of Engineering seeks to appoint a Research Associate. The primary objective is to investigate how shifting climate patterns alter flood and drought hazard patterns and wildfire occurrence. This role is UE07 and is available on a fixed-term basis for up to 12 months. Grade UE07: £41,885-£49,798 per annum School of Engineering, College of Science & Engineering Institute for Infrastructure and Environment Full Time: 35 hours per week Fixed Term: contract up to 12 months The Opportunity: Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. This role sits at the critical intersection of climate adaptation, wildfire dynamics, and water security. The primary objective is to investigate how shifting climate patterns alter flood and drought hazard patterns and wildfire occurrence. Additionally it will explore how fire-modified landscapes drive changes in hydrology. You will develop an integrated modelling framework that connects climate change projections (e.g. CMIP6 or ISIMIP) to hydrological responses, specifically focusing on the cascading impacts of fire on water systems under future Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs). A core component of the work involves modelling future hazard occurrences at the global scale (spatially and temporally) – from floods to droughts and wildfire. The work will develop a probabilistic framework, handling uncertainty using a ensemble approach, to deliver a regional analysis of changing global hazards (floods (fluvial and pluvial), droughts (meterological and hydrological) and wildfire). Hotspots of increasing hazard magnitude/severity or duration will be identified and will feed into a wider project (led by the University of Bristol) exploring the global health burden of water supply under different SSPs. Floods and droughts will be detected using threshold metrics and events extracted from global climate projections (e.g. CMIP6/ISIMIP). Wildfire occurrence will be modelled not merely as a static hazard, but as a dynamic driver of landscape change. You will utilise large-scale climate ensembles (e.g., CMIP6 ISIMIP) and land-use projections to simulate future fire frequencies, severities, and spatial extents. This fire occurrence modelling will serve as the foundational input for assessing how vegetation loss and soil property changes—such as increased water repellency—alter the hydrological cycle. Working at a large scale, you will be responsible for the necessary data processing, synthesis, and downscaling required to bridge the gap between coarse global climate models and actionable local risk insights. You will employ probabilistic approaches to handle uncertainties in both climate projections, water jazards and fire behaviour, ensuring robust estimates of future water-related hazards. The outputs will directly feed the wider project consortium (University of Bristol and Newcastle – exploring the potential global health burden from SSPs). This position demands strong skills in geospatial analysis, climate model and data knowledge, and statistical programming to translate complex, multi-hazard interactions into outputs. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. We are also open to considering requests for hybrid working (on a non-contractual basis) that combines a mix of remote and regular on-campus working. Delete/amend statements if not appropriate (e.g. if role already part-time or if flexible working and/or hybrid working arrangements are not possible). Click her to view the full job description (opens in a new browser tab) How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - CV - Cover letter stating relevant previous experience and motivation to join this project. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: A competitive salary. An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 24 November 2026 Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. Engineering has been taught at Edinburgh in one form or another since 1673. In 2002, the present School of Engineering was formed from the previously existing departments of Chemical Engineering, Civil Engineering, Electronics and Electrical Engineering, and Mechanical Engineering. Since then, the School has grown from strength to strength, growing rapidly in the last 5 years, now having the largest number of students among the seven Schools in the College of Science and Engineering, offering undergraduate degree programmes across all disciplines and taught Masters courses in niche areas. All of the undergraduate degree programmes are accredited by the relevant professional institutions. The School of Engineering addresses diverse complex challenges across the entire field of engineering, at the micro- and macro/global scales. At the microscopic scale, its research supports the design and deployment of novel nanotechnology and devices for biological monitoring and medical diagnosis; at the macro/global scale, it optimises the security and sustainability of the built environment and develops engineering solutions to climate change issues. In REF 2021 the joint submission from the University of Edinburgh and Heriot-Watt University to General Engineering was ranked 1st in Scotland and 3rd in the UK for quality and breadth of research. The ranking cited is based upon the breadth and quality of research which apply standard formula (as used by the Times Higher Education) to the REF 2021 results. The School has a strong track record in producing more than 50 technology spin-outs and developing industry links that enable our graduates to build career-long relationships. Current research income in the School was c.£22.0M in 2020/21. We aim to ensure that our culture and systems support flexible and family-friendly working and recognise and value diversity across all our staff and students. The School has an active programme offering support and professional development for all staff; providing mentoring, training, and networking opportunities. The School of Engineering holds a bronze Athena SWAN award, in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education & research. Further information, including details of undergraduate & postgraduate taught programmes: www.eng.ed.ac.uk "
    },
    "https://elxw.fa.em3.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/15129": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Department: Chemistry Category: Academic JobFunction: Researcher JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Edinburgh - Kings Buildings, Midlothian, United Kingdom Postdoctoral Research Associate (King-Smith Group) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position to work under the supervision of Dr Emma King-Smith on a project funded by the EPSRC. The successful candidate will work on projects to deliver new machine learning models for the delivery of novel therapeutic candidates. You can find out more about the King-Smith Group at the King-Smith Group Website. Grade: UE07 and salary scale: £41,885-£49,798 School of Chemistry / College of Science and Engineering Full Time (35 hours per week), however we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. Fixed term (18 months) The Opportunity: Postdoctoral Research Associate (King-Smith Group) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position to work under the supervision of Dr Emma King-Smith on a project funded by the EPSRC. The successful candidate will work on projects to deliver new machine learning models for the delivery of novel therapeutic candidates. You can find out more about the King-Smith Group at the King-Smith Group Website. Applicants should have: • A PhD in chemistry, computer science, or related discipline (or have submitted a PhD thesis prior to taking up the appointment). • Possess a deep interest in the development of new machine learning methods and their application to the chemical sciences. • A proven track record of successfully completing research projects. • Excellent written, oral and interpersonal communication skills are essential. Click to view a copy of the full Job Description How to apply Please include the following documents in your application: - Up to date CV with details of two referees - Cover letter (max 1 page) - Research summary (max 2 pages) As a valued member of our team, you can expect: • A competitive salary. • An exciting, positive, creative, challenging and rewarding place to work. • To be part of a diverse and vibrant international community. • Comprehensive Staff Benefits, including generous annual leave entitlement, a defined benefits pension scheme, a wide range of staff discounts, family-friendly initiatives, and flexible work options. Check out the full list on our staff benefits page (opens in a new tab) and use our reward calculator to discover the value of your pay and benefits. Championing equality, diversity, and inclusion The University of Edinburgh holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advance gender equality in higher education. We are members of the Race Equality Charter, and we are also a Stonewall Proud Employer, actively promoting LGBTQ+ equality. Prior to any employment commencing with the University, you will be required to evidence your right to work in the UK. Further information is available on our right to work webpages webpages (opens new browser tab) The University is able to sponsor the employment of international workers in this role. If successful, an international applicant requiring sponsorship to work in the UK will need to satisfy the UK Home Office’s English Language requirements and apply for and secure a Skilled Worker Visa. Key dates to note The closing date for applications is 24th November 2026 Unless stated otherwise the closing time for applications is 11:59pm UK time. If you are applying outside the UK the closing time on our adverts automatically adjusts to your browsers local time zone. The School of Chemistry at the University of Edinburgh has a long and distinguished history. The first Chemistry Professor was appointed to the Chair in 1713 and the Edinburgh University student-led Chemical Society is regarded as the oldest chemical society in the world. The School is run as a single academic unit with approximately 160 staff (made up of academic, postdoctoral researchers and professional services staff) and 800 students (undergraduate, taught postgraduate and research students). Our vision is for all students and staff to harness their curiosity, utilising outstanding and diverse talents to pursue knowledge that can be used to tackle global challenges and improve people’s lives. S ustainability, collaboration and inclusion are at the heart of all our school activities. School of Chemistry researchers deliver advances in both core and interdisciplinary chemistry across five overlapping themes: Catalysis & Synthesis, the Chemistry-Biology Interface, Functional Materials, Structural Chemistry & Chemical Dynamics, and Energy, Environment & Sustainable Chemistry. Our research underpins solutions to major global challenges including health, climate change, and sustainability, and we collaborate extensively with other disciplines and industry partners to take our research towards use. Our 55 group leaders work with a similar number of postdoctoral researchers and approx.155 research students to deliver £32M of current research grants across government, charity and industry funders, as well as a wide variety of public engagement activities. Along with the University of St Andrews we formed EaStCHEM in 2004 to combine the research activities of two of Scotland’s leading schools of chemistry. Combined, we are the largest chemistry unit research in the UK. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework (REF 2021), we were one of only three chemistry submissions to achieve a 100% “world-leading” score for our research environment. We hold a Silver Athena SWAN award, in recognition of excellence in science, engineering and technology employment in higher education relating to supporting women in Science. We are particularly proud of establishing the Christina Miller Fellowships, which have enabled seven talented postdoctoral researchers from under-represented groups to establish themselves as independent group leaders. For further information about our school is available on the School of Chemistry Website ( https://chem.ed.ac.uk/ ) (opens in a new browser tab). "
    }
  },
